// Runtime/SchedRuntime.maxon — ⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE (M) AND THE PROCESSOR (P) — the GMP scheduler's structure,
// and since W213-C1 they are TWO STRUCTS rather than one.**
//
// `GtRuntime.maxon` beside this file owns the GREEN THREAD (G): its struct, its stack, its run queue, its
// timers. This file owns the two things G alone cannot express — **which OS thread is running it (M)** and
// **the per-processor state that thread holds while it does (P)**. Go's model exactly, except that the two
// are SPLIT here, because a P can outlive any one M's grip on it.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE INVARIANT THIS FILE EXISTS TO STATE, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE ONCE.**
//
// > **A COROUTINE — what this runtime calls a GT that `async` created — belongs to the STRAND of the green
// > thread that created it. A run queue holds a strand's owner as a token, never a coroutine, and the one
// > machine that holds the token runs every member of the strand (`GtRuntime.GtOffStrandHolder`); a strand
// > moves between machines only through `__sched_lock`. Therefore every refcount read-modify-write on a box
// > happens on the machine running that box's strand, one machine at a time, and refcounts are plain
// > (`MmRuntime.emitAdjustRefcount`). The cross-thread writers that remain — the IOCP completion loop
// > re-readying a parked GT, and machines publishing, popping and stealing tokens — touch queues, status
// > words, counters and claims, every one of which states its `MultiMSharing` class; none touches a
// > refcount.**
//
// ⭐ **THE LAST CLAUSE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED, AND IT HAD TO BE — the plain refcount rests on it.** An
// emitted program creates an OS thread at **three** `osThreadCreate` sites: `buildIoInit`'s completion
// thread, `__sched_startm`'s worker M, and `emitSchedSysmonLazyStart`'s syscall monitor. The monitor holds
// no processor and runs no green thread, so it never dereferences a box. A worker M RUNS strands, so every
// refcount it touches belongs to the strand it is running — which is the invariant, not an exception to it.
// The completion thread owns no green thread at all, and its whole Maxon call graph is
// `__io_completion_loop` → `__gt_ready_locked` → the queue pushes: no refcount anywhere. ⚠ **A rung that
// gives that thread more to do owes this measurement again** — the refcount door's argument is exactly this
// graph.
//
// ⚖ **USER, 2026-08-27:** *"async is not supposed to create a new green thread. it allows a function to yield
// the current green thread while waiting for a blocking operation"*, and *"the language is supposed to
// guarantee that only 1 green thread can hold references to a box at a time"*. Crossing threads is actors.
// `GtRuntime.GtOffOwner` is where the ownership is a FIELD rather than a convention.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE PROCESS RUNS ON AS MANY Ms AS IT HAS STRANDS TO RUN.** `__sched_init_procs` builds one P per
// processor the machine reports (`emitResolveMaxProcs`), and `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` sets that count exactly.
//
// ⭐ **BUT THE COUNT IS NOT WHAT STARTS AN M; THE WORK IS.** The only thing that starts one is
// `__sched_wake_or_spawn`, reached when a strand's token is published to a run queue — a `spawn`, or a strand
// readied while no machine held it — and the machine that fired a timer or reaped a child for a strand takes
// that token itself rather than waking another. So a program whose only green thread is `main` keeps one M at
// every `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`, while a program that spawns services really does run on several.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT THE FLIP CHANGED IS WHETHER ANY OF IT RUNS BY DEFAULT.** G1 built the whole P/M substrate — the
// CAS claim, the Dekker park, the wake, the per-thread system stack — and W212 built the run-queue hierarchy on
// top of it; both were correct, shipped, and reachable only from a driver script that set the variable by
// hand. `specs/sched-default-procs.md` is where that is stated as the rung's own subject.
//
// ⚠ **A PIECE THAT IS STILL UNREACHED SAYS SO AT ITS OWN DECLARATION**, which is this file's idiom for
// *"built, waiting for its producer"* (`POffRunnext`). G2's
// audit below is unchanged and still discharged.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE AUDIT IS NO LONGER PROSE — IT IS `MultiMSharing`, A REQUIRED ARGUMENT ON EVERY RUNTIME `.data`
// SLOT'S BIRTH (`pushRuntimeGlobal`, below).** This paragraph used to BE the audit: a list derived by grepping
// the tree for *"safe because single-M"*, which is what found its last four items — and exactly the property
// a list in a comment cannot keep, because nothing makes the next slot join it. Every slot the six producers
// in this runtime lay out now states its own class where it is created, and a slot whose author has not
// decided does not compile. The classes and what each promises are on the enum.
//
// ✅ **THE PROSE SWEEP THIS PARAGRAPH TELLS YOU TO RUN WAS RUN AT THE DEFAULT FLIP, AND HERE IS WHAT IT
// FOUND.** `MultiMSharing` covers the `.data` slots structurally; what it cannot cover is an ARGUMENT
// somewhere else in the tree whose warrant is the machine count, and the grep turned up fourteen. Every
// conclusion survived — none of them actually needed one machine — but the REASONS did not, and each now
// states the one that holds: for the compiler's four module-level latches, `Diagnostics`' capture latch
// (this compiler has no `spawn`, so its `async` frames are members of one strand, which runs one member at
// a time at every count); for the spec harness, `SpecWorkerPool`'s one-M section. **The class of defect is a
// comment that justifies a real property with a premise that has expired, and it is not caught by anything
// the compiler runs.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **WHAT G2 FLIPPED, AND WHAT IT LEFT — THE SHORT FORM. The long form is at each slot.**
//
//   1. **THE REFCOUNT READ-MODIFY-WRITE — G2 MADE IT ATOMIC AND ⭐ EC10 TOOK THE PREFIX BACK OFF**, because
//      the invariant at the top of this file removed the second party rather than serialising against it.
//      What SURVIVES from G2 is the half that was load-bearing: `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner` DECREMENTS
//      FIRST and picks its arm from the value the decrement displaced — *the order, not the prefix, is what
//      makes it correct*, which G2's own note said and EC10 is the experiment for. `MmRuntime.
//      emitAdjustRefcount` carries the argument and the measurement. ⚠ G2 cited its reproducer as
//      `temp/g2/rc-field.maxon`; `temp/` is gitignored and swept, so that file no longer exists and the
//      measurement is now `scripts/multicore-stress/refcount-torture.maxon`, COMMITTED, driven by
//      `multicore-stress/refcount-race.sh`.
//   2. **EVERY `.data` COUNTER**, through the one `MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate`, which is what the six
//      scheduler counters (`__gt_live_count`, `__gt_trace_counter`, `__io_pending`, `__io_pipe_seq`,
//      `__sched_nmspinning` and `__sched_active_workers`) call instead of each spelling load/add/store by
//      hand. ⚠ Since EC8 that door emits an `atomicRmw` only
//      where a second OS thread can exist (`multiM`, which is `usage.usesGt`) — the six above are built
//      only under `usesGt`, so THEY are always atomic; what stopped being atomic is the box layer's
//      counters in a program that has one thread, where there is no second writer to serialise against.
//      ⚠ **THE WORD "EVERY" IS LOAD-BEARING**, and a counter stepped far from its declaration is the one
//      that escapes it — `__sched_active_workers` is stepped at a worker's birth and exit, inside
//      `buildSchedLoop`. A list of what obeys a rule is worth
//      grepping against the ops it forbids: `StdOp.globalAddr` followed by `StdOp.atomicRmw` is that grep.
//   3. **THE TWO PARK STORES**, under `__sched_lock` — the timer store's count/base/capacity and the process
//      store's count, plus the three swap-removes `__gt_promise_drop` reaches. What is indivisible there is a
//      TRANSACTION over several words, which no atomic can express. The wake is deliberately outside the
//      lock; `emitSchedEnqueueLocked` / `emitSchedWakeAnIdleM` carry that requirement in their names.
//   4. **THE THREE OUT-PARAM SCRATCH REGIONS** (`__gt_trace_digits`, `__gt_clock_ticks`, `__gt_clock_freq`)
//      are FRAME SLOTS now, not `.data` — per-call storage for a per-call need, which also deleted three
//      globals and one `__slab_alloc`.
//   5. **THE ASYNC RELEASER's CLAIM** is a CAS (`GtRuntime.emitGtReleaserCall`), the one item on the list
//      whose failure was a DOUBLE FREE rather than a lost count.
//   6. **THE SUBPROCESS HANDLE TABLE** — its lazy creation under the lock, its per-slot claim by CAS.
//
// ✅ **THE TWO ITEMS THAT WERE STILL RACY ABOVE ONE M ARE BOTH CLOSED.**
//   • **THE ALLOCATOR (S5).** It was ONE shard with no lock and no ownership gate, and
//     `scripts/multicore-stress/alloc-torture.maxon` died at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=2` and above with exit 86
//     (`slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd`, INV-1) or a segfault. S5 sharded it PER P: each processor owns its own
//     mcache row and is the sole writer of every span cached there, every span carries the P that owns it, a
//     free by a P that does not own the span CAS-pushes onto the owner's remote-free queue, and an OS thread
//     with no P uses a dedicated raw row behind a lock. `SlabRuntime.maxon`'s header records the mechanism
//     and `specs/slab-sharding.md` pins the part one processor can reach.
//     ⚠⚠ **THE PREMISE IS NOW *"A P HAS AT MOST ONE M AT ANY INSTANT"*, AND W213-C1 IS WHERE IT STOPPED
//     BEING *"A P IS BOUND TO EXACTLY ONE M FOR THE M's LIFE"*.** The weaker sentence is the one the shard
//     actually needs and always needed: only the M HOLDING a P allocates from that P's mcache row
//     (`SlabRuntime.emitSlabShardRow` keys on `M->currentP->id`, and an M holding no P takes the raw shard
//     under the lock), so the row still has exactly one writer at a time however many Ms hold the P over its
//     life. ⭐⭐ **AND W213-C2 IS THE RUNG THAT CASHED THAT RESTATEMENT: A P NOW REALLY DOES MOVE.** C1 wrote
//     *"nothing in C1 migrates a P — the policy is the NEXT rung"* and restated the invariant against the
//     day it would; a parked worker releases its processor onto `__sched_pidle` and the next
//     `__sched_startm` may hand it to a DIFFERENT machine. The weaker sentence is what carries it, with
//     nothing in the shard needing to change: a processor on the idle list is held by nobody and is taken
//     off it by exactly one machine, under `__sched_lock`, so *"at most one M at any instant"* holds
//     across the move. **Writing the invariant down before the thing it guards arrived is what made this
//     rung a no-op for the allocator.**
//   • **`ClockRuntime`'s `__clock_wall_ticks` and `__thread_cpu_ticks_out` (W212)** — the same out-param
//     scratch shape item 4 fixed here, in a file G2 did not own. The fix is item 4's, applied one file over:
//     `func.stackSize` plus a `StdOp.stackRecordAddr` each, and the two `.data` words deleted.
//
// ⇒ **a green thread that allocates, retains, releases, sleeps, spawns a child, traces or reads any clock is
// safe on a worker M.** ⚠ **AND "P MIGRATION IS NOT BUILT" IS NO LONGER THE RIGHT SENTENCE — W213-C2 BUILT
// HALF OF IT.** A processor moves between machines at every park and wake; what is still missing is the
// half that takes one away from a machine that is BUSY, so a green thread blocked in a kernel call holds
// its processor for the duration. That is `entersyscall`/`handoffp`/`sysmon`, and it is W213's next rung.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **W213-C1 BUILT THE STRUCTURE THAT MIGRATION NEEDS AND DELIBERATELY CHANGED NO SCHEDULING.** TLS now
// points at an **M** — see its section below — which owns the syscall stack, the running green thread and
// the scheduler context that were on the P, and holds `currentP`, **a field that can read 0**. That zero is
// the entire content of the rung: it is the state a handoff has to be able to write down, and until C1
// nothing in this runtime could express *"an M with no processor"* because TLS pointed at the processor.
// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND W213-C2 IS WHERE THAT ZERO ACQUIRED A PRODUCER.** C1's own sentence here read *"an M
// still takes one P at birth and holds it until it dies; only the shape changed"*, and C2 is the change it
// was waiting for: **the idle state moved off the P too**, so a worker that finds nothing to run RELEASES its
// processor onto `__sched_pidle` and blocks on its OWN event with `M->currentP == 0`. A P can now sit idle
// with no machine, a machine can sit idle with no processor, and `__sched_startm` binds one to the other —
// **which is the door `handoffp` calls.** What has NOT changed is the syscall itself: a green thread inside a
// kernel call still takes its processor in with it, because nothing yet takes one away from a RUNNING
// machine. That is `entersyscall`, and it is the next rung.
//
// ⭐ **WHY A SEPARATE FILE FROM `GtRuntime`.** Not tidiness: the two answer different questions, and one of
// them is read by code the other cannot see. `emitLoadCurrentGt` below is called from the x64 SHIM and the
// x64 PROLOGUE GUARD — hand-assembled Target-tier chunks — as well as from a dozen Std builders, so the P
// layout is a contract shared across the tier boundary exactly as the GT layout is. Buried in `GtRuntime`'s
// 5,900 lines it would read as one more green-thread field.

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE MULTI-M SHARING CLASS OF A RUNTIME `.data` WORD (G2)
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⛔⛔ **A `.data` WORD IS REACHABLE FROM EVERY OS THREAD BY CONSTRUCTION, SO EVERY ONE OF THEM OWES AN
// ANSWER — AND THE ANSWER IS A REQUIRED ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A COMMENT.** G1 left the audit as the prose
// list this file's header used to carry, derived by grepping the tree for *"safe because single-M"*; the
// grep is what found its last four items, which is exactly the property a prose list cannot keep. G2 turns
// it into `pushRuntimeGlobal`'s `sharing` parameter: a slot that nobody has classified is not a slot the
// compiler will build, because the call that creates it does not compile without one.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT A ROSTER + A CHECKER.** A table keyed by label would be a SECOND list of the
// slots, kept in step with the producers by nothing, and its checker would have to run somewhere and could be
// forgotten. The classification travels ON the slot's own creation, which is the shape `StdOpMeta` uses one
// tier up — *"a new variant cannot be added without declaring all of it"* — and it has that shape's property:
// there is no state at which the fact is missing, so there is nothing to check.
export enum MultiMSharing
	// Every mutation is `StdOp.atomicRmw` or `StdOp.atomicCas` IN EVERY BUILD WHERE A SECOND M CAN EXIST.
	// The counters (`MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate`) and the claim words are here.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE QUALIFIER IS EC8's AND IT IS NOT A LOOPHOLE.** `emitGlobalAccumulate` emits a plain
	// load/add/store when `usage.usesGt` is false — a program with no green threads has ONE OS thread, so
	// there is no second writer for an atomic to serialise against and the `lock` prefix is paid on every
	// allocation in the language to guard a race that cannot occur. The class states the discipline a slot
	// owes WHEN THE HAZARD EXISTS; it never claimed a prefix is emitted where no thread can race.
	atomicStep

	// Every mutation, and every read WHOSE ANSWER IS USED, happens inside `__sched_lock`. A word whose
	// transaction spans several words — the FIFO's head/tail pair, the timer store's count/base/capacity
	// triple, the process store's count — cannot be made indivisible by an atomic, because what must be
	// indivisible is not a word.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE EXCEPTION IS EXPLICIT AND NARROW: AN UNLOCKED READ THAT ONLY PICKS AN ARM, WHERE BOTH ARMS ARE
	// CORRECT WHATEVER IT ANSWERS.** A stale answer costs a wasted look or a skipped look, never a wrong value:
	// whatever a peek decides, the LOCKED operation behind it is what actually takes something, and it answers
	// "nothing" to a racer exactly as it would to an empty list.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE SITES ARE NAMED, BECAUSE AN EXCEPTION NOBODY CAN ENUMERATE IS A LOOPHOLE.** Four read
	// `__gt_run_queue_head`, all through the one door `emitSchedGlobalQueueIs` and all in `SchedRuntime`:
	// `emitSchedAnyRunnable`'s global-tier peek (reached from `buildSchedLoop`'s `widlefire`, which chooses between
	// searching again and parking, and from `__sched_handoffp` and `__sched_retake`, which choose whether a
	// processor is worth handing on); `__sched_find_runnable`'s 61-schedule fairness peek (`frfairpeek`); its
	// global-batch peek (`frglobalpeek`); and `__sched_any_work`. One reads `__sched_npidle`:
	// `__sched_find_runnable`'s spinner cap (`frspincap`), which chooses whether this machine may join the search —
	// a stale answer costs one spinner too many or one search left to the spinners the cap counted, which is Go's
	// own reading of the same word (`sched.npidle.Load()`). One reads `__sched_midle`'s head:
	// `__sched_wake_poller`'s peek (`wppeek`), which chooses whether to take the lock and look for a parked
	// machine, and whose stale "empty" that function argues. The published earliest deadline (`__gt_timer_when`) is
	// read through the one door `GtRuntime.emitGtLoadTimerWhen` by every due test — `buildSchedLoop`'s
	// `wchecktimers`, the polls `GtRuntime.emitGtPollStores` makes for `widlepoll`, `wpolled` and the strand
	// runner's yield — and, with the parked-child count (`__gt_proc_count`), by the owed-poller test
	// (`emitSchedPollerOwed`) and the children's poll. A stale answer costs one needless look or one look deferred
	// to the next, and the locked walks behind them decide what fires; `GtTimerWhenLabel` argues why a deferred
	// fire is not a lost one. The poller's claim and deadline (`__sched_lastpoll`, `__sched_poll_until`) are read
	// by `__sched_wake_netpoller`, by the owed-poller test and by `__sysmon`'s stale-poll peek, each of which
	// argues its two arms at its own site. One reads `__sched_phase`: the main machine's loop-top exit test
	// (`emitSchedLoopExitTest`), whose stale answer costs one more lap before the locked test at `wstoptest` sees
	// it. `GtRuntime.__gt_owner_done` reads it too, but that read is half of a Dekker pair and carries its own
	// argument.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE MORE READS A WORD TO REPORT IT RATHER THAN TO PICK AN ARM**: `__sched_idle_processor_count`
	// answers `__sched_npidle`. One aligned word, so every answer is a count some locked writer really stored;
	// a caller that wants it settled polls it, as `specs/sched-processor.md` does.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND TWO WALK `__sched_allm` UNLOCKED, WHOSE ARGUMENT IS A DIFFERENT ONE**: `__sched_stop_workers`'s join
	// walk and `__sched_park_wake_count`. They are WALKS, and a stale answer is not what makes them safe: the list
	// is APPEND-ONLY over never-freed structs and `emitSchedListPushLocked` publishes a node's link BEFORE the
	// head, so a walker can never reach a node whose link is unwritten. `SchedAllMLabel` carries it in full.
	//
	// A further site is not covered by any of this: it needs its own argument at its own line, or the lock.
	underSchedLock

	// Written before any worker M can exist, and never after. Bring-up is single-threaded BY CONSTRUCTION and
	// not by convention: the first `__gt_init` is reached by the only thread there is, because
	// `__sched_init_procs` — which it calls — is what BUILDS the P array a worker is spawned onto, and the
	// spawner (`__sched_wake_or_spawn`) is reachable only from an enqueue, which is reachable only after
	// bring-up returns. A slot nothing writes at all (`__sched_shutdown_flag`, whose teardown does not exist
	// yet) is sealed trivially and rides this class too.
	sealedBeforeWorkers

	// A plain read-modify-write whose lost update is BOUNDED and SELF-CORRECTING, argued at its own site.
	// One member: `__sched_max_active_workers`, a monotone high-water mark — a lost sample loses a transient
	// reading while the mark still converges to the true peak, and it can never UNDER-report two live
	// workers, because the reader's own increment is already visible in the value it compares.
	monotoneApproximation

	// ONE aligned machine word, written whole and read whole, whose contract does not say WHICH writer's
	// value a reader gets. No tearing is possible on any lane the compiler emits, so every observable value is
	// a value somebody really wrote; what multi-M costs is precision, not safety. Each member argues its own
	// site: `__subp_last_error`, `__sched_shutdown_flag` and `__sched_needspinning`.
	lastWriterWins

	// ⛔ **THE ANSWER `pushRuntimeGlobal` REFUSES, and the reason it is a member rather than an omission is
	// that "I do not know" is the answer a new slot arrives with.** A shared word with no discipline is the
	// failure this whole rung exists to remove — a lost update nothing reproduces on demand — so a slot that
	// wants this class is not a slot that may be added: fix it (one of the four above), or take the surface
	// it belongs to as its own rung and put the slot in a file that owns it.
	unclassified
end 'MultiMSharing'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DOOR A RUNTIME `.data` SLOT IS BORN THROUGH**, and the only place the
// `DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(<label>), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0)` incantation is spelled —
// it was written out at thirty sites across six producers, which is thirty chances to get the type or the
// seed wrong and, until this rung, thirty slots whose sharing nobody had to state.
//
// `seed` defaults to 0 because the zero image IS the initial state for almost every slot (see
// `gtRuntimeGlobals`); the two worker counters and `__gt_timer_when` are the exceptions and say so by passing one.
//
// `valueType` defaults to a machine word because almost every runtime slot holds one — a pointer, a count,
// a lock word. A slot that holds LESS says so, and the width is not cosmetic: `GlobalDataTable.layOut`
// orders `.data` by storage size descending, so a narrower runtime slot lands behind every user global of
// every width instead of displacing the ones narrower than a word.
//
// ⚠ **THE `usage`/`--debugstream` GATES STAY AT THE CALL SITES.** Whether a slot is laid out at all is a
// question about the PROGRAM; this door answers a question about the SLOT.
export function pushRuntimeGlobal(out DataSectionEntryArray, label ByteArray, sharing MultiMSharing, seed ParsedInt = 0, valueType StdType = StdType.i64)
	match sharing 'classified'
		atomicStep or
			underSchedLock or
			sealedBeforeWorkers or
			monotoneApproximation or
			lastWriterWins then break 'classified'
		unclassified then panic("pushRuntimeGlobal: '{String.from(label)}' is laid out with no multi-M sharing class. A `.data` word is reachable from every OS thread by construction, so it needs one of: atomicStep (every mutation is an atomicRmw/atomicCas wherever a second M can exist), underSchedLock (every read and write inside `__sched_lock`), sealedBeforeWorkers (written only during bring-up, which no worker M can race), monotoneApproximation (a plain RMW whose lost update is bounded and self-correcting), or lastWriterWins (one word whose contract never said which writer wins). Argue the choice at the site. See MultiMSharing.")
	end 'classified'

	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(label), valueType: valueType, value: seed))
end 'pushRuntimeGlobal'

// One machine word — the stride of the `P*` array `__sched_procs` points at, the width of every scalar field
// in BOTH structs below, and the stride of one ring slot. Distinct from `GtRuntime.StackWordBytes` (a STACK
// word) and `GtArgSlotBytes` (an async ARGUMENT slot) though all three are 8: three facts, three names, so
// moving one cannot move the others.
//
// ⚠ It is declared ABOVE both structs because both derive offsets from it — it used to sit in the middle of
// the P, which read as though it were the P's own stride, and W213-C2's M tail needs the same number.
let PointerSlotBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE MACHINE (M) STRUCT — ONE PER OS THREAD THAT RUNS MAXON CODE (W213-C1)
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHAT AN M OWNS IS WHAT BELONGS TO A THREAD OF EXECUTION, AND THE TEST IS ONE QUESTION: *"if this
// thread's P were taken away while it is blocked in a kernel call, would this field still be true of it?"***
// A syscall scratch stack, the green thread currently executing, and the scheduler context that thread was
// switched in FROM all answer YES — they belong to the thread that is RUNNING, not to the processor it
// happens to hold. A run queue, a ring, a steal counter and an allocator shard index answer NO — they belong
// to the processor, and the whole point of W213 is that they can be handed to somebody else.
//
// ⛔⛔ **EVERY ONE OF THESE FIELDS WAS ON THE P UNTIL THIS RUNG, AND THREE OF THEM WOULD HAVE BEEN A SILENT
// CORRUPTION THE DAY A HANDOFF LANDED.** The failure shape is identical in all three and it is the one
// `POffReservedSystemStackSP` records from G1: two Ms writing one region, interleaved, with no fault.
//
//   • **the 64 KB syscall stack** — two Ms inside shimmed kernel calls would set RSP to the same top.
//   • **`currentGt`** — the green thread executing inside the kernel call is the BLOCKED M's; a second M
//     adopting that P would overwrite the word naming it, and the blocked M's shim reads that same word
//     after the call to restore the TIB.
//   • **the inline scheduler GT (Go's `g0`)** — its TIB bounds are captured by the first context switch away
//     from it, and those bounds are one OS THREAD's real stack. A second M adopting a P whose `g0` carries
//     the first M's bounds would publish another thread's stack limits into its own TIB.
//
// ⭐ **C1 MOVED THE STATE AND CHANGED NO POLICY, AND ITS SENTENCE HERE IS NOW HISTORY.** It read *"an M
// still takes one P at birth and holds it until it dies"*, which W213-C2 falsified one rung later: a worker
// releases its processor at every park (`emitSchedReleaseProcLocked`) and takes one back through
// `MOffNextP`. The fields C1 moved are the reason that was possible — a machine parked with no processor
// still owns the stack it is on, the green thread it was running and the context it runs its scheduler on.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **A QUESTION ABOUT MACHINES WALKS THE ROSTER, NOT THE P ARRAY.** An M parked with no P is on no P, so
// a walk of `__sched_procs` cannot see it at all. `__sched_allm` below is the roster and `MOffAllNext` its
// link; the teardown's signal and join and sysmon's retake walk it.
//
// ⚠ **AN M IS NEVER FREED**, exactly as a P is never freed: it is created by the OS thread that will be it
// and lives until the process ends. `__sched_worker_loop`'s exit clears `currentP` and leaves the struct and
// its 64 KB region behind, because the only road to that exit is process teardown — and the roster keeps a
// pointer to it for ever, which is what makes the teardown join able to name a thread after that thread has
// stopped touching anything.

// ⭐⭐ **THE P THIS M CURRENTLY HOLDS, OR 0 — THE FIELD THIS WHOLE RUNG EXISTS TO CREATE.** Every P read in
// the program now goes through it — `emitLoadCurrentP` where the walk starts at TLS, one `emitLoadField` at
// the sites that already hold the M — so *"this thread has no processor"* is one load and one compare
// rather than a state the runtime had no way to write down.
//
// EXPORTED because `SlabRuntime`'s shard read walks it: the allocator's row is keyed on `P->id`, and the
// thread that has no P is exactly the thread that must take the raw shard.
export let MOffCurrentP = 0x00 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE GREEN THREAD THIS M IS EXECUTING** — Go's `m.curg`, and it was `P->currentGt` from G1 until this
// rung. It is read by the syscall shim and the prologue stack guard on every guarded call, which is why it
// sits one word into the struct TLS already points at: the hot read is `gs:[tebOffset]` + one field load,
// exactly the two it was before.
//
// EXPORTED because those readers span both tiers: builder-built Std here and in `GtRuntime`, and the
// hand-assembled x64 and arm64 shims and prologue guards.
export let MOffCurrentGt = 0x08 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THIS M's OWN SYSCALL STACK — the field whose two previous homes are both recorded as bugs.** Every
// Win32 / libSystem call a green thread makes runs on a 64 KB scratch stack instead of the GT's tiny one
// (the syscall shim). It was ONE `.data` GLOBAL until G1, whose safety invariant was the prose *"single-M
// runs one thing at a time"*; G1 moved it to the P because multi-M falsified that. **This rung is the same
// correction one level further, and the G1 argument applies verbatim to it**: a P handed to a second M while
// the first is still inside a kernel call on that region puts two Ms on ONE stack, as silent interleaved
// corruption rather than a fault.
//
// EXPORTED because the shim and the prologue guard's cold path (both hand-assembled) read it.
export let MOffSystemStackSP = 0x10 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THIS M's OWN SCHEDULER GREEN THREAD — an INLINE GT struct, not a pointer.** Go's `g0`: the context the
// M runs its scheduler loop on, and the `from` half of every `__gt_context_switch` into a real green thread.
// Inline because it is created with the M, lives exactly as long, and a pointer would be a second allocation
// and a second lifetime for a struct that has neither.
//
// Its `stackBase` is 0 (the slab's zero), which is load-bearing two ways: the syscall shim's guard reads it
// as *"this is a real OS thread stack, call directly"*, and the prologue stack guard reads it as *"nothing to
// grow"*. It runs the machine's scheduler loop and, on the main machine, the exit path — no user code, and
// nothing that parks. Its TIB bounds are captured by the first `__gt_context_switch` AWAY from it — and those
// bounds are THIS OS thread's, which is the whole reason the struct may not live on a P that can move.
//
// EXPORTED for `GtRuntime.__gt_run_strand`, which switches into a strand's members from it.
export let MOffSchedulerContext = 0x18 as ByteOffset

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE M's TAIL — THE IDLE STATE
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⛔⛔ **EVERY FIELD BELOW IS THE MACHINE's AND NOT A PROCESSOR's, BY ONE TEST: *"if this thread's processor
// were taken away, would this field still be true of it?"*** An idle machine holds no processor, and every
// one of these answers YES:
//
//   • **the wait handle** — a machine with no processor still has to be parkable and wakeable.
//   • **the OS thread handle** — it names this thread, and a processor moves between threads, so a join
//     through a processor could name the wrong thread and succeed silently, because `WaitForSingleObject` on
//     a live handle succeeds.
//   • **the spinning bit** — Go's `mp.spinning`, a per-M bool.
//   • **the idle-list links** — a machine parked with no processor is on `__sched_midle`, which is what
//     `__sched_checkdead` counts.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TAIL STARTS PAST THE INLINE GT, AND THAT IS A CROSS-TIER REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN TIDINESS.**
// `MOffCurrentGt` (0x08) and `MOffSystemStackSP` (0x10) are read by FOUR hand-assembled Target-tier chunks —
// the x64 and arm64 import shims and prologue stack guards — which bake those two displacements as
// immediates. Fields appended here leave all four byte-identical, so cross-target parity is a property of
// WHERE the fields sit rather than of two matching edits.

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THIS M's OWN PARK — Go's `m.park`, and the field that makes an M with no processor WAKEABLE.**
// An auto-reset event, created with the M by `emitSchedAllocM`. It was `POffWakeEvent`, one per P, and an M
// reached it through the processor it held; a parked M holds none, so the handle had to come with it.
//
// ⭐ **AUTO-RESET IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS THE WHOLE MISSED-WAKEUP ARGUMENT FOR ONE WINDOW** (`osEventCreate`'s
// contract: `CreateEventA(NULL, FALSE, FALSE, NULL)`; on arm64-macOS the same op is a malloc'd pthread
// mutex+cond whose `signal` word `takeSignal` clears, which is the same semantics). A `startm` that signals
// between this M's `__sched_lock` release and its `osWaitHandle` leaves the event SIGNALLED, and the wait
// returns at once. Everything before that release is covered by membership in `__sched_midle`, taken under
// the one lock — see `buildSchedLoop`'s park.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE ONE WAITABLE OBJECT THIS MACHINE BLOCKS ON** — the scheduler loop's park (`wdowait`), and a
// robbed worker's park at shutdown (`__sched_syscall_regain`). The main machine's is also what `main`'s
// completion and the exit drain's last debit signal (`emitSchedSignalMainMachine`).
//
// ⚠ **A STRAY SIGNAL LEFT PENDING** — a signal to the main machine while it was running, or one that landed after
// the wait it was for had already ended — costs its next wait one extra lap of a loop that re-tests everything.
// That is a lap, not a lost wakeup.
let MOffParkEvent = ((MOffSchedulerContext as ParsedInt) + GtStructSize) as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE `CreateThread` HANDLE OF *THIS* M — moved off the P, and the move is a CORRECTNESS FIX rather
// than a tidying (W213-C2).** `POffOsThreadHandle` stored the handle of the M that originally claimed that
// P, and `__sched_stop_workers` joined by walking `__sched_procs`. Both were true while a P belonged to one
// M for that M's life. With a P released at every park and re-taken by whoever `startm` hands it to, the
// P-keyed handle names a thread that has long since moved on — and joining it SUCCEEDS, because the handle
// is still live, so the teardown would return with a different worker still running and `__mm_leak_check`
// would read counters that are still moving. The join walks `__sched_allm` now.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS 0 FOR THE MAIN OS THREAD, WHICH THIS RUNTIME NEVER CREATED, AND THAT ZERO IS READ AS AN IDENTITY
// BY TWO PARTIES.** The teardown's join skips it, which is why the roster walk needs no "except me" arm; and
// `buildSchedSyscallRegain`'s shutdown escape reads it to tell the machine that RUNS the process exit from a
// worker the exit discards. Both reads are exact rather than probable because `__sched_startm` stores this
// word before releasing `__sched_lock`, so no machine can observe its own zero after taking that lock.
let MOffOsThreadHandle = MOffParkEvent + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE P A `startm` HAS HANDED THIS M, OR 0 — Go's `m.nextp`, and the field item 4 of this rung is
// about.** A waker takes a processor out of `__sched_pidle` and must give it to the M it is about to signal;
// the M is asleep and cannot take it itself, so the P is DELIVERED here and collected on the far side of the
// wait. That is what *"bind an M to a P it is HANDED"* means, and it is the one primitive
// `entersyscall`/`handoffp` needs that no amount of scanning could supply: `handoffp(pp)` names its P.
//
// ⚠ **A ZERO HERE ON WAKE IS NORMAL AND IS NOT AN ERROR.** An M is also woken by the main machine's signal, by
// `__sched_wake_poller` and at teardown, none of which hands it anything; it then goes back round the final look.
// `buildSchedLoop`'s `wafterwait` is the one reader, and it is written for both.
let MOffNextP = MOffOsThreadHandle + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS M HOLD A UNIT OF `__sched_nmspinning`? — Go's `mp.spinning` (`proc.go:3228`), which is
// where it always was and where `POffSpinning` said it belonged.** 1 means *"one unit of the global count
// belongs to this M"*. See `SchedSpinningLabel` for what the count buys and `emitSchedSpinningTransition`
// for the four things that can happen to a credit.
//
// `__sched_nmspinning` is a COUNT and a count alone cannot say WHO is counted, so a machine returning from a
// search has no way to know whether it owes a decrement. This is the bool that says so.
//
// ⛔⛔ **BOTH WRITERS USE A CAS, AND THE SECOND WRITER IS WHY.** The owning machine claims a credit at
// `__sched_find_runnable`'s spinner cap and releases it when it stops searching — but a WAKER also sets this
// word, HANDING OVER the credit it reserved before signalling (`buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s gate reserves at
// most one spinning machine per wakeup, and the reservation has to survive the whole `osSetEvent`→wake
// latency or it buys nothing). Those
// two writers race in exactly one window: a machine whose `osWaitHandle` has returned but which has not yet
// reached its own claim can be claimed by a waker on the way. A plain test-and-set there loses one of the two
// decisions and the count DRIFTS UP — and a drifted count suppresses wakeups for the life of the process,
// which is lost work, not a slow path. With a CAS on both sides exactly one wins: the machine wins and counts
// its own credit while the waker's becomes surplus and is given back, or the waker wins and the machine
// silently inherits it.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT W213-C2 CHANGED IS ONLY WHICH STRUCT THE WORD SITS ON, AND IT MAKES THE RACE NARROWER RATHER THAN
// WIDER**: a credit handed to a machine can no longer be inherited by a DIFFERENT machine through a P that
// moved, and the hand-over now requires `mget` under `__sched_lock` rather than winning a lock-free flag.
// `SpinningTransition.stopSpinning` carries which of MC1's two reclaim sites that closes by construction.
//
// ⚠ **A `.data`-free slot, for `POffStealCount`'s reason** — a `.data` word appears in the `data {` section
// of every green-thread golden in the corpus, and this is per-machine state, not program state.
let MOffSpinning = MOffNextP + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// The two states, named for `PStatusUnused`/`PStatusActive`'s reason one field family up: the CAS that
// claims the word and the CAS that releases it must agree about which value means *held*, and a pair of
// bare literals at two sites is that fact written twice.
let MSpinningFree = 0 as ParsedInt
let MSpinningHeld = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE ROSTER'S LINK — Go's `allm`.** Every M pushes itself here at birth, under `__sched_lock`, and
// is never removed: an M is never freed, so the roster is append-only and a walker needs no snapshot.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A SEPARATE LINK FROM `MOffIdleNext` AND THE TWO MAY NOT SHARE ONE WORD.** An M on the idle list
// is also on the roster — the teardown must signal and join a PARKED worker, which is the common case at
// exit — so a single link would splice one list into the other the first time an M parked. Two lists, two
// links, and each door names the one it threads.
let MOffAllNext = MOffSpinning + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_midle`'s FORWARD LINK — Go's `m.idleNode.next`.** The list is doubly linked through this
// word and `MOffIdlePrev`, and both are written only under `__sched_lock` by the list's three doors:
// `emitSchedMidlePutLocked` (Go's `mput`), `emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked` (the pop, `mgetSpecific` and a
// signalled machine's self-removal) and the membership test `emitSchedMidleHolds`.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO "AM I ON THE IDLE LIST" FLAG BESIDE IT, DELIBERATELY.** Membership of a list guarded by a
// mutex already answers that question, atomically with the act of joining; `buildSchedLoop`'s park
// carries the missed-wakeup argument that rests on it. The back-link is what makes the answer O(1).
let MOffIdleNext = MOffAllNext + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE SYSCALL BRACKET'S WORDS (W213-C3) — "IS THIS MACHINE INSIDE A KERNEL CALL, AND MAY ITS PROCESSOR
// BE TAKEN AWAY?"
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE STATE IS ON THE MACHINE, NOT ON THE PROCESSOR AND NOT ON THE GREEN THREAD.** Go marks the *G*
// `_Gsyscall` and mediates ownership through a lock bit in the G's status word (`_Gscan`). Here `GtOffStatus` is
// stored UNCONDITIONALLY by the hand-assembled trampoline and by the IOCP completion loop — which is precisely why
// `GtOffTeardown` had to be a field of its own (see its header), and why a lock bit there would not survive
// either.
//
// ⇒ **the machine owns all three facts** — `MOffCurrentP`, `MOffCurrentGt` and this word — so `__sched_retake`
// reads the triple off ONE struct, reaching it through the processor it holds (`POffM`, Go's `pp.m`).
//
// ⛔⛔⛔ **THE WORD CARRIES A GENERATION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE PROTOCOL IS SOUND — A BARE
// `Active` IS NOT A UNIQUE TOKEN.** The first cut of this rung kept the state and the generation in two
// words and had sysmon compare-and-swap `Active → Stolen`. **That is an ABA, and it was caught in review
// before it shipped:** between sysmon reading the generation and reaching its CAS — a dozen instructions,
// but sysmon is an ordinary preemptible OS thread on a ~15 ms quantum — the victim can leave call *N* on
// the fast path, find nothing runnable, `wpark`, RELEASE its processor onto `__sched_pidle`, have another
// machine claim it, be handed a different processor, and enter call *N+1*. Its state word reads `Active`
// again, so the CAS SUCCEEDS — against a different call, on a processor a second machine is now running.
// The handoff that follows would start a machine on a ring another machine already owns: two lock-free
// writers on one ring and one allocator shard, which is `emitSchedClaimProc`'s heap corruption arriving by
// a new road.
//
// ⇒ **the value sysmon compare-and-swaps must be one NO SUBSEQUENT CALL CAN PRESENT**, which is what Go's
// generation counter is for and why `setBlockOnExitSyscall` re-validates `g→m→p` under a lock bit at all.
// The compiler folds the generation INTO the state word instead:
//
//     bit 0       MSyscallInCall   this machine is inside a bracketed kernel call
//     bit 1       MSyscallStolen   sysmon has taken this machine's processor
//     bits 2..63  the GENERATION, stepped on every entersyscall
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERATION IS STRICTLY MONOTONE BECAUSE NOTHING EVER SUBTRACTS FROM IT.** `entersyscall` adds
// `MSyscallEnterDelta` (one generation step plus the in-call bit); `exitsyscall` subtracts `MSyscallInCall`;
// `__sched_syscall_regain` subtracts `MSyscallStolen`. Not one of the three touches bits 2 and up except to
// increase them. So a word `v` observed with the in-call bit set names exactly ONE call for the life of the
// process, and sysmon's `CAS(word, v, v + MSyscallStolen)` succeeding is a PROOF that the machine is still
// inside that call. ⚠ At 62 bits the counter wraps after ~4.6e18 bracketed calls; at a million a second
// that is 146,000 years, so the wrap is not a state this process can reach.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND THAT PROOF IS WHAT MAKES THE PROCESSOR STILL VALID AT THE HANDOFF.** A machine changes
// `MOffCurrentP` only at `emitSchedAdoptProc` and `emitSchedReleaseProcLocked`, and both run OUTSIDE a
// bracketed call. So while the in-call bit has been continuously set for ONE generation, the processor
// cannot have moved — which is exactly what the CAS establishes, and it is why `__sched_retake` may read the
// processor before the CAS and hand it off after.
//
// The whole protocol, in one instruction each way and one CAS:
//
//   • **entersyscall** (`X64GtRuntime.emitGtEnterSyscall`): `lock add [M+state], MSyscallEnterDelta`. ONE
//     instruction. Before it no thief can act on this call; after it one may.
//   • **the thief** (`__sched_retake`): read `v`; require `v & MSyscallStateMask == MSyscallInCall`; require
//     `v` to equal the word it recorded a poll ago; then `CAS(word, v, v + MSyscallStolen)` — **the
//     linearization point** — and only on success `CAS(MOffCurrentP, p, 0)` and `__sched_handoffp(p)`.
//   • **exitsyscall** (`X64GtRuntime.emitGtExitSyscall`): `lock xadd [M+state], -MSyscallInCall`, whose
//     answer is the PRIOR word. The stolen bit in that answer is the whole verdict: clear ⇒ this machine
//     still holds its processor; set ⇒ `__sched_syscall_regain` must obtain another.
//
// ⭐ **THE SECOND CAS's ANSWER IS DISCARDED, AND *NOW* THAT IS ARGUED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** Once the state
// CAS has succeeded, `p` is this thief's and nobody else's — it is off `__sched_pidle`, marked
// `PStatusActive`, and named by no machine the moment the CAS lands. The `CAS(MOffCurrentP, p, 0)` can still
// fail, and in exactly one way: the victim raced ahead, saw the stolen bit and regained a DIFFERENT
// processor. A plain store would then wipe a processor the machine legitimately holds; as a CAS it simply
// declines. **Both outcomes leave `p` the thief's, which is why the handoff below is unconditional and the
// answer is not read.**
//
// ⛔ **THE STOLEN BIT OUTLIVES THE CALL, DELIBERATELY, AND `__sched_syscall_regain` IS WHAT CLEARS IT.**
// Between `exitsyscall` and a regained processor the word reads `MSyscallStolen` alone: the in-call bit is
// down, so `__sched_retake` skips this machine (it requires exactly `MSyscallInCall`), and the state bits are
// non-zero, so `__sched_stop_workers` does not join it. **That second property is not incidental** — see
// `buildSchedSyscallRegain`, where it is what keeps the exit path from blocking for ever on a machine that is
// waiting for a processor it may never get.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE BIT ARITHMETIC RESTS ON BRACKETED CALLS NEVER NESTING, AND THAT IS AN INVARIANT OF THE SHIM
// RATHER THAN A HOPE.** Two `entersyscall`s without an intervening exit would add `MSyscallEnterDelta` twice
// and carry `MSyscallInCall` into the stolen bit — a machine that had never been robbed reading as robbed.
// It cannot happen: a shimmed import is reached only from Maxon code, the kernel never calls back into it,
// and the bracket sits on the shim's switched road alone, which a call made on the system stack never takes
// (`X64GtRuntime.emitGtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack`). ⇒ **anything that ever makes the shim re-entrant owes this
// encoding a second look.**
//
// ⚠ **THE ORDERING, STATED FOR W223's arm64 AUDIT (§7).** Every mutation of this word is a LOCKed
// read-modify-write or a CAS, so on x64 each is already a full barrier and the protocol needs no fence of its
// own. On arm64 the same three points need acquire/release on this one word, and the thief's first READ of it
// is an acquire too: it reads `MOffCurrentP` after that read and before its CAS, and only the acquire orders
// the two loads. Its write of `MOffCurrentP` is a compare-and-swap.
//
// `atomicStep` in `MultiMSharing`'s vocabulary, though it is a per-machine FIELD rather than a `.data` slot:
// two threads write it, and every mutation is `lock add`, `lock xadd` or `StdOp.atomicCas`.
export let MOffSyscallState = MOffIdleNext + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// *"This machine is inside a bracketed kernel call."* Bit 0.
export let MSyscallInCall = 1 as ParsedInt

// *"sysmon has taken this machine's processor."* Bit 1 — the only bit in this word with a different party on
// each of its two edges: `__sched_retake` sets it, `__sched_syscall_regain` clears it.
export let MSyscallStolen = 2 as ParsedInt

// The two state bits together. A word whose state bits are ALL ZERO is a machine running ordinary Maxon code,
// which is the slab's zero and therefore already right for a machine before its first shimmed call.
let MSyscallStateMask = 3 as ParsedInt

// One generation — the first bit above the state bits, so adding it steps the generation and leaves the state
// alone, which is the property `MSyscallEnterDelta` is built out of.
let MSyscallGenStep = 4 as ParsedInt

// What `entersyscall` adds: a new generation AND the in-call bit, in ONE `lock add`. Stated as the sum of its
// two terms rather than as `5`, so that widening the state bits moves the delta with them instead of leaving a
// literal behind that still says five.
export let MSyscallEnterDelta = MSyscallGenStep + MSyscallInCall

// ⭐ **sysmon's OWN NOTES ABOUT THIS MACHINE — Go's per-P `sysmontick` (`proc.go:6682`), kept on the M though
// the retake walks processors.** The exact state word it last observed, and the time it first saw it. The word's
// generation counts THIS machine's calls, so a note kept on the processor would be compared against another
// machine's word once the processor changed hands — two machines can both sit in-call at one generation — and a
// fresh call would read as one the monitor had been timing. On the machine a note is only ever compared with
// the word it was taken from.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT RECORDS THE WHOLE WORD AND NOT A TICK, WHICH IS WHAT COLLAPSED TWO FIELDS INTO ONE.** With the
// generation folded into the state word, *"the same call"* and *"the same word"* are ONE comparison — so the
// separate tick this pair used to sit beside is gone, and with it the window in which the two could be read a
// moment apart and describe different calls.
//
// ⛔⛔ **WITHOUT THE COMPARISON `print` WOULD BE INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A ONE-SECOND READ.** `WriteFile` is
// bracketed and is the hottest bracketed slot there is; a sysmon that saw the in-call bit on two consecutive
// polls and concluded *"stuck"* would retake a processor from a machine that had made a thousand short calls
// in between. Requiring the WORD to be unchanged is what makes the conclusion *"this machine has been inside
// ONE call since I last looked"*.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER NEEDS ANY SYNCHRONISATION AND NEITHER OWES A `MultiMSharing` CLASS**, because there is exactly
// one sysmon thread and it is the only reader and the only writer of both. They live on the M rather than in a
// parallel array so a machine created after sysmon started arrives with its notes zeroed by the same slab
// allocation that zeroes everything else on it — an array indexed by machine would be a structure whose growth
// has to be synchronised against `__sched_startm`.
//
// ⚠ **A ZERO NOTE CANNOT BE MISTAKEN FOR AN OBSERVATION**, which is what makes the slab's zero safe here: a
// machine sysmon can act on has the in-call bit set and a generation of at least one, so its word is at least
// `MSyscallEnterDelta` and never compares equal to an unwritten note.
let MOffSysmonWord = MOffSyscallState + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)
let MOffSysmonWhen = MOffSysmonWord + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_midle`'s BACK-LINK — Go's `m.idleNode.prev`, and what makes both removals from the middle of
// the list O(1).** A machine woken by a signal that handed it no processor unlinks itself, and `mgetSpecific`
// takes one particular machine; a singly linked list could answer either only by walking from the head. 0 on the
// head and on every machine that is not on the list, which is why *"on the list"* is `head == m or prev != 0`.
let MOffIdlePrev = MOffSysmonWhen + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐ **HOW MANY TIMES THIS MACHINE'S BLOCKING WAITS HAVE RETURNED, FOR ANY REASON** — a hand-over, a signal, a
// deadline. The reading `__Builtins.schedParkWakeCount()` sums over `__sched_allm`, and the number an idle
// program keeps near zero. Owner-written, so a plain step; per-machine rather than `.data` for
// `POffStealCount`'s reason.
let MOffParkWakes = MOffIdlePrev + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐ **`__sched_regain_head`'s LINK** — the machines back from a kernel call whose processor was taken, each
// waiting on its own park event for `pidleput` to hand it one (`emitSchedPidlePutLocked`). Separate from the idle
// links because such a machine holds a green thread and is not idle.
let MOffRegainNext = MOffParkWakes + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// The three head scalars, the inline GT, the idle tail, the syscall bracket's three, the idle back-link, the
// wake count and the regain link. Derived, never written down as a number, for `PStructSize`'s reason.
let MStructSize = ((MOffRegainNext as ParsedInt) + PointerSlotBytes) as ParsedInt

// ==========================================================================
// The ProcContext (P) struct
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐ **THE HEADER IS A FIXED, PORTABLE LAYOUT THROUGH 0x78**, so a debug agent, an allocator shard index and
// a netpoll all graft onto it without a field moving. Fields this file does not use are RESERVED rather
// than omitted, for the reason `GtRuntime` reserves the GT's 0x60–0xA0 window: a field added later at a
// different offset is a layout fork, and a layout fork under a shared debugger is a silent wrong answer.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A P IS NOT ITS M, WHICH IS WHY THREE FIELDS SIT ON THE M INSTEAD.** A P bound to one OS thread for
// life keeps `currentGt` at 0x18, a system stack at 0x70 and an inline `mainThread` at 0x80; a P that can
// be HANDED to another M cannot, so those three live on the M (see its section above) and their P slots are
// reserved rather than reused. `id`, `rng`, `idleFlag`, `wakeEvent`, `osThreadHandle` and `status` stay exactly
// where they are. ⚠ **SIX header words are RESERVED and read a permanent zero rather than a value** —
// `currentGt`, the system stack, `idleFlag`, `wakeEvent`, `osThreadHandle` and 0x50 — which is worth saying
// plainly to anyone grafting a debug agent onto this header.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE TAIL CARRIES A 256-SLOT RING**, which a linked local queue has no equivalent of, so only the
// HEADER is portable; the tail grafts onto nothing and the total size is its own.

// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-P LOCAL RUN-QUEUE RING (Go's `runq`) — TWO MONOTONIC COUNTERS, NOT TWO POINTERS.** They
// count how many green threads have EVER been pushed to and popped from this ring; the ring slot is
// `counter mod RunqSlots` and the length is `tail - head`. That is what makes the pop a single CAS: a thief
// and the owner both advance `runqhead` by compare-and-swap, and a counter that only ever rises has no ABA
// to have (a head that reads `h` twice really did not move — a pair of pointers can look identical after a
// full lap). `POffRunqBase` at the P's tail is the slot array itself.
//
// ⛔ **THERE IS NO LENGTH WORD, AND 0x10 IS THEREFORE STILL RESERVED.** A LINKED-LIST queue needs a `len`
// beside its head/tail because its length cannot be computed; a ring's is `tail - head`, and a third word
// maintained beside two that already answer the question is a fact stored twice — which is exactly how a
// queue comes to disagree with itself. It stays reserved rather than reused so the offset never forks.
//
// ⚠ NEITHER IS EXPORTED: *"does this ring hold anything?"* is asked through `emitSchedAnyRunnable` and
// `__sched_any_work`, because where the ring keeps its counters and what makes it empty belong to the file
// that owns the P.
let POffRunqHead = 0x00 as ByteOffset
let POffRunqTail = 0x08 as ByteOffset
let POffReservedLocalQueueLen = 0x10 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **A RESERVED `currentGt` SLOT — the running green thread lives on the M (`MOffCurrentGt`).** A global
// cannot say *"the thread THIS M is running"* once there is more than one M, and neither can the P: the
// green thread executing inside a kernel call belongs to the M that is blocked there, not to the processor
// a handoff is about to give away, and the shim reads that word again AFTER the call to restore the TIB —
// so a second M adopting the P in between would answer the first M's shim with its own green thread.
//
// It stays reserved rather than reused, so nothing else's value ever lands at this offset.
let POffReservedCurrentGt = 0x18 as ByteOffset

// This processor's index in `__sched_procs`, `0 .. numProcs-1`. It is also the shard index the sharded
// allocator will key its mcache by, which is why it
// is an INDEX rather than a pointer or a thread id.
//
// EXPORTED because that day arrived: the sharded allocator reads it to pick its mcache row.
export let POffId = 0x20 as ByteOffset

// The xorshift state a work-stealing P picks its victim with — its first reader arrived with the stealing
// rounds in `__sched_find_runnable`. Seeded to `id + 1` at init, because a zero xorshift state is a FIXED
// POINT: a P seeded 0 would draw 0 for ever and always try to steal from P[0].
let POffRng = 0x28 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **A RESERVED PARK-FLAG SLOT, AND NOTHING HERE HAS A TWIN OF IT.** It would be one half of a Dekker
// pair: 1 meaning *"this M has published that it is about to block on `wakeEvent`"*,
// written to 1 by the parking M with a `lock cmpxchg` (the LOCK prefix being the StoreLoad fence) and taken
// 1 -> 0 by whichever enqueuer won the right to signal.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE MECHANISM IS ABSENT, NOT RELOCATED.** Such a pair is needed only where the park publishes its
// intention OUTSIDE any lock and then re-searches, so *"is this M idle?"* and *"has this M gone to sleep yet?"* had to be
// answerable by a racing enqueuer through one word. With the idle state on the M, the whole park —
// releasing the processor, the final look at every queue, and joining the idle list — happens inside ONE
// `__sched_lock` critical section (`buildSchedLoop`'s park), and so does the whole of `wakep`. Two parties
// serialised by a mutex do not need a Dekker protocol between them, and **a flag beside the list would be a
// second idle mechanism**, with nothing making the two agree.
//
// ⇒ the missed-wakeup argument is *"the re-search and the list-join are one transaction, and `wakep` takes
// the same lock"*. `buildSchedLoop`'s park states it as an interleaving argument over the two critical
// sections, and the auto-reset event (`MOffParkEvent`) covers the only window left, between the release and
// the wait itself.
let POffReservedIdleFlag = 0x30 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **A RESERVED PER-P WAKE-EVENT SLOT — the park event lives on the M (`MOffParkEvent`).** An M that
// reached its wait handle through the processor it held would have none while holding no P. Reserved
// rather than reused for `POffReservedCurrentGt`'s reason — nothing else's value may land at this offset.
let POffReservedWakeEvent = 0x38 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **A RESERVED WORKER-THREAD-HANDLE SLOT — the handle lives on the M (`MOffOsThreadHandle`), AND THIS
// SLOT IS A LANDMINE.** A handle here would name the M that CLAIMED this P, and `__sched_stop_workers`
// would join by walking `__sched_procs`. Both facts hold only while a P belongs to one M for that M's life,
// which is not true here — after which the walk
// would join a thread that no longer has anything to do with this processor, succeed (the handle is still
// live), and return while the worker it meant to wait for was still stepping the allocation counters
// `__mm_leak_check` reads three calls later.
let POffReservedOsThreadHandle = 0x40 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE CLAIM WORD — `PStatusUnused` (0) or `PStatusActive` (1).** A P struct exists for every processor
// the program may ever run on, so *"does this P exist"* and *"is an M running on it"* are different
// questions and only this one answers the second.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS CLAIMED BY CAS UNTIL W213-C2 AND IS NOW A PLAIN STORE UNDER `__sched_lock`, AND THE CAS's
// PREMISE IS WHAT WENT AWAY RATHER THAN ITS DANGER.** `emitSchedClaimProc` existed because the whole
// wake/spawn phase ran with the run-queue lock DELIBERATELY RELEASED — the release being the StoreLoad
// fence the `idleFlag` protocol depended on — so several Ms reached one unclaimed P at once, and a
// read/test/store let two of them start workers on the SAME P: one allocator shard, two lockless writers,
// the same slot handed to both threads. With the
// idle state on the M there is no `idleFlag` protocol and therefore no fence to preserve, so `wakep` takes
// `__sched_lock` and the claim is one of the things it does while holding it. ⛔ **The hazard is unchanged
// and the discipline is not weaker: the word has exactly TWO writers, `emitSchedPidlePutLocked` and
// `emitSchedPidleClaimLocked`, both of which assert the lock in their names and neither of which can be
// reached without it. A third writer added outside the lock restores the bug in full.**
//
// ⭐ **AND IT IS NOT A SECOND COPY OF "IS THIS P ON `__sched_pidle`", THOUGH IT IS DERIVABLE FROM IT.** The
// list is a structure with an order and a head; this is a per-P word two readers want without walking one —
// `__sched_find_runnable`'s steal loop, which skips a victim no M is filling, and a debug agent reading
// this header at this offset. Both are stepped by the same two doors, in the same
// critical section, which is the shape `MOffSpinning` and `__sched_nmspinning` already have: a per-party bit
// beside a collective structure, with one door for the pair.
let POffStatus = 0x48 as ByteOffset
let PStatusUnused = 0 as ParsedInt
let PStatusActive = 1 as ParsedInt

// Reserved: part of the portable header, and nothing reads or writes it.
let POffReservedPendingWaiter = 0x50 as ByteOffset

// Go's `runnext` — the one green thread a P runs before consulting its local ring.
//
// ⛔⛔ **RESERVED AND DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT, AND THE REASON IS NO LONGER A QUOTATION — IT IS A MEASUREMENT
// ON THIS TREE (MC1), RE-TAKEN AT W213-C3 AND UNCHANGED.** `proc.go:7530-7541` refuses `runnext` outright
// when the runtime has no `sysmon`: *"A runnext goroutine shares the same time slice as the current
// goroutine … To prevent a ping-pong pair of goroutines from starving all others, we depend on sysmon to
// preempt long-running goroutines … If there is no sysmon, we must avoid runnext entirely or risk
// starvation."*
//
// ⛔⛔⛔ **THIS SLOT USED TO SAY IT WAS WAITING FOR *"W213's ARRIVAL"*, AND W213 HAS ARRIVED AND CHANGED
// NOTHING — WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT AND IS WHY THE STALE DEPENDENCY IS REPLACED HERE RATHER THAN TICKED
// OFF.** The compiler now HAS a `__sysmon` (W213-C3): it retakes a processor from a machine stuck in a blocking
// kernel call. **Read Go's sentence again and the mechanism it names is `preemptone` — PREEMPTION — not
// `sysmon` as such.** the compiler's monitor cannot preempt a green thread that is RUNNING Maxon code, because the compiler
// is cooperative by construction, so the safety mechanism `runqput`'s guard is asking for still does not
// exist.
//
// ⭐⭐ **MEASURED, NOT INFERRED — `scripts/multicore-stress/runnext-starvation-probe.maxon`, ONE PROGRAM, TWO
// COMPILERS, `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=1`, THREE RUNS EACH, ON A TREE WHOSE `__sysmon` IS LIVE:**
//
//   • **shipped (no `runnext`):** `bystander-ran` then `pingpong-done`, **3 of 3**.
//   • **with an experimental slot** — Go's own two sites, `__sched_runq_put` taking `p.runnext` when it is
//     free and `__sched_runq_get` consulting it ahead of the ring, and nothing else: `pingpong-done` then
//     `bystander-ran`, **3 of 3**, the bystander waiting out all 4,000 bounces.
//
// ⇒ **the readings are IDENTICAL to MC1's, taken before any monitor existed.** A monitor that can only
// retake a processor from a SYSCALL has nothing to say about two green threads that never make one, which is
// exactly the population Go's guard is about. The experimental build was reverted; the probe is what is left
// of it, and re-running it is how the next rung re-asks this question.
//
// ⇒ **the slot stays reserved, and it now waits for PREEMPTION rather than for W213.** That is a
// substantially larger rung — a cooperative runtime has no signal to deliver and no safe point to deliver it
// at — and it is the honest name of the dependency.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE QUESTION "DOES THE COMPILER'S COOPERATIVE MODEL MAKE GO'S OBJECTION INAPPLICABLE?" WAS ASKED
// PROPERLY AND ANSWERED NO.** `scripts/multicore-stress/runnext-starvation-probe.maxon` is two services that ready
// each other and a third that must still get a turn. **MEASURED at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=1`, one program, two
// compilers: on the shipped one the bystander runs FIRST on 3 of 3 runs; with a runnext slot published by
// `__sched_runq_put` and consulted by `__sched_runq_get` — Go's own two sites — the bystander runs LAST on
// 3 of 3, after all 4,000 bounces.**
//
// ⛔ **THE 61-SCHEDULE FAIRNESS CHECK DOES NOT RESCUE IT, AND THAT IS THE PART THAT LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD.**
// `GtFairnessInterval` consults the GLOBAL QUEUE; a starved thread that reached a P's LOCAL RING is never
// in it, and nothing but a ring overflow ever puts one there. So the one mechanism the compiler has that Go's
// argument does not account for is aimed at a different queue from the one that starves.
//
// ⇒ **and it stays reserved for a SAFETY MECHANISM rather than for a producer.** The throughput case for it
// is real (a just-readied thread staying on the M that readied it is the direct cure for cross-M stealing),
// which is exactly why it may not be taken on the throughput case alone.
let POffRunnext = 0x58 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **A RESERVED FREE-LIST PAIR — the GT-struct free list is one list, not one per P (`GtFreePutName`).**
// Reserved, not reused, for `POffReservedCurrentGt`'s reason.
let POffReservedFreeListHead = 0x60 as ByteOffset
let POffReservedFreeListLen = 0x68 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **A RESERVED SYSCALL-STACK SLOT — the scratch stack lives on the M (`MOffSystemStackSP`), AND THIS
// SLOT IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT FOR THAT.** ONE GLOBAL REGION
// (`__gt_system_stack_top`) whose safety invariant was prose: *"single-M runs one thing at a time"*. Multi-M
// falsified that by definition — two Ms inside shimmed calls would both set RSP to the same top and write the
// same 64 KB — and the failure shape is silent interleaved corruption, not a clean fault. G1's cure was to
// give each P its own, which makes the invariant *"an M can only reach its own P"*.
//
// ⛔ **THAT CURE IS EXACTLY AS SOUND AS THE SENTENCE UNDER IT, AND W213 IS THE RUNG THAT FALSIFIES THE
// SENTENCE.** Once a P can be taken from an M blocked in a kernel call, "its own P" stops naming one M —
// and the two Ms are back on one 64 KB region with G1's failure shape restored in full. The stack belongs to
// the M that is EXECUTING, which is what C1 makes it. Reserved, not reused, for `POffReservedCurrentGt`'s
// reason.
let POffReservedSystemStackSP = 0x70 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE HEAD OF THIS P's MPSC REMOTE-FREE QUEUE — a Treiber stack, and it is LIVE.** An M that frees a
// slot whose span another P owns CAS-pushes it here, and the owner drains it on its next slow path
// (`SlabRuntime.maxon`'s header for the mechanism, and its two readers at the push and the drain).
//
// ⛔ **THIS NOTE SAID "RESERVED AND LEFT ZERO — the compiler's slab has one shard and no ownership gate" UNTIL THE
// W212 REVIEW, AND S5 HAD FALSIFIED IT.** The sharding landed, gave the slot its two callers, and left the
// sentence here saying the opposite of this file's own header three screens up — one fact written twice,
// with the second copy rotting. A W213 author reading only this line would conclude a P carries no
// cross-thread state at all, which is exactly the premise P migration must NOT be built on.
export let POffRemoteFreeHead = 0x78 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THIS P's SCHEDULE COUNTER — Go's `schedtick`, and the ONLY thing the fairness check reads.** One up
// per green thread `__sched_find_runnable` hands back, and every 61st of them consults the GLOBAL queue
// before the local ring. Written by the owner alone, so a plain load/add/store: a thief has no reason to
// know how often its victim has scheduled.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS WHERE THE SHARED HEADER ENDS, AT 0x80.** Everything from here on is this file's own tail, which
// grafts onto nothing.
let POffSchedTick = 0x80 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **HOW MANY GREEN THREADS THIS P HAS STOLEN — the reading `__Builtins.schedStealCount()` sums.** It is a
// per-P word and not a `.data` counter for the reason the whole rung is arranged around: a `.data` word is
// visible in every golden's `data {` section, so one added here would move the emitted image of every
// green-thread program in the corpus to carry a number nothing but a torture harness reads. Owner-written,
// like `schedTick`, and summed across `__sched_procs` by `__sched_steal_count`.
let POffStealCount = POffSchedTick + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY TIMES THIS PROCESSOR HAS BEEN RETAKEN FROM A MACHINE STUCK IN A KERNEL CALL — the reading
// `__Builtins.schedRetakeCount()` sums, and the one way a program can observe that a HANDOFF really happened
// rather than that its output merely came out in a better order (W213-C3).**
//
// ⚠ **A PER-P WORD AND NOT A `.data` COUNTER, FOR `POffStealCount`'s REASON EXACTLY** — a `.data` word appears
// in the `data {` section of every green-thread golden in the corpus, and this is per-processor state. It sits
// beside the steal count because the two are the same shape asked of the same array, which is why they are
// summed by ONE parameterised body (`buildSchedPerProcSum`) rather than by two copies of one loop.
//
// ⚠ **SYSMON IS ITS ONLY WRITER, SO IT IS A PLAIN LOAD/ADD/STORE.** There is one sysmon thread; nothing else
// steps it, and a reader that wants it exact reads it after its work has finished, which is when it is.
let POffRetakeCount = POffStealCount + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐⭐ **HOW MANY SLOTS HAVE BEEN FREED ONTO THIS PROCESSOR'S REMOTE-FREE QUEUE BY SOMEBODY ELSE — the
// reading `__Builtins.slabRemoteFreeCount()` sums, and the ONE way a program can observe that the
// cross-processor free road was TAKEN rather than merely built.** `multicore-stress/service-torture` and
// `multicore-stress/service-fanin-torture` push thousands of heap boxes across machines in each direction, and until
// this slot existed nothing anywhere read a number that would move if that road stopped being taken: the
// sharded allocator's remote arm was EXERCISED but not OBSERVED, so a regression that quietly routed every
// free down the local arm would have left both tortures and the whole suite green.
//
// ⚠ **A PER-P WORD AND NOT A `.data` COUNTER, FOR `POffStealCount`'s REASON EXACTLY** — a `.data` word
// appears in the `data {` section of every green-thread golden in the corpus, and this is per-processor
// state. It sits beside the two counters above because it is the same shape asked of the same array, which
// is why all three are summed by ONE parameterised body (`buildSchedPerProcSum`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS CREDITED TO THE **TARGET** P — THE ONE WHOSE QUEUE RECEIVES THE SLOT — AND NOT TO THE FREEING
// P. THE CHOICE IS FORCED, NOT A PREFERENCE.** The sum is over every P either way, so the total is the same
// number whenever both parties have a P; what differs is the case where the FREEING side has none.
// `SlabRuntime.emitSlabCurrentP` answers `MspanOwningPNone` for three real situations — before
// `__sched_init_procs` has run, on an OS thread holding no M (the IOCP completion thread for its whole life),
// and on an M holding no P — and every one of those can free a box whose span a live P owns. That IS a
// cross-processor free and it takes this road; crediting the pusher would have to store through a sentinel,
// so it would need a guard, and the guard would DROP exactly those frees from the count. The target, by
// contrast, is a live P by construction: `buildSlabFree` has already ruled out both `MspanOwningPNone` and
// `MspanOwningPParked` before it reaches the push. ⇒ target-credit needs no branch and loses no free.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THAT IS WHY IT IS THE ONE PER-P COUNTER STEPPED WITH AN `atomicRmw` RATHER THAN A PLAIN
// LOAD/ADD/STORE.** Its two neighbours are single-writer — a P counts its OWN steals, and sysmon is the only
// party that retakes — so each may use a plain read-modify-write. This one is MPSC by construction, the same
// many-producers property that makes the queue beside it a Treiber stack: every other machine in the process
// may be crediting one P at the same instant, and a plain step would lose exactly the counts a contended run
// exists to produce. One `lock xadd` on a path that already executes a CAS loop.
// ⚠ All three step through `MmRuntime.emitFieldAccumulate`, whose `multiM` argument HAS NO DEFAULT for
// `emitGlobalAccumulate`'s stated reason: the wrong answer here is a count that is merely TOO SMALL, which no
// suite, no leak gate and no fixpoint can see — so the sequence is shared and the decision is not.
// EXPORTED, unlike its two neighbours, because its one WRITER is in another file: the credit happens at
// `SlabRuntime.emitSlabRemotePush`, where the slot is handed over. The sum stays here with the walk.
export let POffRemoteFreeCount = POffRetakeCount + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_pidle`'s LINK — Go's `p.link` as `pidleput`/`pidleget` use it (`proc.go:7425`/`:7454`),
// and the slot that makes *"a processor with no machine"* a state this runtime can write down (W213-C2).**
//
// ⛔⛔ **A PARKED MACHINE HOLDS NO PROCESSOR, AND `handoffp` CANNOT BE BUILT WITHOUT THAT.** `handoffp`'s last
// step is literally `pidleput(pp, 0)` — *park the P, not the M* — so an idle processor has to be a state
// distinct from a busy one, with somewhere to put it. Without it, a green thread that blocks in a kernel call
// takes a whole processor into the kernel with it, which is the cost `specs/sched-syscall-handoff.md`
// measures.
//
// ⭐ **THE LIST IS LIFO AND THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE**, which is Go's too: the most recently parked P is the
// one whose allocator shard and ring are still warm in this core's caches, so handing it back first is the
// cheaper pairing. Nothing depends on the order for correctness — every P is interchangeable to a scheduler
// that steals.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE PAIRING MAKES THE STEAL COUNT BIMODAL, AND THE LOW MODE IS A WIN, NOT A STALL.** A run in which
// `__sched_startm` hands work back to the machine that just released the processor keeps it on one core and
// steals almost nothing — and the low-steal runs are the fast ones. ⚠ **It is VARIANCE and not a speedup to
// quote**, and a bimodal population needs a sample large enough to see both modes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **GO'S TWO MASKS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED, AND THE REASON IS THAT NEITHER HAS A SUBJECT HERE.**
// `proc.go:7425` clears `timerpMask` only when the P's timer count is 0 and `proc.go:7454` sets it BEFORE
// unlinking — *"Timer may get added at any time now"* — because a Go P owns its own timer heap, so a P
// parked while holding timers would strand them until somebody swept it. **the compiler's timer store is GLOBAL**
// (`__gt_timer_count`, one `.data` word, walked by `__gt_timer_check`), so an idle P holds no timer to
// strand and there is no per-P bit for the ordering to be about. `idlepMask` is the other half of the same
// structure — a bitmap letting `stealWork` skip idle Ps without touching them — and the compiler's steal loop reads
// `POffStatus` on the P it is already addressing, which answers the same question with nothing to keep in
// step. ⇒ **the ORDERING those two comments protect is preserved by there being nothing to order**, and
// porting the masks would have been two words maintained beside facts that already answer for them.
let POffPidleNext = POffRemoteFreeCount + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐ **HOW MANY GT STRUCTS THE FREE LIST HAS SERVED TO SPAWNS ON THIS P — the reading
// `__Builtins.schedGtRecycleCount()` sums.** Stepped by `__gt_free_get` on every allocation the list answered,
// owner-written like `schedTick`, and a per-P word for `POffStealCount`'s reason.
let POffGtRecycleCount = POffPidleNext + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE THAT LAST RELEASED THIS PROCESSOR ONTO `__sched_pidle`, OR 0 — Go's `p.oldm`
// (`runtime2.go:786`).** `__sched_startm` asks for that machine first (`mgetSpecific`), so a processor goes
// back to the thread whose core last ran its ring and its allocator shard; any other idle machine will do
// when that one is busy.
//
// ⭐ **A PLAIN POINTER WHERE GO NEEDS A WEAK ONE**, because an M is never freed here: the word always names a
// real M struct, and the only question a reader asks of it — is that machine on `__sched_midle`? — is answered
// under the lock by `emitSchedMidleHolds`. Written by `emitSchedReleaseProcLocked` and read by
// `__sched_startm`, both under `__sched_lock`.
let POffOldM = POffGtRecycleCount + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE HOLDING THIS PROCESSOR, OR 0 — Go's `p.m`.** Written beside `M->currentP` by the two doors
// that move a processor (`emitSchedAdoptProc`, `emitSchedReleaseProcLocked`), and cleared by the retake that wins
// it. Its one reader is `__sched_retake`, which finds the machines worth asking about by walking the processors
// and reads it unlocked: that function's header argues why a stale answer is only a candidate.
let POffM = POffOldM + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **THE RING'S 256 SLOTS, AT THE P's TAIL.** Go's `runq [256]guintptr` (`runtime2.go:807`). FIXED
// rather than grown: the overflow has somewhere to go (the global queue), so a full ring is a routing
// decision and never an error, and a growable ring would need a lock on the one path this whole design
// exists to take a lock OFF.
//
// It sits at the TAIL because everything above it is either the portable header or an 8-byte scalar, and a
// 2 KB array in the middle would push every one of them past an offset a debug agent already knows.
let RunqSlots = 256 as ParsedInt

// `counter mod RunqSlots` as a mask, which is what makes the slot computation an `and` rather than an
// `idiv`. It is `RunqSlots - 1` and is only a mask because `RunqSlots` is a power of two — stated here so
// that a future `RunqSlots` that is not one fails in ONE place rather than addressing the wrong slot.
let RunqIndexMask = RunqSlots - 1

// ⭐ **HALF A RING — the overflow batch, the steal grab and the global batch are all this number.** Written
// as a SHIFT and not as `RunqSlots / 2` because `/` in this language answers a FLOAT whatever its operands
// are (`n / 2` of two ints is `128.0`), and a constant that has to be truncated at each of its four uses
// would be the same fact converted four times.
let RunqHalfSlots = RunqSlots shr 1

let POffRunqBase = POffM + (PointerSlotBytes as ByteOffset)

// The portable header, the inline GT, the tail scalars and the ring. See the layout header.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THE BYTE COUNT THAT USED TO BE WRITTEN HERE IS GONE ON PURPOSE — IT HAD DRIFTED TWICE.** It read
// *"0x990 = 2448"* from the day the compiler's GT struct was 0xF8, EC10 grew that struct to 0x118 and nothing here
// moved, and it was corrected to *"0x9B8 = 2488"* — which A1's quiescence words then falsified one rung
// later, and which W213-C2 falsified again by taking the whole idle tail off this struct.
// Every offset above is derived from `GtStructSize`, so the CODE has been right the whole time: a prose
// number beside a derived one is the same fact written twice, and this is the copy that rots. It is not
// written down again.
let PStructSize = ((POffRunqBase as ParsedInt) + RunqSlots * PointerSlotBytes) as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **HOW OFTEN THE GLOBAL QUEUE IS CONSULTED FIRST — Go's 61 (`proc.go:3457`).** Without it two green
// threads that keep
// re-readying each other own the local ring outright and everything the global queue holds starves. A ring
// OVERFLOW is what routinely puts work there, so this is not a corner case: it is the other half of the
// overflow.
//
// ⚠ **The compiler TESTS IT AFTER THE INCREMENT WHERE GO TESTS IT BEFORE**, so the first check falls on the 61st
// schedule rather than on the 0th. Go's tick is bumped in `execute` and read in `findRunnable`, which makes
// its very first schedule a global check; here that would be a special case with no reader, since the global
// queue is empty before anything has overflowed into it. The property both spell is the one that matters —
// nothing waits in the global queue for more than 61 schedules.
let GtFairnessInterval = 61 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **FOUR ROUNDS OF STEALING BEFORE A P GIVES UP — Go's `stealTries` (`proc.go:3849`).** More than one
// because a victim that is momentarily empty may be about to publish; bounded because a P that finds nothing
// four times over should park and let an enqueuer wake it rather than burn a core.
let StealRounds = 4 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE MOST GREEN THREADS ONE GRAB TAKES OFF THE GLOBAL QUEUE — half a ring, Go's first term
// (`proc.go:3492`, `globrunqgetbatch(int32(len(pp.runq)) / 2)`).** The grab is `min(this, size,
// size/numProcs + 1)` (`globrunqgetbatch`, `proc.go:7345`): the last term leaves a share of a queue several
// processors are draining for the others, so they need not steal it back. `size` is `__sched_runq_size`.
let GlobalBatchMax = RunqHalfSlots

// ==========================================================================
// The `.data` slots
// ==========================================================================

// The `TlsAlloc` slot index, kept so a teardown rung can `TlsFree` it, and read once at adoption to publish
// this OS thread's P. The per-read path never touches it — it reads the precomputed byte offset below.
let SchedTlsIndexLabel = b"__sched_tls_index"

// ⭐⭐ **THE PRECOMPUTED TEB BYTE OFFSET, `index * 8 + 0x1480` — what makes the current-P read TWO LOADS AND
// NO CALL.** Windows keeps a thread's first 64 TLS slots inline in its TEB at `TEB + 0x1480`, and `gs:`
// addresses the TEB, so `mov reg, gs:[thisOffset]` IS `TlsGetValue(index)` with no call, no range check and
// no clobbered argument register.
//
// ⚠ **ZERO MEANS "THE SCHEDULER HAS NOT INITIALIZED YET", AND EVERY HAND-ASSEMBLED READER MUST TEST IT.**
// Before `__sched_init_procs` runs, `gs:[0]` is `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` — a non-null pointer on Windows — so
// reading through an unchecked zero offset hands back garbage and the next field load faults. The prologue
// stack guard runs inside `main` before any spawn, so that is a reachable path and not a defensive one.
export let SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel = b"__sched_tls_teb_offset"

// The base of the `P*` array — `numProcs` machine words, one pointer per processor.
let SchedProcsLabel = b"__sched_procs"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE M ROSTER — Go's `allm`, and the structure W213-C1 recorded as missing (W213-C2).** The head of
// an append-only intrusive list threaded through `MOffAllNext`; every M pushes itself at birth
// (`emitSchedAllocM`, under the lock, by whoever creates it) and nothing ever removes one, because an M is
// never freed.
//
// ⭐ **ITS READERS ARE THE WALKS THAT ASK A QUESTION ABOUT MACHINES**: the teardown's signal and its join
// (`__sched_stop_workers`) and the wake count (`__sched_park_wake_count`) — *"wake every thread"*, *"wait for
// every thread"*, *"how often has any thread woken"*. The P array cannot answer any of them: a worker parked
// with no processor is on no P at all.
//
// ⭐ **A LIST AND NOT AN ARRAY, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE RUNG.** `SchedRuntime.maxon`'s two scans used
// to start at index 1 bounded by `numProcs`, which capped the M count at the P count — measured as
// `schedMaxActiveWorkers()` reading exactly 1/2/4 at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` 1/2/4. `handoffp` must be able to
// start an M **while the blocked M still exists**, which is more Ms than Ps, and an array sized by the
// processor count is precisely the shape that cannot express it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`underSchedLock` NAMES THE WRITES; TWO WALKS READ IT UNLOCKED AND ARE THE CLASS'S ENUMERATED
// EXCEPTION — see `MultiMSharing.underSchedLock`.** Every push is under the lock. `__sched_stop_workers`'s
// JOIN walk deliberately releases first (it may not block on a thread while holding a lock that thread
// needs), and `__sched_park_wake_count` is a program's query, which takes no lock for a reading.
//
// ⛔ **THE UNLOCKED WALKS ARE SOUND BY A PROPERTY OF THE PUSH, NOT OF THE WALK.** `emitSchedListPushLocked`
// stores the joining machine's `MOffAllNext` BEFORE it stores the head, so a walker that reads the head can
// only ever reach a machine whose own link word is already published; and the list is APPEND-ONLY over
// structs that are never freed, so a cursor cannot be led off it by a concurrent mutation. The only thing a
// racing `__sched_startm` can do is prepend a machine the walk does not see, and the teardown cannot race a
// machine into existence at all, because `__sched_startm` reads the shutdown flag under the same lock that
// sets it.
let SchedAllMLabel = b"__sched_allm"

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY MACHINES ARE ON `__sched_allm` — Go's `sched.mnext - sched.nmfreed`, and the count the
// thread limit is checked against.** Stepped by `emitSchedRegisterMLocked`, the one door onto the roster, and
// never down, because an M is never freed.
//
// `underSchedLock`: its writer and its one reader (`__sched_startm`'s spawn arm) both hold the lock.
let SchedMcountLabel = b"__sched_mcount"

// ⭐ **THE MOST MACHINES A PROCESS MAY CREATE — Go's `sched.maxmcount` (10000, `proc.go`'s `schedinit`).** A
// machine is an OS thread with its own 64 KB syscall stack and event, and none is ever reclaimed, so a
// runaway spawn arm would otherwise exhaust the address space and fail somewhere unrelated. Reaching it aborts
// with `RuntimeAbort.schedulerThreadExhaustion`, which is Go's `throw("thread exhaustion")`.
let SchedMaxMachines = 10000 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE IDLE-M LIST — Go's `sched.midle`, doubly linked through `MOffIdleNext`/`MOffIdlePrev`.** An M
// with nothing to run puts itself here and blocks on its own `MOffParkEvent`; a `startm` takes one off and
// hands it a processor. Its doors are its own rather than the shared singly linked pair, because two
// removals take a machine out of the MIDDLE — `mgetSpecific` and a signalled machine unlinking itself — and
// the back-link is what makes both O(1). All of them require `__sched_lock` (Go: `assertLockHeld` at `mput`,
// `mget` and `mgetSpecific` alike).
//
// ⭐⭐ **A LIST RATHER THAN A SCAN OF THE PROCESSORS, BECAUSE A PARKED MACHINE HOLDS NO PROCESSOR TO BE FOUND
// BY.** Membership is decided under the one lock, with no `idleFlag` beside it (`POffReservedIdleFlag`), so
// there is no fence for a lock-free scan to preserve.
//
// `underSchedLock`, for `SchedAllMLabel`'s reason and one more: joining this list and taking the final look
// at the run queues are ONE transaction, which is what makes the park free of a missed wakeup.
let SchedMidleLabel = b"__sched_midle"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE IDLE-P LIST — Go's `sched.pidle`, threaded through `POffPidleNext`, and the list `handoffp`
// parks a processor on.** A processor whose M has parked, or which no M has ever taken.
// `emitSchedPidlePutLocked` (Go's `pidleput`) and `emitSchedPidleGetLocked` (`pidleget`) are its only doors:
// each moves membership, `POffStatus` and `__sched_npidle` together in one critical section, so the three
// cannot disagree.
let SchedPidleLabel = b"__sched_pidle"

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY PROCESSORS ARE ON `__sched_pidle` — Go's `sched.npidle`.** A count beside the list because
// its readers want the number without walking one: the spinner cap in `__sched_find_runnable`
// (`2 * nmspinning < numProcs - npidle`) and `__Builtins.schedIdleProcessorCount()`. Stepped only inside the
// two pidle doors.
//
// `underSchedLock`, and both readers are enumerated unlocked reads (`MultiMSharing.underSchedLock`).
let SchedNpidleLabel = b"__sched_npidle"

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY GREEN THREADS THE GLOBAL RUN QUEUE HOLDS — Go's `sched.runq.size`.** The global batch's
// `size/numProcs + 1` term reads it (`GlobalBatchMax`). Stepped inside `GtRuntime`'s `__gt_enqueue` and
// `__gt_dequeue`, the only two functions that mutate that queue, so no caller can move the queue without
// moving the count. EXPORTED for those two.
//
// `underSchedLock`: both doors run under the lock, and so does the one read.
export let SchedRunqSizeLabel = b"__sched_runq_size"

let SchedGtFreeHeadLabel = b"__gt_gfree_head"

// ⭐ **HOW MANY Ps EXIST — the bound every scan walks, and the ceiling on live Ms.** TWO words
// (`__sched_num_procs` beside `__sched_max_procs`) are needed only where a P can be retired without
// shrinking the array. There is ONE here, because nothing retires a P and a second word with no writer is a
// variable's clothes on a constant — the same argument `CpuParallelRuntime` made against a `.data` slot for a `ret 1`.
// The day a P is retired, the pair splits here, in one place.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS ALSO THE ANSWER `__Builtins.schedProcessorCount()` GIVES, WHICH IS WHY IT IS EXPORTED.** That
// query is the processor count's only observable consequence that does not depend on how the work happened
// to be scheduled: `emitResolveMaxProcs` settles this word to `osCpuCount`, or to `min(requested,
// osCpuCount)` when `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` names a count, before a single green thread runs — so reading it is
// reading the resolution itself. `CpuParallelRuntime` owns the query's body.
export let SchedNumProcsLabel = b"__sched_num_procs"

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY MACHINES ARE ON `__sched_midle` — Go's `sched.nmidle`.** Stepped by the list's two doors
// (`emitSchedMidlePutLocked`, `emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked`), so it is the list's length by construction;
// `__sched_checkdead` subtracts it from `__sched_mcount`.
//
// `underSchedLock`: both doors and the one reader hold the lock.
let SchedNmidleLabel = b"__sched_nmidle"

// ⭐⭐ **THE MAIN MACHINE** — the M the main OS thread became in `__sched_init_procs`. `main`'s completion and
// the exit drain's last debit signal its park event (`emitSchedSignalMainMachine`), because it is the machine
// whose loop is waiting for them. `sealedBeforeWorkers`: written once at bring-up.
let SchedMainMLabel = b"__sched_main_m"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHERE THE PROCESS IS IN ITS LIFE (`SchedPhase`) — THE ONE WORD `__sched_checkdead` DECIDES BY.**
// `underSchedLock`: every write is under the lock (`emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked`). Two reads are unlocked, both
// with acquire (`emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord`): the main machine's loop-top exit test, an enumerated arm-picking read
// (`MultiMSharing`), and `GtRuntime.__gt_owner_done`'s last debit, whose argument is at that function.
let SchedPhaseLabel = b"__sched_phase"

export enum SchedPhase
	// `main` has not completed. Every machine idle with nothing pending is a DEADLOCK:
	// `RuntimeAbort.schedulerDeadlock`.
	mainRunning = 0

	// `main` has completed (`GtRuntime.__gt_runner_done`) and the main machine is on its way through the exit
	// drain, which runs until `__gt_live_count` is zero. The last `__gt_owner_done` signals it.
	draining = 1

	// Every machine went idle during the drain with nothing pending: nothing that still owns a green thread can
	// ever run, so the drain ends and the leak gate reports the count (`greenThreadLeak`).
	stuck = 2
end 'SchedPhase'

// ⭐ **HOW MANY Ms ARE LIVE, AND THE HIGH-WATER MARK OF THAT COUNT.** The mark is what
// `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()` answers, which is the only way a program can observe that more than
// one M ever ran. Both are seeded to **1** in `.data` rather than by an initializer — see
// `schedRuntimeGlobals`.

let SchedActiveWorkersLabel = b"__sched_active_workers"
export let SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel = b"__sched_max_active_workers"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **HOW MANY Ms ARE ALREADY *LOOKING* FOR WORK — Go's `sched.nmspinning` (`proc.go:3228`), and the
// one number that turns a publish from a syscall into a load.**
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE COST IT REMOVES IS THE BULK OF MULTI-M's OVERHEAD**: one `SetEvent`/`WaitForSingleObject` round
// trip per publish, where the woken machine finds the queue already drained as often as not.
//
// ⭐ **THE RULE, WHICH IS GO'S: AT MOST ONE SPINNING M IS CREATED PER WAKEUP.** A publisher that sees a
// non-zero count does nothing at all — an M is already searching, and the *"delicate dance"* below
// guarantees that M's search follows this publish. `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s gate is the reader.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE DANCE IS THE WHOLE SUBTLETY AND BOTH HALVES ARE REQUIRED (`proc.go:3638-3715`).**
//
//   • **AN M GOING NON-SPINNING DECREMENTS *FIRST*, THEN RE-CHECKS EVERY WORK SOURCE** — the global queue and
//     every active processor's ring (`__sched_any_work`) — with a StoreLoad barrier in between: the decrement
//     is a locked read-modify-write and the re-check's first read is an acquire. The other order loses work
//     submitted between the check and the decrement: the publisher reads a count that is about to be zero,
//     skips the wake, and the M that would have found the work has already looked. ⇒ if a publisher observed
//     a non-zero count, the M it counted had not yet decremented when that read happened, so its re-check
//     strictly follows the publish and cannot miss it. `buildSchedLoop`'s `wspinrecheck` is the parking
//     machine's re-check.
//   • **AN M THAT *FINDS* WORK STOPS SEARCHING, SO IT OWES THE SEARCH TO SOMEBODY ELSE** — Go's
//     `resetspinning` calls `wakep` for exactly this. Without it a spinner that takes one thread off a queue
//     holding two leaves the second beside a dozen parked Ms until somebody looks again, and one published to a
//     busy machine's ring until that machine's thread yields. `buildSchedLoop`'s `wrecheck` is that call.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A RESERVATION THAT FOUND NO PROCESSOR IS THE ONE UNIT NO SPINNER ANSWERS FOR** — the dance's third
// party, and what `__sched_needspinning` is for. `__sched_wake_or_spawn` counts a unit in before it knows
// whether a processor is idle, and a publisher that reads that unit skips its wake; if no processor was idle,
// no machine takes the unit over and none re-checks on its behalf. The machine that must look is then one
// already holding a processor, and the flag is how it learns so.
//
// `atomicStep`: every mutation is `StdOp.atomicRmw` or `StdOp.atomicCas` — see `emitSchedSpinningTransition`
// and the gate.
let SchedSpinningLabel = b"__sched_nmspinning"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **"WORK MAY BE WAITING THAT NO SPINNER WILL FIND" — Go's `sched.needspinning` (`proc.go:3625`).** Raised,
// inside `__sched_lock`, by `__sched_startm` when a reserved wake starts nobody: no processor is idle, or the
// machine for one could not be created. While the reservation stood a publisher could read it and skip its
// wake, and the machine that must search is then one already holding a processor — possibly one on its way
// to release it WITHOUT having spun, whose park re-checks only the global queue. So a machine that parks with
// no spinning credit reads this in its park's critical section and, finding it raised, searches again as a
// spinner instead of releasing (`wpark`).
//
// Cleared by every machine that becomes a spinner (`SpinningTransition.becomeSpinning`), because a spinner
// re-checks every queue before it stops.
//
// `lastWriterWins`: one word, raised under the lock and cleared outside it. A raise lost to a concurrent clear
// loses nothing — the clearer is a spinner, and it re-checks every queue before it parks.
let SchedNeedSpinningLabel = b"__sched_needspinning"

// Set at teardown so a parked worker leaves its loop instead of blocking for ever. ⚠ The worker loop reads
// it TWICE, because the second read is half of the missed-wakeup argument rather than a duplicate of the
// first. See `buildSchedLoop`.
//
// ⭐ **IT HAS A WRITER AS OF SV1 (`__sched_stop_workers`), WHICH IS WHY IT MOVED CLASS.** It was
// `sealedBeforeWorkers` on the honest ground that NOTHING wrote it; the teardown does, and it writes it
// AFTER the workers exist, so that class would now be a false statement. `lastWriterWins` is the true one
// and the discipline it names is enough: a single monotone 0→1 store by the main thread, one aligned word,
// and a reader that gets the stale 0 parks once more, and the teardown's signal ends that park.
let SchedShutdownFlagLabel = b"__sched_shutdown_flag"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **HAS THE SYSTEM MONITOR THREAD BEEN STARTED? — the one-shot that keeps a program which never
// schedules a GREEN THREAD from carrying an extra OS thread (W213-C3).**
//
// ⛔ **THE TRIGGER IS `__sched_wake_or_spawn` AND NOT `__sched_init_procs`, AND IT IS NOT `__sched_startm`'s
// SPAWN ARM EITHER — BOTH ALTERNATIVES WERE TRIED AND BOTH ARE WRONG.** `__sched_init_procs` runs for every
// `async`-only program too, and an `async` frame is a COROUTINE that no worker M can ever run (see this file's
// header), so that placement would start a monitor for a process that can never have a second machine to
// monitor. The spawn arm is the opposite error: at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=1` there is never an idle processor to
// spawn onto, so `__sched_startm` places nobody and the monitor would never start **on exactly the
// configuration that needs it most** — which is `specs/sched-syscall-handoff.md`'s `procs: 1` case.
//
// ⇒ **the honest trigger is *"this program has published a strand token"***, and that is precisely what
// reaching `__sched_wake_or_spawn` means: a wake is owed only when `GtRuntime.__gt_ready_locked` published one.
//
// ⚠ **THE READ BEFORE THE CAS IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION OF THE CAS — IT IS WHAT KEEPS THE PUBLISH PATH FREE OF A
// `lock` PREFIX.** `__sched_wake_or_spawn` runs once per publish, which `service-torture` measures in the
// hundreds of thousands; a `lock cmpxchg` there to re-answer a question that is settled after the first
// microsecond of the process is the shape `SchedSpinningLabel`'s gate exists to avoid. A stale 0 read costs a
// lost CAS and nothing else.
//
// `atomicStep`: the only mutation is the `atomicCas` at that gate.
let SchedSysmonStartedLabel = b"__sched_sysmon_started"

// ⭐ **HOW LONG A MACHINE MUST HAVE BEEN INSIDE ONE KERNEL CALL BEFORE ITS PROCESSOR IS TAKEN — Go's 10 ms
// (`proc.go`'s `retake`), carried unchanged because the quantity it trades off is the same one.** Below it the
// handoff costs more than the stall it removes (a machine started, a search run and a park paid for a call
// that was about to return); above it a genuinely blocked machine holds a processor for longer than it needs
// to. It is compared against `__gt_now_ns`, which is why it is written in nanoseconds.
let SysmonRetakeAfterNs = 10 * 1000 * 1000 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE MONITOR'S SLEEP BETWEEN LAPS — Go's ladder (`proc.go`'s `sysmon`): 20 µs, doubling once fifty laps
// in a row have retaken nothing, capped at 10 ms.** A short first interval notices a blocking call soon after
// it starts; the back-off keeps a process that is not blocking from paying a thread wake every 20 µs. The
// sleep is `NetpollVerb.sleepOnTimer`, the one wait fine enough for the floor on every lane.
let SysmonMinSleepUs = 20 as ParsedInt
let SysmonMaxSleepUs = 10 * 1000 as ParsedInt

// How many consecutive laps that retook nothing start the back-off. Go's 50, and the same reasoning: a process
// that has not blocked in fifty laps is one whose next block can afford to be noticed a little later. Any
// retake snaps the interval straight back to `SysmonMinSleepUs`.
let SysmonIdlePollsBeforeBackoff = 50 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **HOW LONG THE POLLER MAY GO UNPOLLED BEFORE THE MONITOR LOOKS ITSELF — Go's 10 ms.** While every machine
// is busy nobody waits in the poller, so an event there would wait for a machine to go idle; the monitor takes
// a non-blocking look instead (`__np_poll(0)`).
let SysmonPollStaleNs = 10 * 1000 * 1000 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE MONITOR'S DEEP SLEEP — Go's `sysmonwait`/`sysmonnote`.** With every processor idle there is no
// processor to retake and no machine to preempt, so the monitor blocks on its own event rather than lapping, and
// the first `pidleget` after that (`emitSchedPidleGetLocked`) wakes it: a processor becoming busy is what could
// make a retake worth taking again. The event is the monitor's, created on its own thread; the flag says it is
// asleep on it. Both are `underSchedLock`: set, cleared and read inside the lock `pidleget` runs under.
let SchedSysmonEventLabel = b"__sched_sysmon_event"
let SchedSysmonWaitLabel = b"__sched_sysmon_wait"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHEN THE POLLER LAST RETURNED, OR 0 WHILE A MACHINE IS BLOCKED IN IT — Go's `sched.lastpoll`.** The
// idle tail claims the poller by finding this non-zero and storing 0; the machine that claimed it stores the
// clock when its wait ends. So *"is anybody polling?"* is one load, and at most one machine blocks in the
// poller at a time. Seeded with the clock at bring-up, because 0 would read as *"somebody is polling"*.
//
// `underSchedLock`: every write is inside the lock — the claim and the release in the idle tail, and the
// monitor's stale-poll claim. The unlocked reads pick an arm and are enumerated at
// `MultiMSharing.underSchedLock`.
let SchedLastPollLabel = b"__sched_lastpoll"

// ⭐⭐ **THE DEADLINE THE BLOCKED POLLER WILL WAKE AT — Go's `sched.pollUntil`**, published with the claim and
// cleared when the wait ends. A deadline armed earlier than it breaks the poller's wait
// (`__sched_wake_netpoller`); one armed later needs nothing, because the poller will look again when it wakes.
// `underSchedLock`, for `__sched_lastpoll`'s reason.
let SchedPollUntilLabel = b"__sched_poll_until"

// ⭐ **THE MACHINES WAITING FOR A PROCESSOR TO RESUME THE GREEN THREAD THEY HOLD** — back from a kernel call
// whose processor `__sysmon` took (`__sched_syscall_regain`). A LIFO through `MOffRegainNext`; `pidleput` hands
// a released processor to the head before it would idle it. `underSchedLock`: every door holds the lock.
let SchedRegainHeadLabel = b"__sched_regain_head"

// ⭐ **HOW LONG THE POLLER WAITS WHILE A CHILD IS PARKED — the interim until children join the poller as a
// source of their own.** Nothing blocks on a parked child, so the poller caps its wait here and reaps after every
// wait.
let ChildPollNs = 1000 * 1000 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE LONGEST ONE POLL WAITS — Go's cap of 1e9 ms.** Every lane's wait takes its timeout as a 32-bit count, and
// `epoll_wait` reads it SIGNED, so a deadline further out than ~24.8 days would wrap into a wait for ever; the
// poller wakes at the cap and waits again for the rest.
let PollerMaxWaitMs = 1000 * 1000 * 1000 as ParsedInt

// ⛔⛔⛔ **A TARGET FORK IN THE SCHEDULER — READ THIS BEFORE THE FOUR SITES THAT ASK IT.**
//
// The syscall bracket announces *"this machine is about to be unreachable; its processor may be taken"* and,
// on the way out, *"give me a processor back if mine was taken"*. A lane that does not emit it leaves
// `MOffSyscallState` permanently zero: `__sched_retake`'s `rtmachine` arm declines every machine,
// `__sched_retake` always answers 0, and `__sysmon` is **an OS thread that wakes a hundred times a second to
// walk the processors and structurally cannot find anything**.
//
// ⇒ **the monitor and the bracket are gated together, and the gate is asked at FOUR sites that must agree**:
// `installSchedRuntime` (build the handoff tier at all), `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn` (the lazy start),
// `schedRuntimeGlobals` (the `.data` word the start CASes) and `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots` (the
// functions no Std caller reaches). A start without a global is a word nothing writes; a global without an
// install is a call to a function that was never built; an unrooted callee is a call fixup with nothing to
// resolve. ⚠ WHERE the bracket's two steps are emitted is a second, narrower question — see
// `targetCallsTheSyscallBracket` — and the install and the root ask it in the same two places.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE KEY IS `HostFacility.greenThreads`, WHICH IS THE ONE ANSWER THAT CANNOT BE WRONG HERE.** The
// bracket is emitted around blocking ops by the isel, and every op it wraps is a call into a scheduler that
// only a lane providing that facility has: the lanes that answer `true` (x64-windows, arm64-macOS,
// arm64-linux) are exactly the lanes `installSchedRuntime` runs on at all, and the lanes that answer `false`
// (x64-linux, wasm32-wasi) never reach this installer. Keying on the OS instead would get an arm64-WINDOWS
// lane wrong in the one direction that matters — `windows` would answer `true` before that lane had a P/M
// substrate to hand a processor to.
//
// ⭐ **IT ALSO KEEPS A LANE WITHOUT THE SUBSTRATE FREE OF THE MONITOR'S `.data` WORD.**
// `__sched_sysmon_started` would otherwise put a line in the `data {` block of every green-thread golden.
export function targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target Target) returns bool
	return targetProvidesFacility(target, facility: HostFacility.greenThreads)
end 'targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls'

// ⭐⭐ **WHETHER THIS LANE'S BRACKET IS A PAIR OF *CALLS*, WHICH IS THE ONE THING THAT DECIDES WHETHER
// `__sched_enter_syscall` AND `__sched_exit_syscall` EXIST.** x64-windows emits both steps INLINE inside its
// import shim, because there the bracket sits in a pre-call window where the argument registers are already
// laid out for the kernel and a `call` would destroy the frame it is about to read — so on that lane the pair
// would be two functions nothing reaches. The arm64 lanes emit them as calls at the isel's one dispatch door,
// which is the only site that covers a hand-assembled chunk, an inline import and a raw `svc` alike.
//
// ⚠ **KEYED ON THE ONE LANE THAT HAS SOMEWHERE ELSE TO PUT IT, WHICH IS THE IMPORT SHIM.** An IAT and the
// shim wrapped around it are an x64-windows mechanism; every other bracketing lane has nowhere else, so it
// brackets at the dispatch door. ⛔ **THE QUESTION IS WHICH LANE HAS SOMEWHERE ELSE TO PUT THE BRACKET, NEVER
// WHICH CPU IT RUNS ON.** A CPU test is the same set by accident and is silently wrong for a second x64 OS:
// that lane would install a `__sysmon` nothing ever announced a syscall to — a monitor that walks
// `__sched_allm` a hundred times a second and structurally cannot find anything, with a retake counter stuck
// at 0 and no error anywhere.
export function targetCallsTheSyscallBracket(target Target) returns bool
	return targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target) and not targetBracketsInsideTheImportShim(target)
end 'targetCallsTheSyscallBracket'

// The lane whose bracket is emitted INLINE, inside `X64GtRuntime.emitGtShimmedIatCall`, rather than as a pair
// of calls at the isel's dispatch door. There is exactly one, and it is named by the mechanism it has rather
// than by its CPU so that a second x64 OS cannot inherit the answer by sharing an instruction set.
function targetBracketsInsideTheImportShim(target Target) returns bool
	return target.cpu == CpuArch.x64 and target.os == Os.windows
end 'targetBracketsInsideTheImportShim'

// ⭐ **THE ONE LOCK OVER THE RUN QUEUE — a `.data` word holding a slab-allocated CRITICAL_SECTION.** It was
// `__io_lock`, allocated by `__io_init` and taken only when the program read a pipe, because the IOCP
// completion thread was then the run queue's only cross-thread mutator. A worker M is a second one, so the
// lock is now allocated by `__gt_init` for EVERY green-thread program and taken at every head/tail mutation.
//
// ⚠ **ONE LOCK RATHER THAN TWO, AND THAT IS A DEADLOCK ARGUMENT.** It also still guards the IOCP `ioParked`
// state machine, which is what it guarded before. Two locks — one for the queue, one for the handshake —
// would have to be held together on the completion thread's enqueue path, and two locks held together have
// an ORDER that two files would each have to remember. A CRITICAL_SECTION is recursive, so one lock covering
// both has no order to get wrong.
export let SchedLockLabel = b"__sched_lock"

// ==========================================================================
// Entry points
// ==========================================================================

// `__sched_init_procs()` — reserve the TLS slot, size and build the P array, and adopt P[0] for the calling
// (main) OS thread. Called once, from `__gt_init`, before that thread does anything else.
export let SchedInitProcsName = b"__sched_init_procs"

// `__sched_worker_loop(m)` — a worker M's whole life. The `lpParameter` `osThreadCreate` hands it IS its M,
// because a thread cannot learn its own joinable handle (`emitSchedAllocM`); the processor it is to run on
// arrives on that machine's `MOffNextP`.
let SchedWorkerLoopName = b"__sched_worker_loop"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_main_loop(until)` — THE MAIN MACHINE RUNNING THE SAME SCHEDULER LOOP AS EVERY WORKER** (Go's
// `mstart` on M0), from one body (`buildSchedLoop`), until `until` says to stop: `main` has completed
// (`__gt_run_main`), or the exit drain has nothing left to wait for (`__gt_drain_at_exit`). See `SchedPhase`.
let SchedMainLoopName = b"__sched_main_loop"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_checkdead()` — Go's `checkdead`, at `mput`.** Called by a machine that has just joined
// `__sched_midle`, under `__sched_lock`. See `buildSchedCheckdead`.
let SchedCheckdeadName = b"__sched_checkdead"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_startm(p, credit)` — Go's `startm(pp, spinning)` (`proc.go:3543`).** `credit` is a
// `SchedStartMCredit`: whether the caller reserved a spinning credit for the machine this call starts. A
// reserved credit is this call's to settle on every road, inside `__sched_lock`: handed to the machine it
// wakes, given back when it creates one, and given back with `__sched_needspinning` raised when no processor
// is idle or no machine can be created.
//
// It takes the processor as its ARGUMENT, because `handoffp` has one — a processor still marked `active`,
// which no idle list holds: pass a P and it binds a machine to that P; pass 0 and it takes one off
// `__sched_pidle` first, which is what `wakep` wants.
//
// ⚠ **IT MUST BE CALLED WITH `__sched_lock` RELEASED**, and takes the lock itself. `osSetEvent` runs after
// the release, because the machine it wakes takes this lock first; `osThreadCreate` runs inside it, for the
// teardown's reason `buildSchedStartM` gives.
let SchedStartMName = b"__sched_startm"

// ⭐⭐ **WHETHER A `__sched_startm` CALLER RESERVED A SPINNING CREDIT — Go's `spinning` argument.** The
// machine started takes over exactly what its caller reserved: a unit of `__sched_nmspinning` handed over or
// given back that nobody took would leave the count below the truth, and a count that reads non-zero with
// nobody searching makes every later publish skip its wake.
enum SchedStartMCredit
	// `wakep`, which took one unit at its gate.
	reserved = 1

	// `handoffp`, which took none: its machine starts as an ordinary searcher and earns a credit at
	// `__sched_find_runnable`'s spinner cap like any other.
	unreserved = 0
end 'SchedStartMCredit'

// `__sched_wake_or_spawn()` — hand a newly-published strand token to an idle M, or start one on a free P.
// EXPORTED for the hand-assembled IOCP completion loop (which wakes after readying a GT) and the
// `DeadFunctionElimination` root that keeps it alive for that call.
export let SchedWakeOrSpawnName = b"__sched_wake_or_spawn"

// ⭐⭐ `__sched_wake_netpoller(when)` — Go's `wakeNetPoller` (`proc.go:4018`): a deadline `when` has just become
// the earliest the scheduler waits for. If a machine is blocked in the poller for a LATER deadline, break its
// wait; if none is polling, `__sched_wake_poller`. Called with `__sched_lock` released, which the wake takes.
// EXPORTED for `GtRuntime.__gt_register_park`.
export let SchedWakeNetpollerName = b"__sched_wake_netpoller"

// ⭐⭐ `__sched_wake_poller()` — a timer or a child is pending and no machine waits in the poller: take one
// PARKED machine off `__sched_midle` and signal it, handing it nothing, so it finds nothing to run and claims the
// poller.
//
// ⛔ **IT STARTS NO MACHINE, WHERE GO'S `wakep` WOULD.** A machine starts for published work (this file's
// header), and a deadline is not work until it fires: the machine that armed it claims the poller itself when it
// runs out of work, and fires what is due at every schedule before then (`wchecktimers`). A machine started here
// would be one a program whose only green thread is `main` never needs — its coroutines run on the one machine
// holding their strand — and is what `multicore-stress/pin-matrix.sh` pins against.
let SchedWakePollerName = b"__sched_wake_poller"

// ⭐ `__sched_stop_workers()` — seal the scheduler and JOIN every worker M out. Called once, from
// `GtRuntime.__gt_drain_at_exit`, on the exit path: set `__sched_shutdown_flag`, signal every MACHINE's own
// park event so a parked one leaves its `osWaitHandle`, then wait on each worker's thread handle. ⚠ **BOTH
// WALKS ARE OVER `__sched_allm` SINCE W213-C2, WHERE THEY WERE OVER `__sched_procs`** — a parked machine is
// on no processor, so the old walk could neither signal it nor join it. EXPORTED for that one cross-file
// caller; the fields are this file's, so the function is here and the exit path merely asks for it.
export let SchedStopWorkersName = b"__sched_stop_workers"

// `__sched_runq_put(gt)` — push a runnable green thread onto the CURRENT P's ring tail, overflowing half the
// ring plus `gt` to the global queue when the ring is full. Go's `runqput` + `runqputslow`.
//
// ⚠ NOT exported: `GtRuntime` reaches it through `emitSchedRunqPut` rather than by name.
let SchedRunqPutName = b"__sched_runq_put"

// `__sched_runq_get(p) -> gt` — take the oldest thread off `p`'s ring by CAS on its head, or 0. Go's
// `runqget`. It takes `p` rather than reading TLS because the SWEEP calls it for the P it already holds.
let SchedRunqGetName = b"__sched_runq_get"

// `__sched_steal(p, victim) -> gt` — grab half of `victim`'s ring into `p`'s and answer one of them, or 0.
// Go's `runqgrab` + `runqsteal`.
let SchedStealName = b"__sched_steal"

// ⭐⭐ `__sched_find_runnable() -> gt` — **THE ONE PLACE THE SCHEDULER DECIDES WHAT RUNS NEXT**, and 0 when
// there is nothing. Go's `findRunnable`, minus everything the compiler has no equivalent of.
//
// ⚠ NOT exported, for `SchedRunqPutName`'s reason: its caller reaches it through `emitSchedFindRunnable`.
let SchedFindRunnableName = b"__sched_find_runnable"

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_any_work() -> 1 if the global queue or any ACTIVE processor's ring holds a green thread, else
// 0` — Go's `checkRunqsNoP` plus its global-queue check.** The re-check a machine owes once it stops spinning
// (`buildSchedLoop`'s `wspinrecheck` and `wrecheck`). It takes no lock and needs no processor, so a machine
// that has just released its own may ask it. A processor on `__sched_pidle` is skipped: its ring is empty by
// `emitSchedPidlePutLocked`'s assertion.
let SchedAnyWorkName = b"__sched_any_work"

// `__sched_sweep_dropped()` — reclaim the dropped green threads sitting at the FRONT of this P's ring and of
// the global queue.
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED AGAIN AT SV1.** Its caller is `__gt_promise_drop`, which renounces a promise — and what a
// promise names is a COROUTINE (tombstone in its owner's queue, swept by `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped`) or a
// GREEN THREAD (tombstone in a ring or in the global queue, swept here). The drop asks which and calls one
// of the two; EC10 could drop this half because nothing produced a green thread.
export let SchedSweepDroppedName = b"__sched_sweep_dropped"

// ⭐ `__sched_steal_count() -> i64` — the sum of every P's steal counter, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedStealCount()`. It is the only way a Maxon program can observe that work stealing
// happened, which is what `scripts/multicore-stress/steal-torture.maxon` asserts on.
//
// ⚠ **IT LIVES HERE RATHER THAN BESIDE `__sched_max_active_workers` IN `CpuParallelRuntime`, AND THE SPLIT
// IS THAT FILE'S OWN TEST APPLIED HONESTLY.** That file holds the queries whose answer costs no scheduler —
// its `__sched_max_active_workers` is a `.data` load that is TRUE (1) for a program with no scheduler at
// all. A steal count is not: it walks `__sched_procs`, which only `__sched_init_procs` builds, so the
// question has no answer without the machinery. `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` therefore routes it to
// `usesGt` — asking it installs the scheduler, because asking it is a question ABOUT the scheduler.
export let SchedStealCountName = b"__sched_steal_count"

// ⭐ `__sched_retake_count() -> i64` — the sum of every P's retake counter, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedRetakeCount()`. Its shape, its cost and its `usesGt` routing are `__sched_steal_count`'s
// exactly, which is why the two share ONE body (`buildSchedPerProcSum`): both walk `__sched_procs` summing one
// per-P word, and a second copy of that loop is one fact written twice.
export let SchedRetakeCountName = b"__sched_retake_count"

// ⭐ `__sched_gt_recycle_count() -> i64` — the sum of every P's `POffGtRecycleCount`, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedGtRecycleCount()`: how many GT structs the free list served instead of the slab. The fourth
// `buildSchedPerProcSum` body, for `__sched_retake_count`'s reason.
export let SchedGtRecycleCountName = b"__sched_gt_recycle_count"

// ⭐ `__sched_idle_processor_count() -> i64` — `__sched_npidle`, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedIdleProcessorCount()`. A program that has spawned nothing reads the idle count a live
// scheduler really has — every processor but the one `main` is running on.
export let SchedIdleProcessorCountName = b"__sched_idle_processor_count"

// ⭐ `__sched_park_wake_count() -> i64` — the sum of every machine's `MOffParkWakes`, and the floor of
// `__Builtins.schedParkWakeCount()`. It walks `__sched_allm` rather than `__sched_procs`, because the count
// belongs to a machine, and a parked machine holds no processor.
export let SchedParkWakeCountName = b"__sched_park_wake_count"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_free_put(gt)` — Go's `gfput`: a reclaimed GT struct goes onto the free list, NEVER back to the
// slab.** Its only caller is `__gt_reclaim`. A struct's memory is a GT record for the life of the process
// (TYPE-STABLE, as Go's `g` records are), so a read through a handle whose thread is gone — `awaitAny` or
// `gtIsComplete` over a promise already awaited (`GtRuntime.GtAnyCompletedName`) — lands in a GT record,
// never in whatever the slab gave the memory to next or in a span the scavenger has decommitted.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE LIST UNDER `__sched_lock`, WHERE GO KEEPS ONE PER P.** A per-P list keeps a struct on the processor
// that reclaimed it, so whether a spawn reuses one or cuts a fresh one depends on which processor the spawning
// thread runs on — and a green thread changes processor at every park. With one list a fresh struct is cut
// exactly when every struct cut so far is in use, so the count depends on how many threads are outstanding at
// each spawn and not on where the spawn ran, which is what `scale-test`'s exact memory columns rest on. The
// cost is one short hold of the lock per spawn and per reclaim.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES `__sched_lock` AND CALLS NOTHING WHILE HOLDING IT**, so it adds no lock order: `__gt_reclaim`
// is never reached with that lock held (`GtRuntime.GtRunnerDoneName`), and the hold covers list links only.
export let GtFreePutName = b"__gt_free_put"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_free_get() -> gt` — Go's `gfget` plus `malg`'s allocation: the ONE door a GT struct is taken
// through**, by `__gt_spawn` and `__gt_cell_alloc` alike. It pops the free list under `__sched_lock` and cuts
// a fresh struct (`__slab_alloc(GtStructSize)`) only when the list is empty. No unlocked peek goes in front of
// the lock: a stale "empty" would cut a fresh struct while a free one waits, the scheduling-dependent count
// `GtFreePutName` exists to rule out.
//
// ⚠ **THE LOCK IS TAKEN UNGUARDED, AND THE PROCESSOR IS ASKED FOR ONLY TO COUNT.** Every caller runs after
// `__gt_init` has allocated the lock — `__gt_run_main` spawns `main` after it, and every other spawn and
// every cell is made by a green thread — so the list is consulted on every road, and only
// `POffGtRecycleCount`'s step needs a processor to hold the count. A thread holding none is served all the
// same and counted nowhere.
//
// ⛔ **A RECYCLED STRUCT IS ZEROED OVER THE WHOLE `GtStructSize` BEFORE IT IS HANDED OUT**, which is the
// contract a fresh slab slot already carries: every "no releaser", "never ran", "no teardown ticket" and
// "empty coroutine queue" reading in `GtRuntime` is the struct's zero, so a recycled one that kept its
// previous tenant's words would release that tenant's arguments and answer that tenant's questions.
export let GtFreeGetName = b"__gt_free_get"

// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-P COUNTER SUMS — `buildSchedPerProcSum`'s names:** one body, one walk of `__sched_procs`,
// one field offset each. That builder refuses a name that is not here, and `isSchedSubstrateQueryCallee`
// below takes every member.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A NAME TEST AND NOT A PREFIX TEST**: the `__sched_` band also holds scheduler INTERNALS a program
// never writes (`isSchedRuntimeCallee`), and one member is a `__slab_` name, because the number it reports is
// the ALLOCATOR's (see `SlabRuntime.SlabRemoteFreeCountName`).
function isSchedPerProcSumCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(SchedStealCountName) or callee.equals(SchedRetakeCountName) or callee.equals(SlabRemoteFreeCountName) or callee.equals(SchedGtRecycleCountName)
end 'isSchedPerProcSumCallee'

// ⭐⭐ **THE QUERIES ANSWERED FROM THE SCHEDULER'S OWN STATE — the roster that says "asking this question IS
// asking for the scheduler".** The per-P sums, the idle-processor count and the per-machine wake count. Two
// facts follow, and BOTH are asked through here so the membership is written down once:
//
//   • `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` routes them to `recordGtUsage` — the state they read exists only once
//     `__sched_init_procs` has built it, so the question installs the machinery it is about.
//   • `TargetFacilities.calleeHostFacility` routes them to `HostFacility.greenThreads` — that machinery is
//     the green-thread floor, so a lane without it must REFUSE the call at its own span (E3104) rather than
//     reach an install that has no TLS slot to emit.
export function isSchedSubstrateQueryCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return isSchedPerProcSumCallee(callee) or callee.equals(SchedIdleProcessorCountName) or callee.equals(SchedParkWakeCountName)
end 'isSchedSubstrateQueryCallee'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_handoffp(p)` — "THIS PROCESSOR HAS BEEN TAKEN FROM A BLOCKED MACHINE; GIVE IT TO SOMEBODY
// WHO CAN USE IT" — Go's `handoffp` (`proc.go:3147`), minus every arm the compiler has no subject for (W213-C3).**
//
// ⛔⛔ **ITS GOVERNING INVARIANT IS GO'S FIRST COMMENT AND IT IS THE THING TO GET RIGHT:** *"handoffp must
// start an M in any situation where findRunnable would return a G to run on pp."* Every arm mirrors one work
// source `__sched_find_runnable` consults, so a work source added there without an arm here is work stranded
// on a processor nobody is looking at.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS CALLED WITH NO PROCESSOR AND NO MACHINE OF ITS OWN**, from the sysmon thread, which is why it may
// take `__sched_lock` freely and why it must not touch TLS.
let SchedHandoffPName = b"__sched_handoffp"

// ⭐⭐ `__sched_retake(now) -> how many processors this pass took` — Go's `retake` (`proc.go:6682`), minus
// PREEMPTION. Go also preempts a long-RUNNING goroutine here; The compiler is cooperative by construction and has no
// preemption at all, so that arm is deliberately absent and `POffRunnext`'s reservation note carries what its
// absence costs.
let SchedRetakeName = b"__sched_retake"

// ⭐⭐ `__sysmon(arg) -> 0` — the monitor thread's whole life. **No processor, no M, allocation-free after
// start, and it never runs Maxon user code.** Started once, lazily; see `SchedSysmonStartedLabel`.
let SchedSysmonName = b"__sysmon"

// ⭐⭐⭐ `__sched_syscall_regain()` — **"sysmon took my processor while I was in the kernel; get me another
// before I touch the scheduler again."** The one road out of `MSyscallStolen`, called from the syscall shim's
// post-call path with the green thread's own result already saved.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT WAITS FOR A PROCESSOR RATHER THAN GIVING THE GREEN THREAD AWAY, WHICH IS WHERE IT DIVERGES FROM
// GO AND THE DIVERGENCE IS FORCED.** Go's `exitsyscallNoP` puts the goroutine on the GLOBAL RUN QUEUE and
// stops the machine, because in Go any M can resume any G. In the compiler it cannot: a green thread inside a
// shimmed call names the scheduler context that switched it in (`GtOffWaiter`), which is this machine's own
// (`MOffSchedulerContext`, `stackBase == 0`) and lives on this OS thread's stack, suspended inside
// `__gt_run_strand`'s context switch — UNMOVABLE. Publishing the green thread would let another machine run it
// and then context-switch into a context living on a different OS thread's stack. ⇒ **the machine that entered the
// syscall is the machine that must leave it**, and all it may do is wait for a processor.
export let SchedSyscallRegainName = b"__sched_syscall_regain"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE BRACKET ITSELF — `entersyscall` / `exitsyscall`, AS TWO Std FUNCTIONS.** The pair a target isel
// wraps around every `blocking` op (`StdDialect.stdOpSyscallClassOf`): one announces *"this machine is about
// to be unreachable; its processor may be taken"*, the other clears the announcement and reads the verdict.
//
// ⭐⭐ **CALLS RATHER THAN INLINE CODE, WHICH IS WHERE THIS DIVERGES FROM x64's SHIM AND THE DIVERGENCE IS
// THE POINT.** x64 emits the same two steps inline because its bracket lives INSIDE the import shim, in a
// pre-call window where the argument registers are already laid out for the kernel and the stack has been
// switched. A bracket at the ISEL's dispatch door has no such window: it is emitted before the op's own
// argument moves and after its result has been captured into a virtual register, which is ordinary call
// position. ⇒ **one door, two calls, and every lowering — a hand-assembled chunk, an inline import, a raw
// `svc` — is covered by construction**, where a shim-level bracket covers only what goes through the shim.
export let SchedEnterSyscallName = b"__sched_enter_syscall"
export let SchedExitSyscallName = b"__sched_exit_syscall"

// ==========================================================================
// The TLS read — the one door to "which M am I", and the P through it
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE M FOR THE CALLING OS THREAD, OR 0 — TWO LOADS AND NO CALL.** `globalAddr` + `loadIndirect` fetch
// the precomputed TEB offset; `tlsSlotLoad` dereferences it through `gs:`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS THE READ THAT USED TO ANSWER A P, AND IT COSTS EXACTLY WHAT IT COST (W213-C1).** TLS points
// at the M now, so the two hot Target-tier readers — the syscall shim and the prologue stack guard, which
// want `currentGt` and the syscall stack and nothing else — reach BOTH off this one pointer with the same
// two loads they spent before. Only a caller that wants the PROCESSOR pays the extra field load, and every
// such caller is scheduler-internal and per-schedule rather than per-call.
//
// ⚠ **A ZERO ANSWER IS REAL AND IT IS NOT THE SAME ZERO AS THE OFFSET'S.** An OS thread with no M is the
// IOCP completion thread, for its whole life; a zero TEB OFFSET is *"the scheduler has not initialized"*.
// Neither is tested here — this door is straight-line by design — so a caller that cannot rule both out
// takes `emitLoadCurrentGtAndProcIdOrZero` below, or tests what it must, as `__sched_runq_put` does.
function emitLoadCurrentM(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let tebOffset = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, ids: ids)
	let m = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(m, tebOffsetId: tebOffset))
	return m
end 'emitLoadCurrentM'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE GUARDED WALK TO A USABLE M — THE OFFSET TEST, THE SLOT LOAD, THE M TEST — WRITTEN ONCE.** Every
// walk to the current M that cannot promise the scheduler exists and this OS thread has an M comes through
// here, so a new way to have no usable M (a DETACHED M, say) is added in one place. A walk that spelled the
// tests out for itself and missed one would be a WRONG ANSWER rather than a compile error — without the
// offset test the slot load reads `gs:[0]`, `NT_TIB.ExceptionList`, a NON-NULL pointer, as though it were an M.
//
// ⭐ **THE BLOCKS ARE THE CALLER'S AND THE OPS ARE THIS FUNCTION'S.** Each caller creates and names its own
// three blocks and hands them here to be filled, so an IR dump keeps that caller's vocabulary and the block
// ORDER — which is the emitted layout — stays where the caller put it. It answers the M, live and non-null
// in `haveMachine`; whatever arm that caller needs BEYOND this walk it adds there, and the one several
// callers need — *"and it holds a P"* — is `emitLoadCurrentPOrBail`.
//
// `bailValues` are the constants the bail edge carries, one per block arg of `bail` — minted fresh in
// whichever block takes the arm, because a branch edge's arguments must be defined in the block that
// branches. A `bail` with no block args (`__sched_runq_put`'s global queue) passes none.
export function emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, haveOffset IrBlock, haveMachine IrBlock, bail IrBlock, bailValues ParsedIntArray, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let tebOffset = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBailIfZero(module, block: cur, tested: tebOffset, bail: bail, bailValues: bailValues, otherwise: haveOffset, ids: ids)

	let m = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(haveOffset, op: StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(m, tebOffsetId: tebOffset))
	emitBailIfZero(module, block: haveOffset, tested: m, bail: bail, bailValues: bailValues, otherwise: haveMachine, ids: ids)

	return m
end 'emitLoadCurrentMOrBail'

// The P this OS thread's M holds, live and non-null in `haveProc`. `noProc` takes all three ways to have
// none — no scheduler yet, an OS thread with no M (the IOCP completion thread), an M holding no processor —
// because a caller that wants a processor's per-P state has none to use in any of them. The blocks are the
// caller's, for `emitLoadCurrentMOrBail`'s reason.
function emitLoadCurrentPOrBail(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, haveOffset IrBlock, haveMachine IrBlock, haveProc IrBlock, noProc IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let m = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: cur, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: noProc, bailValues: ParsedIntArray.create(), ids: ids)

	let p = emitLoadField(module, block: haveMachine, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let pIsNone = emitCmp(module, block: haveMachine, a: p, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveMachine, op: StdOp.condBranch(pIsNone, thenBlockId: noProc.id, elseBlockId: haveProc.id))
	return p
end 'emitLoadCurrentPOrBail'

// Terminate `block` with *"`tested` is 0 → `bail`, otherwise `otherwise`"*, giving the bail edge this
// block's own copy of each of its arguments.
function emitBailIfZero(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tested ValueId, bail IrBlock, bailValues ParsedIntArray, otherwise IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isZero = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: tested, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)

	var edgeArgs = ValueIdArray.create()

	for value in bailValues 'eachBailValue'
		edgeArgs.push(emitConst(module, block: block, value: value, ids: ids))
	end 'eachBailValue'

	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(isZero, thenBlockId: bail.id, elseBlockId: otherwise.id))

	if edgeArgs.count() > 0 'carriesArguments'
		block.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(bail.id, argIds: edgeArgs))
	end 'carriesArguments'
end 'emitBailIfZero'

// ⭐⭐ **THE P THIS OS THREAD'S M IS HOLDING.** One field off the M, and the field is the rung: it reads 0
// for an M that holds no processor, which is a state a handoff will produce and which nothing could express
// while TLS pointed at the P itself.
//
// ⛔ **ITS PRECONDITION IS THAT THIS OS THREAD HAS AN M**, which is exactly `emitLoadCurrentGt`'s
// precondition below and is satisfied the same way: every caller here runs behind an entry point that opens
// with `__gt_init`. A thread with no M reads 0 from TLS and this load would be through null — so a caller
// that cannot promise one tests the M first (`__sched_runq_put`) or takes the guarded door.
//
function emitLoadCurrentP(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: emitLoadCurrentM(module, block: block, ids: ids), offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadCurrentP'

// ⭐ **THE GREEN THREAD THIS M IS RUNNING.** The one door every `GtRuntime` reader goes through, stated once
// so the M indirection cannot be spelled differently at two of a dozen sites.
//
// ⛔⛔ **ITS PRECONDITION IS THAT THE SCHEDULER HAS INITIALIZED, AND IT IS A REAL OBLIGATION.** Before
// `__sched_init_procs` the TEB offset is 0, which addresses `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` — a NON-NULL pointer — so
// the `currentGt` load lands on `ExceptionList + 0x08` and the process dies; MEASURED as a segfault. Every
// `GtRuntime` caller meets it because `__gt_run_main` initializes the scheduler before any user code runs,
// and the runtime is reached only from user code or from an M's scheduler context. A caller that CANNOT make
// that claim takes `emitLoadCurrentGtAndProcIdOrZero` below instead.
export function emitLoadCurrentGt(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: emitLoadCurrentM(module, block: block, ids: ids), offset: MOffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadCurrentGt'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME READ FOR A CALLER THAT MAY RUN BEFORE THE SCHEDULER EXISTS — or on an OS thread that owns
// no P.** Answers the running green thread AND the id of the processor running it, or 0 for each. It costs
// three branches, which is why it is not the door above: every scheduler-internal reader has already run
// `__gt_init` and would pay them for nothing.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT ANSWERS A PAIR RATHER THAN A GREEN THREAD, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS ONE CALL AND NOT TWO.** Both
// halves hang off the ONE `tlsSlotLoad` — the green thread is the M's own field and the processor is one
// more hop through `MOffCurrentP` — so asking the two questions separately would mean a second TLS read and
// a second chain of branches to reach values the first walk already had in hand.
//
// **THE THREE ZEROS ARE THREE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND ALL THREE MUST BE ASKED**, exactly as the
// hand-assembled twin in `X64GtRuntime.emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail` states. The first two are not asked HERE —
// they are `emitLoadCurrentMOrBail`'s, shared with every caller that makes the same walk — and only
// the third is this function's own:
//   * a zero TEB OFFSET means `__sched_init_procs` has not run — and `gs:[0]` is `NT_TIB.ExceptionList`, a
//     non-null pointer, so reading through it is a wild load rather than a null one.
//   * a zero M means this OS thread has none, which the IOCP completion thread does for its whole life.
//   * ⭐ **a zero `currentP` is the THIRD, it was W213-C1's own arm, and W213-C2 GAVE IT A PRODUCER**: an M
//     that holds no processor is every parked worker, for the whole of its park. C1 built this arm against a
//     state nothing could yet reach, on the ground that a walk reading `P->id` without asking would be the
//     first thing a handoff broke; one rung later it is reached by any program whose workers go idle. It
//     answers the green thread it really found beside a zero processor id.
// The first two arms answer 0 for BOTH halves, which for the processor id is a stand-in rather than a truth
// — a caller that cannot promise the scheduler exists cannot be told the id of a processor that does not
// exist either.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE STAND-IN IS NOT SELF-IDENTIFYING — DO NOT BUILD A RULE ON IT.** An earlier version of this
// paragraph argued that `P0` could not be mistaken for a real answer "because the same arm's green thread is
// 0 and no real green thread ever is". That is false in the direction that matters: a REAL M holding P[0],
// whose `MOffCurrentGt` is 0 — an M that exists but is running nothing at this instant — stamps exactly the
// same `gt=0 P0`. The pair is therefore ambiguous between "no scheduler" and "a live, idle P0", and the only
// reason nothing is wrong today is that its one consumer (`DebugStreamRuntime`) has its `gt=0x… P<n>` text
// stripped by `normalizeTrace` before any golden sees it. A consumer that wanted to TELL THE TWO APART would
// need a third answer here, not a cleverer reading of these two.
//
// It terminates `cur` and returns the block to CONTINUE IN, whose two block args are the answers — the shape
// every branching emit helper in this runtime uses, because the Std tier has no select.
export function emitLoadCurrentGtAndProcIdOrZero(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gtId ValueId, procIdId ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtproc", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtmerge", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(gtId, argType: StdType.i64))
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(procIdId, argType: StdType.i64))

	// The first two zeros are the shared walk's, and both answer 0 for BOTH halves.
	var bothHalvesZero = ParsedIntArray.create()
	bothHalvesZero.push(0)
	bothHalvesZero.push(0)
	let m = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: cur, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: merge, bailValues: bothHalvesZero, ids: ids)

	// The green thread is the M's own, so it is answered on BOTH arms below — the processor is what a P-less
	// M cannot supply, not the thread it is running.
	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: haveMachine, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
	let p = emitLoadField(module, block: haveMachine, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	let machineZero = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let machineZeroProc = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noProc = emitCmp(module, block: haveMachine, a: p, b: machineZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveMachine, op: StdOp.condBranch(noProc, thenBlockId: merge.id, elseBlockId: haveProc.id))
	haveMachine.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: twoArgs(gt, second: machineZeroProc)))

	let procId = emitLoadField(module, block: haveProc, record: p, offset: POffId, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveProc, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	haveProc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: twoArgs(gt, second: procId)))

	return merge
end 'emitLoadCurrentGtAndProcIdOrZero'

// ⚠ **THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `emitStoreCurrentGt` TWIN.** The only writer is `GtRuntime.emitGtExecute`,
// which already HOLDS the M — it is handed one — so a helper that re-read TLS to store through it would be a
// second TLS read on the dispatch path, dressed as symmetry. A one-line `emitStoreField(record: m, offset:
// MOffCurrentGt, …)` at a site that has `m` is the right spelling, and it is what that site uses.

// ⚠ **AND NO Std-TIER READER OF `MOffSystemStackSP` EITHER.** Both of its readers — the syscall shim and the
// prologue stack guard's cold path — are hand-assembled Target-tier chunks that reach the field directly;
// nothing in builder-built Std switches a stack. An exported helper here would have been a second spelling
// with no caller, which reads to the next person as though the field had a Std consumer it does not have.

// ==========================================================================
// `.data` and installation
// ==========================================================================

// ⛔ **THERE IS NO `DefaultMaxProcs` CONSTANT ANY MORE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE DECISION.** It was `1`, and
// this is where that decision physically lived; the default is now the MACHINE'S OWN PROCESSOR COUNT, which
// is not a number a compile-time constant can hold. It is read by `StdOp.osCpuCount` inside
// `emitResolveMaxProcs`, once, at scheduler bring-up — so the default and the ceiling are one reading of one
// machine rather than a constant and a clamp that could disagree.

// The environment variable that SETS the processor count for a deliberate run.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS GOMAXPROCS: AN EXACT SETTING, CLAMPED TO `[MinimumProcessorCount, osCpuCount]`, WHICH CAN
// LOWER THE COUNT AS WELL AS RAISE IT.** The ceiling is the reason there is one: a P nothing can ever run
// on is a P nobody can schedule.
//
// ⚠ **A VALUE THAT IS NOT A PROCESSOR COUNT IS THE DEFAULT, NEVER AN ERROR** — unset, longer than the
// buffer, non-numeric, or below one. `emitResolveMaxProcs` carries which readings land where.
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED BECAUSE THE SPEC HARNESS NOW WRITES IT.** `<!-- procs: N -->` puts this variable into the
// environment of one case's program (`SpecParser.ProcsMarker`, `SpecTestRunner.runEnvironmentFor`), and the
// name the harness SETS has to be the name the emitted scheduler READS. Written down twice, the marker would
// go on being accepted, the child would go on resolving the default, and every case that named a count would
// report the answer as though the count had been honoured.
export let MaxProcsEnvName = b"MAXON_MAX_PROCS"

// The scratch a `GetEnvironmentVariableA` for a decimal count needs. Far more than any processor count
// spells, so the "buffer too small" answer is unreachable for a well-formed value; a value that overflows it
// is not a processor count, and the read DECLINES rather than retrying.
let MaxProcsEnvBufBytes = 32 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE BASE OF THIS OS THREAD'S PER-THREAD SLOT ARRAY — the one number that turns `tlsAlloc`'s answer
// into the byte offset `tlsSlotLoad` reads at.** `__sched_init_procs` computes `index * 8 + base` once and
// publishes it in `__sched_tls_teb_offset`; every later read is a base-register load at that offset, with no
// call at all. That is the whole reason the current-P read is cheap enough to sit in a prologue guard.
//
// **Windows — 0x1480.** The byte offset of a thread's first 64 TLS slots within its TEB, which `gs:`
// addresses; `gs:[0x1480 + index*8]` IS `TlsGetValue(index)` for those slots.
//
// **Darwin — 0.** `pthread_getspecific(key)` is `tsd[key]`, and the TSD array's base IS the masked thread
// pointer. MEASURED on this host: `tsd[key] == (TPIDRRO_EL0 & ~7) + key*8`, so the offset a slot index turns
// into is `key*8` with nothing added. ⚠ **A ZERO BASE MAKES OFFSET 0 AMBIGUOUS IN A WAY WINDOWS'S IS NOT** —
// there, 0 can only mean "`__sched_init_procs` has not run"; here it would ALSO be key 0's slot. It is not,
// because Darwin's dynamic keys start well above 0 (measured: the first three this host hands out are 258,
// 259, 260), and `StdToArm64Conversion.lowerTlsAlloc` ASSERTS that at run time rather than trusting it —
// which is what keeps the "offset 0 means no scheduler" test every reader makes honest.
//
// ⚠ It is keyed on the OS rather than the CPU: it is an operating system's thread-local storage layout, and
// a hypothetical arm64-Windows would want 0x1480 exactly as x64-Windows does.
function tlsSlotArrayBase(target Target) returns ParsedInt
	return match target.os 'os'
		windows gives WindowsTebTlsSlotsOffset
		linux gives LinuxThreadPointerBase
		macos gives DarwinTsdArrayBase
		wasi gives noTlsSlotArray(target.os)
	end 'os'
end 'tlsSlotArrayBase'

// The refusal for the one target with no per-OS-thread slot at all. ⚠ **IT WAS SHARED WITH LINUX UNTIL L4**,
// on the argument that *"the Linux images are raw static binaries with no libc to write a thread pointer"* —
// which was true and was the reason arm64-linux could WRITE ONE FOR ITSELF: nothing else on that lane reads
// or writes `TPIDR_EL0`, so `mrt_host_tls_alloc` claims it (`Arm64LinuxRuntime`). WASI is different in kind
// and not in schedule: a component has no OS-thread concept for a slot to be per.
//
// ⭐ **BOTH LINUX LANES ARE SERVED BY THE ONE `linux` ARM, AND EACH PUBLISHES THE POINTER ITSELF.** The base
// is 0 on both because each backend CHOSE its thread pointer to be its slot block's base; what differs is
// only the instruction that writes it — an `msr TPIDR_EL0` on AArch64, an `arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS)` on
// x86-64, where the register is not addressable in user mode at all. Neither difference is visible here,
// which is the point of keying this on the OS.
function noTlsSlotArray(os Os) returns ParsedInt
	panic("tlsSlotArrayBase: {os.name} has no per-OS-thread slot this scheduler could publish a P in — a WASI component has no OS-thread concept at all. `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, so a source-written `async` is refused at its own span; reaching here means a COMPILER-INSERTED `__sched_init_procs` got past that name-keyed gate")
end 'noTlsSlotArray'

let WindowsTebTlsSlotsOffset = 0x1480 as ParsedInt
let DarwinTsdArrayBase = 0 as ParsedInt

// **Linux — 0, for Darwin's reason arrived at from the opposite direction.** There the base is 0 because the
// masked thread pointer IS the TSD array's base; here it is 0 because the backend CHOSE the thread pointer to
// be its slot block's base, having found the register unclaimed. Both make offset 0 ambiguous in the way
// Windows's 0x1480 is not — it would also be key 0's slot — and both answer it the same way, by refusing to
// hand out key 0 at run time rather than by trusting the arithmetic
// (`Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxTlsAllocChunk`, `X64LinuxRuntime.buildX64LinuxTlsAllocChunk`).
let LinuxThreadPointerBase = 0 as ParsedInt

// The scheduler's `.data` slots. THREE gates rather than one, and the split is always the same question —
// *can a program that never spawns a green thread read this word?* The worker COUNTERS can
// (`__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()`) and so can the processor count
// (`__Builtins.schedProcessorCount()`); the P machinery itself is meaningless without a scheduler.
export function schedRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	// ⭐ **SEEDED TO 1 IN `.data`, NOT BY AN INITIALIZER.** A program with no scheduler runs on exactly one M
	// — its own — and never runs `__gt_init`, so the true answer must already be in the slot, and costs no code.
	if usage.usesSchedMaxActiveWorkers or usage.usesGt 'workerCounters'
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedActiveWorkersLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, seed: 1)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.monotoneApproximation, seed: 1)
	end 'workerCounters'

	if usage.usesGt 'procs'
		// Written by `__sched_init_procs` and by nothing else.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedTlsIndexLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedProcsLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)

		// ⭐ **THE THREE HEADS W213-C2 ADDS, AND ALL THREE ARE `underSchedLock` FOR THE SAME REASON.** Each is
		// the head of an intrusive list whose transaction spans TWO words — the head and the joining struct's
		// own link — which is exactly what that class exists for and exactly what no atomic can make
		// indivisible. ⚠ **NOT `sealedBeforeWorkers`, though the array beside them is**: `__sched_procs` is
		// written once at bring-up and read for ever, while these three are mutated by every park and every
		// wake for the life of the process.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedAllMLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedMidleLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedPidleLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)

		// A fourth intrusive head, for the same reason, and one no reader peeks at: `GtFreeGetName` says why.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedGtFreeHeadLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)

		// The three counts kept beside those structures, each stepped only inside the doors that mutate its
		// structure and therefore under the same lock. `__sched_npidle`'s two unlocked reads are enumerated
		// under `MultiMSharing.underSchedLock`.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedNpidleLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedRunqSizeLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedMcountLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedNmidleLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)

		// The main machine, written once before any worker exists, and the process phase the deadlock check and
		// the exit drain decide by.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedMainMLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedPhaseLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)

		// The poller's claim and deadline, the machines waiting to regain a processor, and the poller itself.
		// Each word's own declaration argues its class.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedLastPollLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedPollUntilLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedRegainHeadLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)

		for entry in netpollRuntimeGlobals() 'poller'
			out.push(entry)
		end 'poller'
	end 'procs'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD GATE, AND IT IS THE ARRAY'S LENGTH WEARING A QUERY'S SECOND HAT.**
	// `__sched_num_procs` is P machinery — the bound `__sched_steal_count` walks to — AND the whole answer
	// `__Builtins.schedProcessorCount()` gives, which a program may ask having spawned nothing. So it rides the
	// query's bit exactly as the worker counters ride theirs, rather than dragging the TLS slot, the `P*` array
	// and the run-queue lock in behind a load.
	//
	// ⚠ It is pushed HERE, between the array it measures and the teardown flag, because the layout order is
	// the `.data` order: moving it would re-lay every green-thread program's section to say nothing new.
	if usage.usesSchedProcessorCount or usage.usesGt 'processorCount'
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	end 'processorCount'

	if usage.usesGt 'procsTeardown'
		// ⚠ `__sched_shutdown_flag`'s one writer is the teardown and therefore runs AFTER the workers — see
		// its own declaration for why that makes it `lastWriterWins` rather than sealed. The lock is
		// `__sched_init_procs`'s like the three above.
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedLockLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)

		// Every mutation is the `atomicRmw` in `emitSchedSpinningTransition` or the gate's `atomicCas`; see
		// `SchedSpinningLabel` for what a lost update would cost (a permanently suppressed wakeup).
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedSpinningLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep)
		pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedNeedSpinningLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins)

		// ⛔ **GATE SITE 3 OF 4 — see `targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls`.** The monitor's one-shot; its only
		// mutation is the `atomicCas` at `__sched_wake_or_spawn`'s gate, and on a lane with no bracket that gate
		// is not emitted, so this word would be a `.data` slot nothing writes — a line in the `data {` block of
		// every green-thread golden on a lane with no monitor to start. See `SchedSysmonStartedLabel` for why
		// the read in front of that CAS is not a second writer.
		if targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target) 'theMonitorExists'
			pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedSysmonStartedLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep)
			pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedSysmonEventLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
			pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SchedSysmonWaitLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock)
		end 'theMonitorExists'
	end 'procsTeardown'

	return out
end 'schedRuntimeGlobals'

// Install the P/M half of the scheduler. Called from `installGtRuntime`, so it rides `usesGt` exactly as the
// green-thread half does — there is no program that wants one without the other.
export function installSchedRuntime(module StdModule, target Target, usesCompletionPort bool)
	buildSchedInitProcs(module, target: target)
	buildSchedRunqPut(module)
	buildSchedRunqGet(module)
	buildSchedSteal(module)
	buildSchedFindRunnable(module)
	buildSchedAnyWork(module)
	buildSchedSweepDropped(module)

	// The GT-struct doors: the one allocation and the one free every GT struct goes through.
	buildGtFreePut(module)
	buildGtFreeGet(module)

	// The per-P sums, from ONE body: they differ in the name they answer to and the field they read, and in
	// nothing else. See `buildSchedPerProcSum`.
	//
	// ⚠ One answers to a `__slab_` name because the number is the ALLOCATOR's — see
	// `SlabRuntime.SlabRemoteFreeCountName` for why the counter is nonetheless a per-P word summed by this
	// scheduler walk, and why that puts its body here rather than in `installSlabRuntime`.
	buildSchedPerProcSum(module, name: SchedStealCountName, offset: POffStealCount)
	buildSchedPerProcSum(module, name: SchedRetakeCountName, offset: POffRetakeCount)
	buildSchedPerProcSum(module, name: SlabRemoteFreeCountName, offset: POffRemoteFreeCount)
	buildSchedPerProcSum(module, name: SchedGtRecycleCountName, offset: POffGtRecycleCount)
	buildSchedIdleProcessorCount(module)
	buildSchedParkWakeCount(module)
	buildSchedCheckdead(module, usesCompletionPort: usesCompletionPort)

	// Every machine's loop, from one body: the workers' and the main machine's.
	buildSchedLoop(module, role: SchedLoopRole.worker, target: target)
	buildSchedLoop(module, role: SchedLoopRole.mainMachine, target: target)
	buildSchedStartM(module, target: target)
	buildSchedWakeOrSpawn(module, target: target)

	// The poller the idle tail blocks in, and the door a newly earliest deadline wakes it through.
	installNetpollRuntime(module, target: target)
	buildSchedWakeNetpoller(module)
	buildSchedWakePoller(module)

	// ⛔ **GATE SITE 1 OF 4 — see `targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls` for the fork.** All four are reachable ONLY
	// from the bracket: `__sched_syscall_regain` from the bracket's exit step, `__sysmon` from the lazy start
	// (gate site 2), and the other two from `__sysmon`. `__sched_syscall_regain` is dead code that SURVIVES
	// the prune wherever the exit step is INLINE, because the x64 shim calls it from hand-assembly and no Std
	// caller reaches it, so it is a `DeadFunctionElimination` root (gate site 4) under this same answer.
	//
	// The bracket's own two functions ride the NARROWER question — see `targetCallsTheSyscallBracket`: a lane
	// that emits its bracket inline calls neither, and two functions nothing reaches do not belong in its
	// binary.
	//
	// ⚠ **`buildSchedPerProcSum` ABOVE IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* GATED ON `targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls`**: every
	// sum compiles on every target the SCHEDULER compiles on, reading a straight 0 where no monitor ever ran and
	// where no free ever crossed a processor. A counter that existed only on the lane with a sysmon would be a
	// worse divergence than a thread that idles. A lane without the scheduler never reaches this installer for
	// a sum: `isSchedPerProcSumCallee` puts every one of them under `HostFacility.greenThreads`, so the call is
	// refused with E3104 at its own span (`specs/builtins-cpu-parallel.md`,
	// `error.the-per-processor-counters-are-refused-off-their-substrate`).
	if targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target) 'theSyscallHandoff'
		// `__sched_handoffp` is `__sched_retake`'s only caller of `__sched_startm`, `__sysmon` is
		// `__sched_retake`'s only caller, and `__sched_syscall_regain` is the bracket's road back.
		buildSchedHandoffP(module)
		buildSchedRetake(module)
		buildSysmon(module, target: target)
		buildSchedSyscallRegain(module)
	end 'theSyscallHandoff'

	if targetCallsTheSyscallBracket(target) 'theBracketIsCalled'
		buildSchedEnterSyscall(module)
		buildSchedExitSyscall(module)
	end 'theBracketIsCalled'

	buildSchedStopWorkers(module)
end 'installSchedRuntime'

// --- Shared single-op emitters ---

// The address of `__sched_procs[index]`.
function emitProcSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let procs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: procs, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitProcSlotAddr'

// `__sched_procs[index]` — the P struct at that index.
function emitLoadProc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: emitProcSlotAddr(module, block: block, index: index, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadProc'

// Take / release the one run-queue lock. Both are two ops, and both are spelled here so `GtRuntime`'s four
// mutation sites and this file's two reach the same word the same way.
export function emitSchedLockEnter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLockEnter(emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedLockLabel, ids: ids)))
end 'emitSchedLockEnter'

export function emitSchedLockLeave(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLockLeave(emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedLockLabel, ids: ids)))
end 'emitSchedLockLeave'

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE `.data`-HEADED INTRUSIVE LISTS
// ==========================================================================
//
// `__sched_allm`, `__sched_regain_head`, `__sched_pidle` and `__gt_gfree_head` are four singly linked LIFOs
// (two over M, one over P, one over GT), and all four are the same instructions: a push that stores the old
// head into the joining node's link, and an unlink of the head that clears the departing node's link. The
// failure mode of a copy is specific rather than stylistic: an unlink that forgets that clear leaves a node
// pointing at a list it is no longer on, which the next push then splices back in. Parameterising on
// `(headLabel, linkOffset)` makes that impossible to get right at one list and wrong at another.
// `__sched_midle` is doubly linked, because
// two of its removals are from the middle, and has its own doors below under the same clearing rule.
//
// ⛔⛔ **EVERY CALL SITE HOLDS `__sched_lock` EXCEPT ONE THAT IS NAMED HERE, AND THE `Locked` IN EVERY NAME
// IS THE ONLY THING THAT CAN CARRY THE REQUIREMENT.** ⚠ **THE EXCEPTION IS `__sched_init_procs`'s BUILD
// LOOP**, which puts every processor but P[0] on `__sched_pidle` with no lock and argues its own exemption at
// the site on `sealedBeforeWorkers` grounds — bring-up is single-threaded BY CONSTRUCTION, because that
// loop is what builds the array a worker could ever be spawned onto. It is stated here as well as there
// because a rule with a silent exception is a rule the next reader will not trust. Go asserts it
// (`assertLockHeld(&sched.lock)` at `mput`, `mget`, `pidleput` and `pidleget`); the
// compiler has no such assert to emit, and `osLockEnter` is a RECURSIVE CRITICAL_SECTION, so getting it
// wrong produces no diagnostic anywhere — only a list that loses a node. The requirement travels with the
// name for exactly the reason `emitSchedEnqueueLocked`'s does.

// Push `node` onto the LIFO whose head is `headLabel`, threading `linkOffset`. Two stores, in this order:
// the node's link takes the old head before the head takes the node, so a walker that somehow read the head
// between them would find a node whose link is at worst stale rather than uninitialised. Under the lock
// that ordering is unobservable; it is written this way because the day somebody makes one of these lists
// lock-free, this is the order that survives.
function emitSchedListPushLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, headLabel ByteArray, linkOffset ByteOffset, node ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: node, offset: linkOffset, value: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: headLabel, ids: ids))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: headLabel, value: node, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedListPushLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **TAKE THE HEAD OFF THE LIFO AT `headLabel`, OR NOTHING.** It terminates `cur`: to `none` when the list
// is empty, to `got` otherwise, where the answer is already unlinked. The Std tier has no select, so a list
// that may be empty is a branch; the caller's blocks carry the caller's labels, so an IR dump of each function
// says what that function calls them. `got` must have `cur` as its only predecessor, which is what puts the
// answer in scope there.
//
// ⚠ **THE DEPARTING NODE'S LINK IS CLEARED, AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS.** A P taken off `__sched_pidle` still
// pointing at the next idle P is a P that, pushed again later, splices a stale suffix back onto the list —
// every node on it then appearing twice, which is two machines handed one processor.
function emitSchedListTakeHeadLocked(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, got IrBlock, none IrBlock, headLabel ByteArray, linkOffset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let head = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: headLabel, ids: ids)
	let noHead = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let listEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: head, b: noHead, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(listEmpty, thenBlockId: none.id, elseBlockId: got.id))

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: got, label: headLabel, value: emitLoadField(module, block: got, record: head, offset: linkOffset, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: got, record: head, offset: linkOffset, value: emitConst(module, block: got, value: 0, ids: ids))
	return head
end 'emitSchedListTakeHeadLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **STEP A COUNT KEPT BESIDE A LOCKED STRUCTURE — `__sched_npidle`, `__sched_mcount` and
// `__sched_runq_size`.** A plain load/add/store, because every writer holds `__sched_lock`; each count is
// stepped only inside the doors that mutate its structure, so the two cannot drift apart. The prior value
// has no reader.
export function emitSchedStepLockedCount(module StdModule, block IrBlock, label ByteArray, delta ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: block, label: label, delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: delta, ids: ids), multiM: false, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedStepLockedCount'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **GO'S `pidleput` — "THIS PROCESSOR IS IDLE".** Membership of `__sched_pidle`, `POffStatus` and
// `__sched_npidle` are one fact written in three places, so they move together or not at all;
// `emitSchedPidleGetLocked` is the other half, and the two are the only doors to all three. **A writer of
// `POffStatus` outside this pair is what restores `emitSchedClaimProc`'s heap corruption.**
//
// ⚠ The status is written before the push. Membership is what makes this processor claimable, so it may not
// be published while the word beside it still says a machine is here.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND IT ASSERTS THE INVARIANT THE LIST EXISTS UNDER, WHICH IS GO'S OWN ASSERTION PORTED WITH THE
// CODE IT GUARDS.** `proc.go:7425`'s `pidleput` throws `"pidleput: P has non-empty run queue"`; this aborts
// with `schedulerIdledAProcessorHoldingWork`. **A processor on `__sched_pidle` has no runnable work — by
// invariant, not by hope**: a ring filled just before its processor went idle would be visible to no machine
// and to no scan, and the drain at `wpark` is what satisfies the guard.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A MACHINE WAITING TO REGAIN A PROCESSOR TAKES IT FIRST** (`__sched_syscall_regain`): the green thread
// it holds is runnable, and it can run nowhere else. The processor stays `PStatusActive` and uncounted, is
// delivered through `MOffNextP` and the machine is signalled — so it never passes through `__sched_pidle`, and
// a machine that joined the waiters under this lock cannot miss a processor released after it did.
//
// ⚠ It terminates `cur` and answers the block to CONTINUE IN, which is the shape every branching emitter in
// this runtime uses — the Std tier has no select, and an abort arm is a branch.
function emitSchedPidlePutLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, p ValueId, holdsWorkLabel ByteArray, emptyLabel ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var holdsWork = module.addBlock(func, label: holdsWorkLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var ringEmpty = module.addBlock(func, label: emptyLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var handOver = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pidleputregain", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var idleIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pidleputidle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let placed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pidleputplaced", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let stillHasWork = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(stillHasWork, thenBlockId: holdsWork.id, elseBlockId: ringEmpty.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: holdsWork, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerIdledAProcessorHoldingWork, ids: ids)

	let waiter = emitSchedListTakeHeadLocked(module, cur: ringEmpty, got: handOver, none: idleIt, headLabel: SchedRegainHeadLabel, linkOffset: MOffRegainNext, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: handOver, record: waiter, offset: MOffNextP, value: p)
	module.appendOp(handOver, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(emitLoadField(module, block: handOver, record: waiter, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)))
	module.setTerminator(handOver, op: StdOp.branch(placed.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: idleIt, record: p, offset: POffStatus, value: emitConst(module, block: idleIt, value: PStatusUnused, ids: ids))
	emitSchedListPushLocked(module, block: idleIt, headLabel: SchedPidleLabel, linkOffset: POffPidleNext, node: p, ids: ids)
	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: idleIt, label: SchedNpidleLabel, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(idleIt, op: StdOp.branch(placed.id))
	return placed
end 'emitSchedPidlePutLocked'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **GO'S `pidleget` — TAKE A PROCESSOR OFF `__sched_pidle`, OR NONE.** It terminates `cur`: to `none`
// when the list is empty, to `got` otherwise — where the processor it answers is already unlinked, marked
// `PStatusActive` and uncounted from `__sched_npidle`. All three happen in the critical section that took it,
// so a processor is never both off the list and marked idle: that window would let a second `pidleget` hand
// it to a second machine.
//
// ⭐ **A PROCESSOR BECOMING BUSY WAKES A MONITOR ASLEEP ON AN ALL-IDLE SCHEDULER** (`SchedSysmonWaitLabel`),
// when `wakesMonitor` says this lane has one. The answer is defined in `cur`, so it is in scope at `got`
// whichever road reaches it.
function emitSchedPidleGetLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, got IrBlock, none IrBlock, wakesMonitor bool, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var taken = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pidlegettaken", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let p = emitSchedListTakeHeadLocked(module, cur: cur, got: taken, none: none, headLabel: SchedPidleLabel, linkOffset: POffPidleNext, ids: ids)
	emitSchedPidleClaimLocked(module, block: taken, p: p, ids: ids)
	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: taken, label: SchedNpidleLabel, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)

	if wakesMonitor 'monitorMayBeAsleep'
		var wakeMonitor = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pidlegetsysmon", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		let asleep = emitCmp(module, block: taken, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: taken, label: SchedSysmonWaitLabel, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: taken, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
		module.setTerminator(taken, op: StdOp.condBranch(asleep, thenBlockId: wakeMonitor.id, elseBlockId: got.id))

		emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: wakeMonitor, label: SchedSysmonWaitLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: wakeMonitor, value: 0, ids: ids), ids: ids)
		module.appendOp(wakeMonitor, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: wakeMonitor, label: SchedSysmonEventLabel, ids: ids)))
		module.setTerminator(wakeMonitor, op: StdOp.branch(got.id))
	end 'monitorMayBeAsleep' else 'noMonitor'
		module.setTerminator(taken, op: StdOp.branch(got.id))
	end 'noMonitor'

	return p
end 'emitSchedPidleGetLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **"A MACHINE HOLDS THIS PROCESSOR" — the store `pidleget` makes, and the one `__sched_init_procs` makes
// for P[0].** P[0] never joins `__sched_pidle`, so it is claimed without being taken off it, and without
// touching `__sched_npidle`, which counts the list alone.
function emitSchedPidleClaimLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffStatus, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: PStatusActive, ids: ids))
end 'emitSchedPidleClaimLocked'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **"THIS M GIVES UP THIS PROCESSOR" — Go's `releasep` + `pidleput`, and the exact mirror of
// `emitSchedAdoptProc`.** The machine stops pointing at the processor, the processor records the machine
// (`POffOldM`), and then the processor is offered to anybody else through `pidleput`.
//
// ⛔ **THE PUSH IS LAST AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ORDERING ARGUMENT.** Membership of `__sched_pidle` is what
// makes this P claimable; a P published there while `M->currentP` still named it would be handed to a second
// M whose `emitSchedAdoptProc` then overwrites a word the first M is still about to clear — two machines
// believing they hold one processor, which is one allocator shard with two lockless writers
// (`emitSchedClaimProc`'s history, arriving by a different road).
function emitSchedReleaseProcLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, p ValueId, holdsWorkLabel ByteArray, emptyLabel ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	let nobody = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, value: nobody)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: POffM, value: nobody)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: POffOldM, value: m)
	return emitSchedPidlePutLocked(module, func: func, cur: cur, p: p, holdsWorkLabel: holdsWorkLabel, emptyLabel: emptyLabel, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedReleaseProcLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **EMPTY `p`'s RING INTO THE GLOBAL QUEUE — what a road idling a processor that may still hold work owes
// `pidleput`'s assertion.** `__sched_lock` held; it terminates `cur` and continues at `cont`. A ring on an
// idle processor is read by nobody, while the global queue is read by every search and every park's final
// look.
//
// ⚠ **IT POPS THROUGH `__sched_runq_get`**, because a THIEF can be advancing this ring's head at the same
// instant: that function's CAS is the protocol, and a lost race answers 0 with the thread already taken.
// `__sched_runq_put` pushes only to its own machine's processor, and the caller is either `p`'s machine or
// `p` has none, so nothing refills the ring and the loop terminates.
function emitSchedDrainRingLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, p ValueId, cont IrBlock, hdrLabel ByteArray, bodyLabel ByteArray, ids ValueMinter)
	var hdr = module.addBlock(func, label: hdrLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: bodyLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))

	let drained = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hdr, op: StdOp.call(drained, callee: SchedRunqGetName, args: argsOf(p)))
	let drainZero = emitConst(module, block: hdr, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let ringDry = emitCmp(module, block: hdr, a: drained, b: drainZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(hdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(ringDry, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: body, gt: drained, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
end 'emitSchedDrainRingLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **PUT A NEW MACHINE ON THE ROSTER — Go's `mcommoninit` linking it onto `allm`.** The push and
// `__sched_mcount`'s step together, so the count the thread limit reads is the roster's length by
// construction. `__sched_lock` held, for `emitSchedAllocM`'s reason.
function emitSchedRegisterMLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedListPushLocked(module, block: block, headLabel: SchedAllMLabel, linkOffset: MOffAllNext, node: m, ids: ids)
	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: block, label: SchedMcountLabel, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedRegisterMLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **GO'S `mput` — PUT `m` AT THE HEAD OF `__sched_midle`.** It terminates `cur` and answers the block to
// continue in, because the old head's back-link is a store only a non-empty list has. `m`'s own back-link is
// already 0: a machine off the list always carries 0 there, which `emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked` guarantees.
function emitSchedMidlePutLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var linkBack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mputlinkback", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joined = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mputjoined", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: cur, label: SchedNmidleLabel, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
	let oldHead = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedMidleLabel, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: m, offset: MOffIdleNext, value: oldHead)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedMidleLabel, value: m, ids: ids)
	let noOldHead = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let wasEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: oldHead, b: noOldHead, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(wasEmpty, thenBlockId: joined.id, elseBlockId: linkBack.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: linkBack, record: oldHead, offset: MOffIdlePrev, value: m)
	module.setTerminator(linkBack, op: StdOp.branch(joined.id))
	return joined
end 'emitSchedMidlePutLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **IS `m` ON `__sched_midle`? — 1 or 0, with no walk.** The head's back-link is 0 and every other
// member's is not, while a machine off the list has a 0 back-link and is not the head: so membership is
// `head == m or prev != 0`. Go's `mgetSpecific` tests the two links against 0 instead, which reads a list's
// only member as absent; asking the head is what counts that one. `m` must not be 0.
function emitSchedMidleHolds(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let isHead = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedMidleLabel, ids: ids), b: m, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let noPrev = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let hasPrev = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffIdlePrev, ids: ids), b: noPrev, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)

	// Both operands are `cmp` results, so they are 0 or 1 and a bitwise OR is the logical one.
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: isHead, b: hasPrev, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedMidleHolds'

// ⭐⭐ **TAKE `m` OFF `__sched_midle` — the one unlink every removal shares: `mget`'s head, `mgetSpecific`'s
// chosen machine, and a signalled machine taking itself off.** `m` must be on the list. It terminates `cur`
// and answers the block to continue in.
//
// ⚠ Both of `m`'s links are cleared, for `emitSchedListTakeHeadLocked`'s reason: a machine that leaves
// still pointing into the list splices a stale suffix back on at its next `mput` — one processor handed to two
// machines — and a stale back-link would make `emitSchedMidleHolds` answer yes for a machine that is gone.
function emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var wasHead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"munlinkhead", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wasInner = module.addBlock(func, label: b"munlinkinner", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var mendNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"munlinkmend", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var backLinkNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"munlinknext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cleared = module.addBlock(func, label: b"munlinkclear", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: cur, label: SchedNmidleLabel, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)

	// Both links are read here, in the block that dominates every block below, so each is loaded once.
	let next = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: m, offset: MOffIdleNext, ids: ids)
	let prev = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: m, offset: MOffIdlePrev, ids: ids)
	let noPrev = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isHead = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: prev, b: noPrev, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(isHead, thenBlockId: wasHead.id, elseBlockId: wasInner.id))

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: wasHead, label: SchedMidleLabel, value: next, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wasHead, op: StdOp.branch(mendNext.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: wasInner, record: prev, offset: MOffIdleNext, value: next)
	module.setTerminator(wasInner, op: StdOp.branch(mendNext.id))

	let noNext = emitConst(module, block: mendNext, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isTail = emitCmp(module, block: mendNext, a: next, b: noNext, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(mendNext, op: StdOp.condBranch(isTail, thenBlockId: cleared.id, elseBlockId: backLinkNext.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: backLinkNext, record: next, offset: MOffIdlePrev, value: prev)
	module.setTerminator(backLinkNext, op: StdOp.branch(cleared.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: cleared, record: m, offset: MOffIdleNext, value: emitConst(module, block: cleared, value: 0, ids: ids))
	emitStoreField(module, block: cleared, record: m, offset: MOffIdlePrev, value: emitConst(module, block: cleared, value: 0, ids: ids))
	return cleared
end 'emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked'

// ⭐⭐ **PAY A WAKE DEBT, AFTER THE RELEASE AND ONLY IF ONE IS OWED.** `owed` is what
// `GtRuntime.__gt_ready_locked` answered; `wake` and `cont` are the caller's blocks, so each keeps its own
// block order. Two properties, and neither is optional:
//
//   • **AFTER the release**, because `LeaveCriticalSection` is the StoreLoad fence
//     `buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s `idleFlag` scan depends on, and a nested leave releases nothing.
//   • **ONLY IF OWED**, because an unconditional wake in a coroutine-only program would find no idle M and
//     then START one on a free P — an OS thread that idles for the life of the process, which is exactly the
//     `workers` reading `multicore-stress/pin-matrix.sh` pins at 1.
export function emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, owed ValueId, wake IrBlock, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let owesNothing = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)
	let nothingOwed = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: owed, b: owesNothing, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingOwed, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: wake.id))
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: wake, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wake, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedWakeIfOwed'

// Append `gt` to the global queue's tail. The caller holds `__sched_lock`.
function emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
end 'emitSchedEnqueueLocked'

function emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedWakeOrSpawnName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
end 'emitSchedWakeAnIdleM'

// ⭐ **PUSH A STRAND TOKEN ONTO THIS PROCESSOR'S RING, WITHOUT THE WAKE.** It requires no lock state of its
// caller: the ring push takes no lock, and an overflow's recursive entry into `__sched_lock` is safe (see
// `buildSchedRunqPut`). EXPORTED for `GtRuntime.__gt_ready_locked`, which publishes a token under the lock and
// answers the wake as a debt.
export function emitSchedRunqPut(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedRunqPutName, args: argsOf(gt)))
end 'emitSchedRunqPut'

// ⭐⭐ **ASK THE SCHEDULER WHICH STRAND TOKEN MACHINE `m` RUNS NEXT, OR 0 — the CONSUME door**, one door
// because the answer is a POLICY (fairness, stealing, the spinner cap, the dropped-thread refusal) rather than
// a queue read. The steal rounds are charged to `m`'s spinning credit.
function emitSchedFindRunnable(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let g = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(g, callee: SchedFindRunnableName, args: argsOf(m)))
	return g
end 'emitSchedFindRunnable'

// ⭐⭐ **TAKE THE NEXT TOKEN OFF THE GLOBAL QUEUE, OR 0.** Popping mutates head AND tail, which every other M
// and the IOCP completion thread also mutate, so it takes the lock. Every search goes through
// `__sched_find_runnable`, and this serves the two places inside it which consult the global queue: the
// 61-schedule fairness check and the batch.
function emitSchedDequeueLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	let g = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(g, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	return g
end 'emitSchedDequeueLocked'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_init_procs`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_init_procs()` — the whole of scheduler bring-up that is about Ms rather than about green
// threads.** Written in builder-built Std rather than hand-assembled bytes, so it gets register
// allocation, a checked frame and the leak-safe backend for free, exactly as `MmRuntime`'s reasoning one
// tier up.
//
// In order: reserve the TLS slot and publish its TEB offset; decide how many Ps to build; allocate the run-
// queue lock and the `P*` array; build each P; adopt P[0] for this OS thread.
//
// ⚠ **THE RUN-QUEUE LOCK IS ALLOCATED HERE AND NOT IN `__io_init`.** It guards the queue for every M, not
// only for the IOCP completion thread, so a green-thread program that never touches a pipe must still have
// it. See `SchedLockLabel`.
function buildSchedInitProcs(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedInitProcsName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var settled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"procsettled", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ploop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopOffer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"poffer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var adopt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"adopt", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The TLS slot, and the `index * 8 + 0x1480` byte offset every later read indexes `gs:` by.
	let tlsIndex = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.tlsAlloc(tlsIndex))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedTlsIndexLabel, value: tlsIndex, ids: ids)
	let slotStride = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: PointerSlotBytes, ids: ids)
	let slotBytes = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: tlsIndex, b: slotStride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let tebBase = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: tlsSlotArrayBase(target), ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, value: emitBin(module, block: entry, a: slotBytes, b: tebBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids), ids: ids)

	// How many Ps: the machine's processor count, unless `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` names another count within it.
	// The answer arrives as `settled`'s block arg, because the decision is a five-way merge.
	let numProcs = ids.mint()
	settled.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(numProcs, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitResolveMaxProcs(module, func: func, cur: entry, settled: settled, ids: ids)

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: settled, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, value: numProcs, ids: ids)

	// The run-queue CRITICAL_SECTION, in a slab region whose pointer the `.data` word holds. Its lifetime is
	// the process's, exactly as the completion port's and the wake events' are — see `SchedLockLabel`.
	let lockBytes = emitConst(module, block: settled, value: OsLockBytes, ids: ids)
	let lock = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(settled, op: StdOp.call(lock, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(lockBytes)))
	module.appendOp(settled, op: StdOp.osLockInit(lock))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: settled, label: SchedLockLabel, value: lock, ids: ids)

	// The `P*` array. `__slab_alloc` zeroes it, so a slot the loop has not reached yet reads as a null P
	// rather than as garbage — which matters only until the loop fills every one, and costs nothing.
	let arrayStride = emitConst(module, block: settled, value: PointerSlotBytes, ids: ids)
	let arrayBytes = emitBin(module, block: settled, a: numProcs, b: arrayStride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let procs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(settled, op: StdOp.call(procs, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(arrayBytes)))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: settled, label: SchedProcsLabel, value: procs, ids: ids)

	// The build loop. Its index is the header's phi — the on-the-fly SSA shape every runtime builder here
	// constructs directly, a runtime function having no parser.
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: settled, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let iterId = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(iterId, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(settled, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	settled.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	// ⚠ The bound is `settled`'s block arg rather than a re-read of `__sched_num_procs`: `settled` dominates
	// this header, so the value is in scope, and reloading it every iteration would be the same fact read twice
	// per turn from a word only this function writes.
	let built = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: iterId, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(built, thenBlockId: adopt.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	// P[i] = a fresh zeroed struct, plus the four fields a zero would be wrong for.
	let structBytes = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: PStructSize, ids: ids)
	let p = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(loopBody, op: StdOp.call(p, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(structBytes)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: emitProcSlotAddr(module, block: loopBody, index: iterId, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: p)
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffId, value: iterId)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: 1, ids: ids)

	// `rng = id + 1` — non-zero, because zero is a fixed point of xorshift. Reserved for the stealer.
	emitStoreField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffRng, value: emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: iterId, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))

	// ⚠ **NO WAKE EVENT IS CREATED HERE — the park is the M's** (`MOffParkEvent`), created with each M by
	// `emitSchedAllocM`. This loop runs once per PROCESSOR, so one event per P would open kernel objects for
	// machines that mostly never start.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY P BUT P[0] JOINS `__sched_pidle`, THROUGH `pidleput`, SO `__sched_npidle` STARTS AT
	// `numProcs - 1`.** A processor no M has ever claimed and one whose M has parked are the same thing to a
	// `__sched_startm` looking for somewhere to put work.
	//
	// ⚠ No lock is taken around this put and none is needed — `sealedBeforeWorkers`'s argument covers the
	// list exactly as it covers the array being built around it: the first `__gt_init` is reached by the only
	// thread there is, because THIS function is what builds the array a worker could ever be spawned onto. The
	// ring is empty, so the door's assertion cannot fire.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **P[0] IS THE ONE THAT DOES *NOT* JOIN HERE, AND THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE THAT FACT IS WRITTEN DOWN.**
	// P[0] is the processor M0 is born holding and enters its scheduler loop with (`__sched_main_loop`), so
	// offering it here would hand a worker the processor M0 is about to run on. From then on M0 releases and
	// takes processors like every other machine.
	let mainProcIndex = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isMainProc = emitCmp(module, block: loopBody, a: iterId, b: mainProcIndex, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isMainProc, thenBlockId: loopNext.id, elseBlockId: loopOffer.id))

	var offered = emitSchedPidlePutLocked(module, func: func, cur: loopOffer, p: p, holdsWorkLabel: b"pofferheldwork", emptyLabel: b"poffered", ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(offered, op: StdOp.branch(loopNext.id))

	// ⚠ **NO SYSCALL STACK IS COMMITTED HERE ANY MORE (W213-C1) — it is the M's, and it is committed by the
	// OS thread that is about to BE one** (`emitSchedBecomeM`). That is not only a change of place: this loop
	// runs once per PROCESSOR, so a 16-processor box used to commit 1 MB of scratch pages at bring-up for Ms
	// that mostly never start, where it is now one 64 KB region per M that actually runs.
	let nextIter = emitBin(module, block: loopNext, a: iterId, b: emitConst(module, block: loopNext, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopNext, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	loopNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextIter)))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE MAIN OS THREAD BECOMES AN M, AND THEN THAT M TAKES P[0] — two acts, in that order, and
	// W213-C1 is the rung that made them two.** Becoming an M is what gives this thread a syscall stack, a
	// scheduler context and a TLS identity; taking a P is what gives it a run queue and an allocator shard.
	// Until C1 they were one store sequence because TLS pointed at the P, which is exactly the conflation a
	// handoff has to undo.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE M IS BUILT BEFORE THE P IS TAKEN, AND THE ALLOCATIONS INSIDE IT RUN WITH NO M AT ALL.**
	// `__sched_tls_teb_offset` is already published by `entry` above while this thread's TLS slot still reads
	// 0, so the `__slab_alloc` for the M struct itself takes `SlabRawShardRow` under the lock — reaching it
	// through `emitSlabCurrentP`'s **no-M** arm (`emitSchedBecomeM` publishes the slot LAST), not through the
	// P-less-M one. ⛔ **Do not read that as coverage of a P-less M**: this thread has no M yet, which is a
	// different zero from an M that holds no processor. **W213-C2 does produce the second one** — a parked
	// worker with `M->currentP == 0` running its timer poll — but not here.
	//
	// ⚠ **M0 IS ALLOCATED AND REGISTERED ON THE SAME THREAD THAT THEN BECOMES IT**, which is the one place
	// the two halves of M creation are not two threads. It costs nothing and it keeps the roster's rule
	// whole: every M is on `__sched_allm` before anything can look for it, and M0's handle is 0 because this
	// runtime never created this thread — the same 0 the teardown's join already tests for.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LOCK IS TAKEN THOUGH THERE IS NOBODY TO CONTEND WITH.** `emitSchedAllocM`'s contract is that
	// the caller holds it, and honouring a precondition that happens to be vacuous here is cheaper than a
	// second spelling of the registration that does not.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: adopt, ids: ids)
	let zeroIndex = emitConst(module, block: adopt, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let p0 = emitLoadProc(module, block: adopt, index: zeroIndex, ids: ids)
	let m0 = emitSchedAllocM(module, block: adopt, nextP: p0, ids: ids)
	emitSchedRegisterMLocked(module, block: adopt, m: m0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: adopt, label: SchedMainMLabel, value: m0, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ **P[0] IS CLAIMED EXPLICITLY BECAUSE IT DID NOT JOIN `__sched_pidle` ABOVE.** Every other processor is
	// marked taken by the `pidleget` that removes it; M0's skipped the list, so its claim is spelled at the one
	// site that knows that — with no `__sched_npidle` step, since the count is the list's — inside this
	// critical section, like every other writer of the word.
	emitSchedPidleClaimLocked(module, block: adopt, p: p0, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: adopt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedBecomeM(module, block: adopt, m: m0, ids: ids)

	// The main thread takes P[0] directly rather than through `MOffNextP`: it is not waking from a park, and
	// the field is stamped above only so that every M is born with the processor it is going to run on.
	emitStoreField(module, block: adopt, record: m0, offset: MOffNextP, value: emitConst(module, block: adopt, value: 0, ids: ids))
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: adopt, m: m0, p: p0)

	// The poller, before any machine can go idle and claim it, and a last poll of "now": 0 would read as a
	// machine blocked in it. Single-threaded, like the rest of bring-up.
	module.appendOp(adopt, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollInitName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: adopt, label: SchedLastPollLabel, value: emitGtNowNs(module, block: adopt, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(adopt, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedInitProcs'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **BUILDING AN M AND *BEING* ONE ARE TWO ACTS ON TWO THREADS SINCE W213-C2, AND ONE MEASURED FACT IS
// WHAT SPLIT THEM: A THREAD CANNOT LEARN ITS OWN JOINABLE HANDLE.** `osThreadCreate` answers the handle to
// its CALLER — `CreateThread`'s return value on Windows, the tagged wait object `mrt_darwin_thread_create`
// answers on arm64-macOS — and neither platform gives the new thread a way to fetch it (Windows'
// `GetCurrentThread` is a pseudo-handle that names "whoever asks", which `WaitForSingleObject` cannot join).
// So the field that has to reach `MOffOsThreadHandle` is knowable only on the creating side, and the struct
// therefore has to exist before the thread does.
//
// ⇒ **`emitSchedAllocM` runs on the CREATOR and `emitSchedBecomeM` on the thread that will be the M.** The
// two touch disjoint fields, which is what makes them safe to run concurrently: the creator writes the park
// event, the handle, `MOffNextP` and the roster link; the new thread writes the syscall stack, `currentGt`,
// its inline GT's owner and its own TLS slot. No word is written by both.
//
// ⚠ **A WORKER's M STRUCT COMES OUT OF THE CREATOR's OWN P SHARD.** `SlabRawShardRow` is exercised by other
// callers: the poller, back from its wait holding no processor, runs `__gt_timer_check` and `__gt_proc_check`
// with `M->currentP == 0` (`wpolled`), which is `emitSlabCurrentP`'s **P-less-M** arm.

// ⭐⭐ **ALLOCATE AND REGISTER AN M — the creator's half.** The struct, its park event, the processor it is
// being given, and its place on the roster.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`__sched_lock` MUST BE HELD, AND THE ROSTER PUSH MUST BE THE LAST THING THE CREATOR DOES BEFORE IT
// RELEASES.** The roster is what `__sched_stop_workers` joins by, so an M that is on it must already carry a
// handle that can be joined and one that is not on it must not be running. `__sched_startm` holds the lock
// across `osThreadCreate` for exactly this reason, and `__sched_stop_workers` sets the shutdown flag inside
// the same critical section — which is what makes *"no machine is created after the flag is set"* a fact
// rather than a small window.
function emitSchedAllocM(module StdModule, block IrBlock, nextP ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let structBytes = emitConst(module, block: block, value: MStructSize, ids: ids)
	let m = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(m, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(structBytes)))

	// The park. AUTO-reset, so a `__sched_startm` that signals between this machine's lock release and its
	// `osWaitHandle` leaves the event signalled and the wait returns at once — see `MOffParkEvent`, which is
	// where the missed-wakeup argument that replaced the Dekker pair lives.
	let parkEvent = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osEventCreate(parkEvent))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffParkEvent, value: parkEvent)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffNextP, value: nextP)

	// Every other tail field is correct at the slab's zero: no handle yet (the caller stores it if there is
	// one), no spinning credit held, no confirmations, both links null, and a syscall word of 0 — *"not in a
	// bracketed call, generation zero"*, which is why a machine created while `__sysmon` is already running
	// cannot be mistaken for one it has been watching (`MOffSysmonWord`).
	return m
end 'emitSchedAllocM'

// ⭐⭐ **"THIS OS THREAD IS NOW THAT M" — the far half, run by the thread itself.** It commits the 64 KB
// syscall stack, makes the M's inline scheduler GT its own current green thread, and publishes the M in this
// thread's TLS slot. It says nothing at all about a PROCESSOR — `emitSchedAdoptProc` is that, and keeping
// them apart is what W213-C1 was.
//
// ⭐ **THE SYSCALL STACK IS `osAllocPages` AND NOT `__slab_alloc`, so the region is its own PAGE-ALIGNED
// commit** — the top is then 16-aligned, which the shim relies on to land RSP 16-aligned at the `call`. Every
// home this region has had (a `.data` global, then the P, now the M) carried the same requirement.
//
// The inline GT arrives zeroed from the slab, and almost every one of those zeros is correct: `stackBase ==
// 0` is what tells the syscall shim and the prologue guard that this context runs on a real OS-thread stack,
// and both coroutine-queue ends are *"empty"*. Its TIB bounds are captured by the first `__gt_context_switch` away from it — and they are THIS
// OS thread's bounds, which is why the struct is the M's and could not stay on a P a handoff can move.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE ONE ZERO THAT IS NOT CORRECT IS `owner`, AND STAMPING IT IS THE THIRD STORE.** This inline GT
// owns itself as a green thread does, which keeps the owner relation total: code that reads the running GT's
// owner reads a real struct on a scheduler context too, rather than a null pointer.
//
// ⚠ **THE TLS PUBLISH IS LAST, AND THE ORDER IS THE POINT.** Everything above runs on a thread whose slot
// still reads 0 — so any allocation it makes takes the raw shard, which is correct for a thread that holds
// no P — and no other party can see a half-adopted M, because nothing but this thread will ever read this
// slot.
function emitSchedBecomeM(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let sysStackSize = emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtSystemStackSize, ids: ids)
	let sysStackBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(sysStackBase, sizeId: sysStackSize))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffSystemStackSP, value: emitBin(module, block: block, a: sysStackBase, b: sysStackSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))

	let schedulerContext = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffSchedulerContext, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, value: schedulerContext)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: schedulerContext, offset: GtOffOwner, value: schedulerContext)

	let tlsIndex = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedTlsIndexLabel, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.tlsSetValue(tlsIndex, valueId: m))
end 'emitSchedBecomeM'

// ⭐⭐ **"THIS M NOW HOLDS THIS P" — the two stores that are the whole of adoption once the M exists.** The
// processor names the machine (`POffM`), then the machine points at the processor. Every road that hands a
// machine a processor comes through here, and `emitSchedReleaseProcLocked` is the only road back, so the two
// words cannot disagree for longer than one of these pairs takes.
//
// ⚠ **THE PROCESSOR IS ALREADY CLAIMED WHEN IT ARRIVES**: `pidleget`, `__sched_init_procs`'s P[0] and a
// retake each leave it `PStatusActive` and off `__sched_pidle`, so nothing here decides who has it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED INTO `emitSchedBecomeM`, THOUGH BOTH CALLERS CALL BOTH.** They are two
// facts with two lifetimes — a thread is an M once and for ever, and an M holds a P for as long as it holds
// it — and the worker loop calls this one alone on every road back from a park.
function emitSchedAdoptProc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, p ValueId)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffM, value: m)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, value: p)
end 'emitSchedAdoptProc'

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY Ps TO BUILD: THE MACHINE'S PROCESSOR COUNT, unless `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` names a different
// count in `[MinimumProcessorCount, osCpuCount]`.** The answer reaches `settled` as its single block arg;
// every arm below terminates in a branch that supplies it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE IS READ FIRST AND UNCONDITIONALLY, BECAUSE IT IS BOTH THE DEFAULT AND THE CEILING.**
// It used to be read only on the one arm that had a value to clamp, since the default was the constant `1`;
// with the default gone there is no arm that does not need it, and reading it once at the top is what keeps
// *"the default"* and *"the ceiling"* the same number by construction rather than by two agreeing readings.
// `osCpuCount` is read directly rather than through `__cpu_count`, which is a separate runtime entry a
// program opts into and which a scheduler must not drag in.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE FLOOR IS APPLIED TO THE RAW ANSWER, BECAUSE `osCpuCount` CAN REPORT ZERO.**
// `GetActiveProcessorCount` returns 0 on failure and `sysconf` returns -1;
// `CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount` clamps for exactly that reason and has the sabotage to prove the arm is
// reached. Nothing downstream re-checks it: a `cpus` of 0 would become `numProcs`, `__slab_alloc(0)` would
// size the `P*` array, the build loop would run zero times, and `adopt` would then read `__sched_procs[0]`
// out of a zero-byte region and write three fields through whatever it found. A wild write, from a comment
// that said the guard was unnecessary. ⭐ **AND THE FLOOR IS LOAD-BEARING IN A SECOND WAY NOW THAT THIS
// READING IS THE DEFAULT** — before the flip a failed read could only lose a clamp on a variable almost
// nobody set; today it decides the count of every program that runs.
//
// ⚠ **A VALUE THAT IS NOT A PROCESSOR COUNT IS THE MACHINE'S COUNT, NEVER AN ERROR, AND THE THREE WAYS OF
// NOT BEING ONE ARE THREE ARMS OF ONE ANSWER.** `osEnvRead` answers 0 for unset; a value too long for the
// buffer declines; the digit walk answers 0 for anything that does not start with a digit, which is the same
// answer a literal `0` gives — so `< MinimumProcessorCount` covers the malformed value and the out-of-range
// one together: parsed < 1 means ignore.
function emitResolveMaxProcs(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, settled IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var cpuFloor = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocscpufloor", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cpuKnown = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocscpuok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lengthOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocslen", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parseStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsparse", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parsed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsparsed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var underCeiling = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsceiling", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeRequested = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maxprocsreq", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let cpus = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.osCpuCount(cpus))
	let cpuFloorConst = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	let belowFloor = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: cpus, b: cpuFloorConst, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let liveCpus = ids.mint()
	cpuKnown.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(liveCpus, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowFloor, thenBlockId: cpuFloor.id, elseBlockId: cpuKnown.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cpuKnown.id, argIds: argsOf(cpus)))
	let flooredCpus = emitConst(module, block: cpuFloor, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cpuFloor, op: StdOp.branch(cpuKnown.id))
	cpuFloor.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cpuKnown.id, argIds: argsOf(flooredCpus)))

	// ⚠ **`cpuKnown` DOMINATES EVERY BLOCK BELOW, WHICH IS WHY `liveCpus` NEEDS NO SECOND BLOCK ARG.** The
	// decimal walk's own blocks hang off `parseStart`, which this block reaches, so the four arms that answer
	// *"the machine's count"* all name the one value this block settled.
	let nameBuf = emitLiteralCString(module, block: cpuKnown, ids: ids, text: MaxProcsEnvName)
	let bufBytes = emitConst(module, block: cpuKnown, value: MaxProcsEnvBufBytes, ids: ids)
	let buf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cpuKnown, op: StdOp.call(buf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(bufBytes)))
	let written = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cpuKnown, op: StdOp.osEnvRead(written, namePtrId: nameBuf, bufPtrId: buf, bufLenId: bufBytes))

	// ⚠ **`osEnvRead` HAS THREE ANSWERS AND THIS SITE DECODES THEM DIFFERENTLY FROM ITS SIBLING, ON PURPOSE.**
	// `GetEnvironmentVariableA` returns 0 for unset, the CHARACTERS WRITTEN when the value fits, and the size it
	// NEEDS when the buffer is too small — and `SubprocessRuntime.emitSubpReadEnvInto` retries at exactly that
	// size, because a `PATH` legitimately overflows any guess. A processor count does not: a value longer than
	// 32 bytes is not one, so declining is the right answer and a retry would be machinery for a case that
	// cannot occur. The two are one API contract decoded twice, which is why they name each other here.
	let tooLong = emitCmp(module, block: cpuKnown, a: written, b: bufBytes, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cpuKnown, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooLong, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: lengthOk.id))
	cpuKnown.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(liveCpus)))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: lengthOk, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let unset = emitCmp(module, block: lengthOk, a: written, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lengthOk, op: StdOp.condBranch(unset, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: parseStart.id))
	lengthOk.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(liveCpus)))

	// The decimal walk hands its accumulator to `parsed` as that block's arg.
	let requested = ids.mint()
	parsed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(requested, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitParseUnsignedDecimal(module, func: func, cur: parseStart, cstr: buf, parsed: parsed, ids: ids)

	let requestFloor = emitConst(module, block: parsed, value: MinimumProcessorCount, ids: ids)
	let belowFloorRequest = emitCmp(module, block: parsed, a: requested, b: requestFloor, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parsed, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowFloorRequest, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: underCeiling.id))
	parsed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(liveCpus)))

	// ⭐ **THE CEILING, AND IT IS THE ONLY TEST LEFT — a request at or below the machine is taken EXACTLY, so
	// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=1` on a 16-way box builds one P.** That direction is what the variable gained with the
	// flip: it used to be able only to raise a default of 1.
	let overCpu = emitCmp(module, block: underCeiling, a: requested, b: liveCpus, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(underCeiling, op: StdOp.condBranch(overCpu, thenBlockId: settled.id, elseBlockId: takeRequested.id))
	underCeiling.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(liveCpus)))

	module.setTerminator(takeRequested, op: StdOp.branch(settled.id))
	takeRequested.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(settled.id, argIds: argsOf(requested)))
end 'emitResolveMaxProcs'

// The unsigned decimal value of the NUL-terminated bytes at `cstr`, delivered as `parsed`'s block arg.
// The walk stops at the first non-digit (the
// terminator included), so a trailing suffix is IGNORED rather than rejected, and a string that does not
// start with a digit answers 0 — which every caller reads as *"leave the default alone"*.
function emitParseUnsignedDecimal(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cstr ValueId, parsed IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var scanHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"decscan", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notBelow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"decnotbelow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"decstep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let indexId = ids.mint()
	let accId = ids.mint()
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(indexId, argType: StdType.i64))
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(accId, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	// The digit band is tested at BOTH ends rather than by one unsigned wrap: the byte load zero-extends, so
	// the wrap trick would work — and it would also rest on the load's width, which is exactly the kind of
	// implicit dependency a later widening breaks silently. Two compares, no premise.
	let ch = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: scanHdr, base: cstr, index: indexId, ids: ids)
	let asciiZero = emitConst(module, block: scanHdr, value: AsciiZeroDigit, ids: ids)
	let below = emitCmp(module, block: scanHdr, a: ch, b: asciiZero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(below, thenBlockId: parsed.id, elseBlockId: notBelow.id))
	scanHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parsed.id, argIds: argsOf(accId)))

	let asciiNine = emitConst(module, block: notBelow, value: AsciiNineDigit, ids: ids)
	let above = emitCmp(module, block: notBelow, a: ch, b: asciiNine, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notBelow, op: StdOp.condBranch(above, thenBlockId: parsed.id, elseBlockId: scanStep.id))
	notBelow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parsed.id, argIds: argsOf(accId)))

	let stepAsciiZero = emitConst(module, block: scanStep, value: AsciiZeroDigit, ids: ids)
	let digit = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: ch, b: stepAsciiZero, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let radix = emitConst(module, block: scanStep, value: DecimalRadix, ids: ids)
	let scaled = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: accId, b: radix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let nextAcc = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: scaled, b: digit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: scanStep, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: scanStep, a: indexId, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanStep, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	scanStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextIndex, second: nextAcc)))
end 'emitParseUnsignedDecimal'

// ==========================================================================
// The per-P ring — `__sched_runq_put` / `__sched_runq_get` / `__sched_steal`
// ==========================================================================

// The byte address of the ring slot `counter` names — `p + POffRunqBase + (counter and 255) * 8`. Stated
// once because five sites index the ring and the modulus is the whole reason the counters may be monotonic;
// a site that spelled the mask itself would be the one that later addressed a slot the others do not.
function emitRunqSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, counter ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: RunqIndexMask, ids: ids)
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: counter, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqBase, ids: ids), index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitRunqSlotAddr'

// ⭐⭐ **PUBLISH THE RING TAIL — AND THIS ONE INSTRUCTION IS THE PUBLISHER'S STORE IN THE DEKKER PAIR.** It
// advances `p.runqTail` by `delta` with a locked read-modify-write where a plain store would do, because the
// later load is `__sched_wake_or_spawn`'s `__sched_nmspinning` gate, and a plain store may be reordered past
// it: on x86 the `lock` prefix forbids that, and on arm64 the store-release exclusive is ordered before the
// gate's acquire read. `SchedSpinningLabel` states the pairing in full, and `__sched_any_work` is the other
// half. It is also the store-release that makes the slot visible to a thief.
//
// ⛔ **DO NOT "OPTIMISE" IT INTO A PLAIN STORE.** The tail has exactly one writer, so the atomicity buys
// nothing and the BARRIER buys everything; the failure it prevents is a green thread stranded in a ring
// while every machine that could run it sleeps — a wake skipped for a spinner that had already looked.
function emitRunqPublishTail(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, delta ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let tailAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.atomicRmw(ids.mint(), addrId: tailAddr, operandId: delta, opcode: StdAtomicRmwOpcode.add))
end 'emitRunqPublishTail'

// `1` if this CAS lost. Every CAS in this file reads it the same way, and `AtomicCasFailed` is the
// contract's own spelling of the answer (`StdDialect`), not a literal 0 restated at each.
function emitCasLost(module StdModule, block IrBlock, result ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let failed = emitConst(module, block: block, value: AtomicCasFailed, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: result, b: failed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitCasLost'

// ⭐⭐ **IS THE GLOBAL RUN QUEUE EMPTY, OR HOLDING SOMETHING? — the ONE spelling of that peek, and the one
// tier a machine with NO PROCESSOR can both see and reach.**
//
// ⭐ **THE PREDICATE IS THE ARGUMENT BECAUSE THE FOUR CALLERS WANT BOTH SENSES OF ONE FACT.** Two of them
// produce a VALUE to combine (`emitSchedAnyRunnable`'s ring pairing, and the park's final look, both `ne`);
// two BRANCH on emptiness and would otherwise negate it (`__sched_find_runnable`'s 61-schedule fairness peek
// and its global-batch peek, both `eq`). Written out at each site that is one `globalAddr` + `loadIndirect` +
// `cmp` triple spelled four times over one `.data` word — and the day that word is renamed, or gains a
// second head, three of the four copies are found by grep and the fourth is found by a wrong answer.
//
// ⚠ The read is one aligned word, unlocked at three callers and locked at the fourth, and at none of them
// is it a claim on the queue's CONTENTS. Whatever it answers, the LOCKED dequeue behind it is what actually
// takes a thread, and that answers 0 to a racer exactly as it would to an empty queue. `MultiMSharing`'s
// `underSchedLock` note enumerates the unlocked readers of this word by name.
function emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pred StdCmpPred, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let globalHead = emitQueueLoadHead(module, block: block, ends: GtQueueEnds.globalLabels, ids: ids)
	let noGlobalHead = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: globalHead, b: noGlobalHead, pred: pred, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedGlobalQueueIs'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS PROCESSOR'S RING OR THE GLOBAL QUEUE HOLD A TOKEN RIGHT NOW?** Asked by a machine whose own
// poll may have readied something (`widlefire`, choosing between searching again and parking), and by
// `__sched_handoffp` and `__sched_retake`, choosing whether a processor is worth handing on.
//
// ⚠ **GO RE-READS THE COUNTERS AND THIS DOES NOT — because the compiler has no `runnext`.** Go's `runqempty`
// loops because a `runnext` slot can hold a thread while head and tail agree; `__sched_runq_get` has no such
// slot (`POffRunnext`), so `tail != head` IS a non-empty ring, and one read of each is exact rather than
// optimistic.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT WALK THE OTHER Ps' RINGS.** Work on another processor's ring is that processor's machine's
// to run, and a wake it is owed is its publisher's to give — `__sched_any_work` is where one skipped for a
// spinner is caught. Both loads are unlocked: they choose an ARM, and both arms are correct whatever they
// answer.
function emitSchedAnyRunnable(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let ringHasWork = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let globalHasWork = emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module, block: block, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)

	// Both operands are `cmp` results, so they are 0 or 1 and a bitwise OR is the logical one — the argument
	// `emitGtIsDropped` makes for its own `and`.
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: ringHasWork, b: globalHasWork, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedAnyRunnable'

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_runq_put(gt)` — PUSH A RUNNABLE GREEN THREAD ONTO THE CURRENT P's RING, WITH NO LOCK.**
// Go's `runqput(pp, gp, next: false)` (`proc.go:7529`) plus `runqputslow` (`proc.go:7576`), which is where a
// full ring goes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **ONLY THE OWNING P's M MAY PUSH A RING, which is what makes the tail a single-writer word and the
// push three instructions — AND THE RULE IS NOW ENFORCED HERE RATHER THAN OWED BY EVERY CALLER (SV1).** The
// IOCP completion thread has no M and therefore no P: `emitLoadCurrentMOrBail` answers 0 for it, and a ring push
// through a null P would store `gt` at offset `POffRunqBase` of address 0. It publishes GREEN THREADS now (a
// parked reader resumed by a completion packet may be one), so the no-ring arm below routes it to the GLOBAL
// queue — the tier that is everybody's and is behind a lock — and no caller has to remember which kind of
// thread it is on.
//
// **THE OVERFLOW IS A ROUTING DECISION, NEVER AN ERROR.** A full ring moves its OLDEST HALF plus the new
// thread to the global queue, so the ring keeps the freshest work and the rest becomes everybody's. The
// 61-schedule fairness check in `__sched_find_runnable` is what stops the half that moved from starving.
//
// ⭐ **THE BATCH IS READ *AFTER* THE HEAD CAS, WHERE GO READS IT BEFORE — AND THE REASON IS THAT GO NEEDS A
// SCRATCH ARRAY AND THE COMPILER DOES NOT.** `runqputslow` copies 128 entries into a frame array, CASes the head and
// then links them, and the copy has to come first because its own linking overwrites each G's `schedlink`.
// Here the batch goes onto the global FIFO one at a time, so there is nothing to stage — and after a WON CAS
// the slots `[h, h+n)` belong to this thread alone: no thief can claim them (the head has moved past) and no
// push can overwrite them, because this function is the ring's only pusher and it has not pushed yet. A 1 KB
// frame array avoided, and one fewer place for two copies of a batch to disagree.
function buildSchedRunqPut(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedRunqPutName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var globalOnly = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqnoproc", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var retry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqretry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var push = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqpush", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var moveLock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovelock", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var moveHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovehdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var moveBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovebody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var moveNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovenext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var moveDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rqmovedone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId

	// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE WAYS TO HAVE NO RING ALL REACH THE SAME ARM.** This caller really does run on a thread
	// with NO M — the IOCP completion loop, through `__gt_ready_locked` — and an M holding no processor
	// is the state a handoff produces; a thread with no ring of its own belongs on the global queue whichever
	// of the three reasons put it there.
	let p = emitLoadCurrentPOrBail(module, cur: entry, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, haveProc: retry, noProc: globalOnly, ids: ids)

	// globalOnly: this OS thread owns no P, so it has no ring to push — the global queue is where a thread
	// belongs when nobody owns it yet, and it is exactly what `__sched_find_runnable`'s batch grab is for. The
	// lock is taken HERE rather than owed by the caller: a CRITICAL_SECTION is recursive, so the one caller
	// that already holds it (every `__gt_ready_locked` caller) pays two counted ops
	// and nothing else — against a precondition no site could check.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: globalOnly, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: globalOnly, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: globalOnly, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(globalOnly, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// retry: read both counters and ask whether the ring is full. `tail - head` IS the length — see
	// `POffRunqHead` for why no third word says so. A plain load of the head is a load-ACQUIRE on the one
	// lane that has green threads (x64 never reorders load/load), which is the ordering Go spells out.
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: retry, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: retry, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let queued = emitBin(module, block: retry, a: tail, b: head, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let capacity = emitConst(module, block: retry, value: RunqSlots, ids: ids)
	let full = emitCmp(module, block: retry, a: queued, b: capacity, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(retry, op: StdOp.condBranch(full, thenBlockId: claim.id, elseBlockId: push.id))

	// push: write the slot, THEN publish the tail. The order is the whole contract with a thief — the locked
	// tail bump is a release, so a thief that sees the new tail sees the slot that tail describes.
	emitStoreField(module, block: push, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: push, p: p, counter: tail, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: gt)
	let oneSlot = emitConst(module, block: push, value: 1, ids: ids)
	emitRunqPublishTail(module, block: push, p: p, delta: oneSlot, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(push, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// claim: take the oldest half by CAS. A thief that beat us to it leaves the ring no longer full, so the
	// lost CAS re-reads and the push above succeeds — Go's `goto retry`, and the reason this is a loop.
	let batchCount = emitConst(module, block: claim, value: RunqHalfSlots, ids: ids)
	let claimedHead = emitBin(module, block: claim, a: head, b: batchCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: claim, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let claimed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(claim, op: StdOp.atomicCas(claimed, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: claimedHead))
	let lostClaim = emitCasLost(module, block: claim, result: claimed, ids: ids)
	let moveIndex = ids.mint()
	moveHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(moveIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(claim, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostClaim, thenBlockId: retry.id, elseBlockId: moveLock.id))

	// ⛔⛔ **THE ACQUIRE GETS A BLOCK OF ITS OWN, AND BOTH NEIGHBOURS WOULD BE A HANG.** `osLockEnter` is a
	// Win32 CRITICAL_SECTION, so it COUNTS: an acquire in the loop header below would recurse it 128 times
	// against the one release at `moveDone`, and an acquire in `claim` would be taken again on every lost-CAS
	// retry. Both leave the lock held for the process's life, and neither is a fault — the failure is a hang in
	// the next thread to want the global queue. One acquire, on the one edge that runs exactly once.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CALLER THAT ALREADY HOLDS THE LOCK REACHES HERE, AND THAT IS SAFE**, because the same recursion
	// counts a nested pair correctly. What such a caller must not rely on is this leave being the StoreLoad
	// fence for its own wake — a recursive leave releases nothing — and none does: the ring's fence is
	// `emitRunqPublishTail`'s locked op, and a batch publisher's is its own outer release.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: moveLock, ids: ids)
	let moveStart = emitConst(module, block: moveLock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveLock, op: StdOp.branch(moveHdr.id))
	moveLock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(moveHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(moveStart)))

	let moved = emitCmp(module, block: moveHdr, a: moveIndex, b: batchCount, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(moved, thenBlockId: moveDone.id, elseBlockId: moveBody.id))

	// ⛔⛔ **A TOMBSTONE IN THE BATCH MOVES LIKE A LIVE THREAD, AND THAT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN A
	// SIMPLIFICATION (SV1).** This loop used to ask `emitGtIsDropped` of every slot and call
	// `__gt_runner_done` on the ones that answered — **while holding `__sched_lock`**, which is the one thing
	// that call may never be made under: it reaches `__gt_reclaim`'s releaser, and a releaser that reaches
	// `__gt_promise_drop` spin-waits with a release that a recursive CRITICAL_SECTION turns into a no-op. See
	// `GtRuntime.GtRunnerDoneName`, where the rule is written down.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING IS LEAKED BY MOVING ONE.** A tombstone on the global queue is refused and reclaimed by
	// whoever pops it — `emitGtTombstoneHandback`, reached from `__sched_find_runnable` with the lock RELEASED,
	// which is the same reclaim on the correct side of the lock. What the drop itself sweeps
	// (`__sched_sweep_dropped`) is the FRONT of this ring and of the global queue, so the bound
	// `async-promise-drop.spawn-drop-loop-bounded` pins is unchanged.
	let slotCounter = emitBin(module, block: moveBody, a: head, b: moveIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let movingGt = emitLoadField(module, block: moveBody, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: moveBody, p: p, counter: slotCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: moveBody, gt: movingGt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveBody, op: StdOp.branch(moveNext.id))

	let moveStep = emitConst(module, block: moveNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextMoveIndex = emitBin(module, block: moveNext, a: moveIndex, b: moveStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveNext, op: StdOp.branch(moveHdr.id))
	moveNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(moveHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextMoveIndex)))

	// The new thread goes to the global tail BEHIND the batch, which is Go's order (`batch[n] = gp`) and the
	// only one that keeps the ring's FIFO promise across an overflow.
	emitSchedEnqueueLocked(module, block: moveDone, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: moveDone, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moveDone, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedRunqPut'

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_runq_get(p) -> gt` — TAKE THE OLDEST THREAD OFF `p`'s RING, OR 0.** Go's `runqget`
// (`proc.go:7650`), minus the `runnext` slot the compiler deliberately does not build (`POffRunnext`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE CAS ON THE HEAD IS WHAT MAKES A DOUBLE POP STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, AND THAT IS THIS RUNG'S
// WHOLE THESIS.** Without it a thief and the owner both pop the same head node — *"kind=local and
// kind=steal_first on the same gt within the same millisecond, immediately followed by SIGSEGV"* — which is
// why a design without the CAS has to put each P's LOCAL queue under the GLOBAL lock.
// Here the two contend on ONE word with a compare-and-swap, so exactly one of them can advance
// past a given index and the loser re-reads. A plain load/store here IS that SIGSEGV.
//
// ⚠ **THE SLOT IS READ BEFORE THE CAS**, which looks like the wrong order and is Go's. A read of a slot the
// CAS then fails to claim is harmless — the value is discarded — while reading it afterwards would be reading
// a slot the winner may already have overwritten.
function buildSchedRunqGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedRunqGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgloop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgtake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var took = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgtook", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var emptyBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgempty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let p = 0 as ValueId
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: loopHdr, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: loopHdr, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(empty, thenBlockId: emptyBlock.id, elseBlockId: take.id))

	let g = emitLoadField(module, block: take, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: take, p: p, counter: head, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: take, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let oneSlot = emitConst(module, block: take, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextHead = emitBin(module, block: take, a: head, b: oneSlot, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(take, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: nextHead))
	let lost = emitCasLost(module, block: take, result: won, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(take, op: StdOp.condBranch(lost, thenBlockId: loopHdr.id, elseBlockId: took.id))

	// `take` is `took`'s only predecessor, so `g` is in scope with no block argument — the dominance argument
	// every branching emitter in this runtime makes for itself.
	module.setTerminator(took, op: StdOp.ret(g))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: emptyBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedRunqGet'

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_steal(p, victim) -> gt` — GRAB HALF OF `victim`'s RING INTO `p`'s, AND ANSWER ONE OF THEM.**
// Go's `runqgrab` (`proc.go:7714`) and `runqsteal` (`proc.go:7782`) as ONE function, because Go's split
// exists to serve a second caller (`runqdrain`) that the compiler has no equivalent of.
//
// **HALF, ROUNDED UP** (`n - n/2`), so a victim holding ONE thread still gives it up — which is what makes a
// pair of processors converge instead of one of them spinning on a queue it is never allowed to empty.
//
// ⚠ **THE `n > half` RE-READ IS NOT DEFENSIVE, it is the one place an inconsistent pair of counters can be
// observed.** Head and tail are read separately while the victim's owner is pushing between them, so a tail
// from before a wrap paired with a head from after it computes a length larger than the ring exists to hold.
// Go re-reads for exactly this, and says so at the same line.
//
// ⛔ **ITS PRECONDITION IS THAT `p`'s RING IS EMPTY, and unlike Go's this one cannot be violated.** Go's
// `runqsteal` carries a `throw("runqsteal: runq overflow")` because its `stealWork` may RUN TIMERS first,
// which readies an arbitrary number of Gs onto the thief's OWN ring before the grab (`proc.go:3886-3893`
// says so outright). the compiler's stealing phase runs no timers, is reached only after `__sched_runq_get` answered
// 0, and the ring's only pusher is the very thread running this — so the free space is the whole ring and
// the grab is at most half of one.
function buildSchedSteal(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedStealName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var grabHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sgrab", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ssized", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var copyHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scopyhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var copyBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scopybody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var commit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scommit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var publish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spublish", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var publishTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spubtail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var answer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sanswer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var failBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sfail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let p = 0 as ValueId
	let victim = 1 as ValueId

	// Our own tail, read once: nobody else writes it, and the copy addresses slots relative to it.
	let ourTail = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(grabHdr.id))

	let vHead = emitLoadField(module, block: grabHdr, record: victim, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let vTail = emitLoadField(module, block: grabHdr, record: victim, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let vLen = emitBin(module, block: grabHdr, a: vTail, b: vHead, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let halfShift = emitConst(module, block: grabHdr, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let vHalf = emitBin(module, block: grabHdr, a: vLen, b: halfShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let grabCount = emitBin(module, block: grabHdr, a: vLen, b: vHalf, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let grabZero = emitConst(module, block: grabHdr, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// `<= 0` and not `== 0`: a torn read can make `vLen` negative, and a negative count would run the copy
	// loop zero times and then answer a slot nobody wrote. Both bad readings land on the same arm.
	let nothing = emitCmp(module, block: grabHdr, a: grabCount, b: grabZero, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(grabHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothing, thenBlockId: failBlock.id, elseBlockId: sized.id))

	let grabCap = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: RunqHalfSlots, ids: ids)
	let inconsistent = emitCmp(module, block: sized, a: grabCount, b: grabCap, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let copyStart = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let copyIndex = ids.mint()
	copyHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(copyIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(sized, op: StdOp.condBranch(inconsistent, thenBlockId: grabHdr.id, elseBlockId: copyHdr.id))
	sized.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(copyStart)))

	let copied = emitCmp(module, block: copyHdr, a: copyIndex, b: grabCount, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copyHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(copied, thenBlockId: commit.id, elseBlockId: copyBody.id))

	let fromCounter = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: vHead, b: copyIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let stolenGt = emitLoadField(module, block: copyBody, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: copyBody, p: victim, counter: fromCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let toCounter = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: ourTail, b: copyIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: copyBody, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: copyBody, p: p, counter: toCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: stolenGt)
	let copyStep = emitConst(module, block: copyBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextCopyIndex = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: copyIndex, b: copyStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copyBody, op: StdOp.branch(copyHdr.id))
	copyBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextCopyIndex)))

	// commit: the copy is only a copy until the victim's head moves past it. A lost CAS means the victim (or
	// another thief) consumed some of what we read, so everything above is discarded and re-read.
	let vHeadAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: commit, record: victim, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let newVHead = emitBin(module, block: commit, a: vHead, b: grabCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let grabbed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.atomicCas(grabbed, addrId: vHeadAddr, expectedId: vHead, replacementId: newVHead))
	let lostGrab = emitCasLost(module, block: commit, result: grabbed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(commit, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostGrab, thenBlockId: grabHdr.id, elseBlockId: publish.id))

	// publish: the LAST of the grabbed threads is the answer and is NOT published — this M runs it now — so
	// the tail advances by one fewer than we took. Taking the last rather than the first leaves the ones that
	// stay in the ring in their original order, which is Go's choice and the FIFO promise's.
	let publishStep = emitConst(module, block: publish, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let keptCount = emitBin(module, block: publish, a: grabCount, b: publishStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let answerCounter = emitBin(module, block: publish, a: ourTail, b: keptCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let answerGt = emitLoadField(module, block: publish, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: publish, p: p, counter: answerCounter, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let publishZero = emitConst(module, block: publish, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nonePublished = emitCmp(module, block: publish, a: keptCount, b: publishZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publish, op: StdOp.condBranch(nonePublished, thenBlockId: answer.id, elseBlockId: publishTail.id))

	emitRunqPublishTail(module, block: publishTail, p: p, delta: keptCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publishTail, op: StdOp.branch(answer.id))

	module.setTerminator(answer, op: StdOp.ret(answerGt))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: failBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedSteal'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_find_runnable`
// ==========================================================================

// Advance `p.rng` one xorshift64 step and answer the new value — `x ^= x<<13; x ^= x>>7; x ^= x<<17`, the
// generator used for victim selection.
//
// ⚠ **THE SHIFT RIGHT IS LOGICAL, AND THE ARITHMETIC ONE WOULD BREAK IT.** xorshift64 is defined over
// unsigned words; an arithmetic shift smears the sign bit and the sequence degenerates. The value is then
// consumed by an UNSIGNED modulus for the same reason: a signed `mod` of a negative draw would index the P
// array below zero.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESULT MUST BE WRITTEN BACK** or the "generator" answers one constant for ever.
function emitXorshiftStep(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let seed = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRng, ids: ids)
	let up13 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: seed, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 13, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let mixed13 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: seed, b: up13, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	let down7 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed13, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 7, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let mixed7 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed13, b: down7, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	let up17 = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed7, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 17, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let stepped = emitBin(module, block: block, a: mixed7, b: up17, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffRng, value: stepped)
	return stepped
end 'emitXorshiftStep'

// `a mod b` where both are known non-negative — the P-index arithmetic, and the ONE place the signedness of
// that modulus is decided. See `emitXorshiftStep` for why a signed one would be a wild index.
function emitUnsignedMod(module StdModule, block IrBlock, a ValueId, b ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let v = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.mod(v, lhs: a, rhs: b, signedness: IntegerSignedness.unsigned))
	return v
end 'emitUnsignedMod'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_find_runnable(spinner) -> gt` — THE ONE PLACE THE SCHEDULER DECIDES WHAT RUNS NEXT, and 0
// when there is nothing to run.** Go's `findRunnable` (`proc.go:3405`) in Go's ORDER, with everything the
// compiler has no equivalent of removed (the trace reader, the GC workers, netpoll's optimistic poll, timer
// stealing):
//
//   1. **every 61st schedule, the GLOBAL queue first** — otherwise a ring that keeps refilling starves it;
//   2. **this P's own ring** (`__sched_runq_get`, a CAS on the head);
//   3. **a BATCH off the global queue** into this ring, so one lock acquisition serves many schedules;
//   4. **four rounds of STEALING**, each visiting every other active P once from a random start — only as a
//      SPINNER (the spinner cap at `frspingate`).
//
// ⭐ **THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THE SCHEDULER DECIDES WHAT RUNS NEXT** — every scheduler loop asks it — which is
// what makes a policy (fairness, stealing, the dropped-thread refusal) a property of the scheduler rather than
// of whichever caller remembered it. What it answers is a STRAND TOKEN: the owner of a strand whose members
// the caller's strand runner (`GtRuntime.__gt_run_strand`) then runs.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE A DROPPED THREAD IS REFUSED.** A ring cannot be spliced, so `__gt_promise_drop`
// renounces a never-run thread and leaves it queued as a tombstone (`GtRuntime.GtOffTeardown`); the candidate
// this function is about to hand back is therefore checked, reclaimed if it is one, and the selection re-run.
// Doing it here rather than in each caller is what makes "a dropped thread never runs" true of every path at
// once — including a thread that reached this P inside a STOLEN batch, which no drop could have reached.
//
// ⚠ **THE CHECK IS NOT A CLAIM, AND IT DOES NOT NEED TO BE.** The POP is the claim: the ring's head CAS and
// the global queue's lock each hand a given thread to exactly one taker, so the party that reads the
// tombstone here is the only party that can. A promise renounced AFTER this read simply leaves a thread that
// runs to completion and is reclaimed by the strand runner's hand-off — the rendezvous makes the late race a
// wasted turn rather than a leak, which is the property that let this stay a plain load.
//
// ⚠ **THE VICTIM WALK IS A RANDOM START PLUS A FULL SEQUENTIAL SWEEP, WHERE GO USES A COPRIME STRIDE**
// (`stealOrder.start(cheaprand())`, an `randomEnum` over a coprime step). Both visit every P exactly once
// per round; the stride exists to decorrelate the ORDER in which many thieves walk the same array, which
// matters at Go's tens-to-hundreds of Ps and buys nothing at the handful the compiler runs — while a coprime table
// is a second structure to build at init and to size. The random START is kept, because that is what stops
// every idle P from hammering P[0].
function buildSchedFindRunnable(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedFindRunnableName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fairPeek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frfairpeek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fairTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frfairtake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var localPop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frlocal", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var globalPeek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frglobalpeek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatch", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchCapMax = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchcapmax", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchSized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchsized", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchtake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchPublish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchpublish", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchtail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var batchUnlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchunlock", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinGate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frspingate", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frspincap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinClaim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frspinclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var roundHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frroundhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var roundNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frroundnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var victimHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var victimTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimtest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var victimActive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimactive", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var victimTry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimtry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var victimNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frvictimnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stole = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frstole", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var candidate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frcandidate", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The candidate merge — every source of a green thread reaches it, and it is the only exit.
	let found = ids.mint()
	candidate.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(found, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let spinner = 0 as ValueId
	let p = emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: entry, ids: ids)

	// The schedule counter. Bumped once per CALL rather than once per thread handed back, so the retry a
	// reclaimed thread causes does not spend a second tick — a dropped thread was never a schedule.
	let tickStep = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let tick = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffSchedTick, ids: ids), b: tickStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: p, offset: POffSchedTick, value: tick)
	let interval = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtFairnessInterval, ids: ids)
	let phase = emitUnsignedMod(module, block: entry, a: tick, b: interval, ids: ids)
	let entryZero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let atFairness = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: phase, b: entryZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(atFairness, thenBlockId: fairPeek.id, elseBlockId: localPop.id))

	// The unlocked peek before the lock — Go's `!sched.runq.empty()` guard, and safe because it decides only
	// whether to LOOK: the locked dequeue behind it is what decides anything.
	let fairEmpty = emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module, block: fairPeek, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fairPeek, op: StdOp.condBranch(fairEmpty, thenBlockId: localPop.id, elseBlockId: fairTake.id))

	let fairGt = emitSchedDequeueLocked(module, block: fairTake, ids: ids)
	let fairTakeZero = emitConst(module, block: fairTake, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let fairMissed = emitCmp(module, block: fairTake, a: fairGt, b: fairTakeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fairTake, op: StdOp.condBranch(fairMissed, thenBlockId: localPop.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	fairTake.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(fairGt)))

	// localPop is also the RETRY target after a reclaimed thread: the fairness tick has already been spent,
	// and the global queue has already been consulted on the arm that led here.
	let localGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(localPop, op: StdOp.call(localGt, callee: SchedRunqGetName, args: argsOf(p)))
	let localZero = emitConst(module, block: localPop, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let localEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: localPop, a: localGt, b: localZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(localPop, op: StdOp.condBranch(localEmpty, thenBlockId: globalPeek.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	localPop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(localGt)))

	let globalEmpty = emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module, block: globalPeek, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(globalPeek, op: StdOp.condBranch(globalEmpty, thenBlockId: spinGate.id, elseBlockId: batchStart.id))

	// `globalPeek` dominates the batch, the spinner cap and the steal rounds, so the processor count all three
	// divide or bound by is read once, there.
	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: globalPeek, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **THE BATCH IS `min(GlobalBatchMax, size, size/numProcs + 1)`** — Go's `globrunqgetbatch`. The share
	// is computed under the lock `__sched_runq_size` is kept under, so it describes the queue this walk takes
	// from; the `size` term is the walk stopping where the queue empties, which under that lock is exactly
	// `__sched_runq_size` threads.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHY IT CANNOT OVERFLOW THE RING.** `localPop` answered 0, so this ring was empty; its only pusher is
	// this M, which is here; a thief can only make it emptier. So the free space is the whole 256 slots and the
	// batch takes at most `GlobalBatchMax` (128) — an invariant, not a hope, and the reason there is no capacity
	// test in the loop below.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: batchStart, ids: ids)
	let queued = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: batchStart, label: SchedRunqSizeLabel, ids: ids)
	let perProc = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(batchStart, op: StdOp.div(perProc, lhs: queued, rhs: numProcs, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	let share = emitBin(module, block: batchStart, a: perProc, b: emitConst(module, block: batchStart, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let batchMax = emitConst(module, block: batchStart, value: GlobalBatchMax, ids: ids)
	let shareOverMax = emitCmp(module, block: batchStart, a: share, b: batchMax, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let batchQuota = ids.mint()
	batchSized.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(batchQuota, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(batchStart, op: StdOp.condBranch(shareOverMax, thenBlockId: batchCapMax.id, elseBlockId: batchSized.id))
	batchStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchSized.id, argIds: argsOf(share)))

	let cappedQuota = emitConst(module, block: batchCapMax, value: GlobalBatchMax, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchCapMax, op: StdOp.branch(batchSized.id))
	batchCapMax.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchSized.id, argIds: argsOf(cappedQuota)))

	let batchBase = emitLoadField(module, block: batchSized, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let batchFirst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(batchSized, op: StdOp.call(batchFirst, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	// The ring takes one short of the quota, because the first thread was taken above and is the answer.
	let batchCap = emitBin(module, block: batchSized, a: batchQuota, b: emitConst(module, block: batchSized, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let batchSizedZero = emitConst(module, block: batchSized, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let batchNone = emitCmp(module, block: batchSized, a: batchFirst, b: batchSizedZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let batchCount = ids.mint()
	batchHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(batchCount, argType: StdType.i64))
	let published = ids.mint()
	batchPublish.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(published, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(batchSized, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchNone, thenBlockId: batchPublish.id, elseBlockId: batchHdr.id))
	batchSized.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchPublish.id, argIds: argsOf(batchSizedZero)))
	batchSized.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(batchSizedZero)))

	let batchFull = emitCmp(module, block: batchHdr, a: batchCount, b: batchCap, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchFull, thenBlockId: batchPublish.id, elseBlockId: batchTake.id))
	batchHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchPublish.id, argIds: argsOf(batchCount)))

	let batchGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(batchTake, op: StdOp.call(batchGt, callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let batchTakeZero = emitConst(module, block: batchTake, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let batchDrained = emitCmp(module, block: batchTake, a: batchGt, b: batchTakeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	var batchStore = module.addBlock(func, label: b"frbatchstore", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(batchTake, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchDrained, thenBlockId: batchPublish.id, elseBlockId: batchStore.id))
	batchTake.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchPublish.id, argIds: argsOf(batchCount)))

	let batchSlot = emitBin(module, block: batchStore, a: batchBase, b: batchCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: batchStore, record: emitRunqSlotAddr(module, block: batchStore, p: p, counter: batchSlot, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: batchGt)
	let batchStep = emitConst(module, block: batchStore, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextBatchCount = emitBin(module, block: batchStore, a: batchCount, b: batchStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchStore, op: StdOp.branch(batchHdr.id))
	batchStore.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(batchHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextBatchCount)))

	let publishZero = emitConst(module, block: batchPublish, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingQueued = emitCmp(module, block: batchPublish, a: published, b: publishZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchPublish, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingQueued, thenBlockId: batchUnlock.id, elseBlockId: batchTail.id))

	emitRunqPublishTail(module, block: batchTail, p: p, delta: published, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchTail, op: StdOp.branch(batchUnlock.id))

	// The release is inside the function rather than at each exit for `emitGtIoCancelArm`'s reason: several
	// arms reach it, and one release block cannot have an arm added later that forgets it.
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: batchUnlock, ids: ids)
	let unlockZero = emitConst(module, block: batchUnlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let batchFailed = emitCmp(module, block: batchUnlock, a: batchFirst, b: unlockZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(batchUnlock, op: StdOp.condBranch(batchFailed, thenBlockId: spinGate.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	batchUnlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(batchFirst)))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SPINNER CAP — Go's `findRunnable` (`proc.go:3528`): a machine steals only if it is already
	// spinning or `2 * nmspinning < numProcs - npidle`,** *"to prevent excessive CPU consumption when
	// GOMAXPROCS>>1 but the program parallelism is low"*. Half the busy processors' worth of spinners is enough
	// to find what they publish; a machine turned away goes to its park, whose final look still covers the
	// global queue. It becomes a spinner HERE, charged to `spinner`, so the credit is held exactly while the
	// steal rounds run and `wfound` / `wpark` give it back. A machine already holding a credit was handed it by
	// the waker that signalled it, which reserved it for exactly this search.
	//
	// `spinGate` dominates the two blocks that enter the rounds, so one first-round index serves both.
	let firstRound = emitConst(module, block: spinGate, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let heldConst = emitConst(module, block: spinGate, value: MSpinningHeld, ids: ids)
	let alreadySpinning = emitCmp(module, block: spinGate, a: emitLoadField(module, block: spinGate, record: spinner, offset: MOffSpinning, ids: ids), b: heldConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spinGate, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadySpinning, thenBlockId: roundHdr.id, elseBlockId: spinCap.id))
	spinGate.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(roundHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(firstRound)))

	// The unlocked read of `__sched_npidle` that `MultiMSharing.underSchedLock` enumerates, beside the plain load
	// of `__sched_nmspinning` the wake gate also makes.
	let spinners = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: spinCap, label: SchedSpinningLabel, ids: ids)
	let idleProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: spinCap, label: SchedNpidleLabel, ids: ids)
	let busyProcs = emitBin(module, block: spinCap, a: numProcs, b: idleProcs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let twiceSpinners = emitBin(module, block: spinCap, a: spinners, b: spinners, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let roomToSpin = emitCmp(module, block: spinCap, a: twiceSpinners, b: busyProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let turnedAway = emitConst(module, block: spinCap, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spinCap, op: StdOp.condBranch(roomToSpin, thenBlockId: spinClaim.id, elseBlockId: candidate.id))
	spinCap.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(turnedAway)))

	_ = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: spinClaim, m: spinner, transition: SpinningTransition.becomeSpinning, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spinClaim, op: StdOp.branch(roundHdr.id))
	spinClaim.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(roundHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(firstRound)))

	// The stealing rounds. `roundHdr` dominates every block below it, so the round index and the random start
	// are in scope throughout without being threaded through a block argument.
	let roundIndex = ids.mint()
	roundHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(roundIndex, argType: StdType.i64))

	let roundCap = emitConst(module, block: roundHdr, value: StealRounds, ids: ids)
	let roundsDone = emitCmp(module, block: roundHdr, a: roundIndex, b: roundCap, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	let giveUp = emitConst(module, block: roundHdr, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let victimStart = emitUnsignedMod(module, block: roundHdr, a: emitXorshiftStep(module, block: roundHdr, p: p, ids: ids), b: numProcs, ids: ids)
	let victimIndex = ids.mint()
	victimHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(victimIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(roundHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(roundsDone, thenBlockId: candidate.id, elseBlockId: victimHdr.id))
	roundHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(giveUp)))
	roundHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(victimHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(giveUp)))

	let victimsDone = emitCmp(module, block: victimHdr, a: victimIndex, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(victimsDone, thenBlockId: roundNext.id, elseBlockId: victimTest.id))

	let victimSlot = emitUnsignedMod(module, block: victimTest, a: emitBin(module, block: victimTest, a: victimStart, b: victimIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids), b: numProcs, ids: ids)
	let victim = emitLoadProc(module, block: victimTest, index: victimSlot, ids: ids)
	let isSelf = emitCmp(module, block: victimTest, a: victim, b: p, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSelf, thenBlockId: victimNext.id, elseBlockId: victimActive.id))

	// A P no M has claimed has an empty ring by construction, and reading it would be a load for nothing.
	let victimStatus = emitLoadField(module, block: victimActive, record: victim, offset: POffStatus, ids: ids)
	let activeConst = emitConst(module, block: victimActive, value: PStatusActive, ids: ids)
	let victimIdle = emitCmp(module, block: victimActive, a: victimStatus, b: activeConst, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimActive, op: StdOp.condBranch(victimIdle, thenBlockId: victimNext.id, elseBlockId: victimTry.id))

	let stolenGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(victimTry, op: StdOp.call(stolenGt, callee: SchedStealName, args: twoArgs(p, second: victim)))
	let stealZero = emitConst(module, block: victimTry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stealFailed = emitCmp(module, block: victimTry, a: stolenGt, b: stealZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimTry, op: StdOp.condBranch(stealFailed, thenBlockId: victimNext.id, elseBlockId: stole.id))

	// The counter `__Builtins.schedStealCount()` sums. A P counts its OWN steals and nothing else writes the
	// word, so `multiM: false` — the plain arm. The prior value has no reader.
	let stealStep = emitConst(module, block: stole, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: stole, record: p, offset: POffStealCount, delta: stealStep, multiM: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(stole, op: StdOp.branch(candidate.id))
	stole.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(stolenGt)))

	let victimStep = emitConst(module, block: victimNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextVictim = emitBin(module, block: victimNext, a: victimIndex, b: victimStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(victimNext, op: StdOp.branch(victimHdr.id))
	victimNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(victimHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextVictim)))

	let roundStep = emitConst(module, block: roundNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextRound = emitBin(module, block: roundNext, a: roundIndex, b: roundStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(roundNext, op: StdOp.branch(roundHdr.id))
	roundNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(roundHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextRound)))

	// The candidate/tombstone/hand-back tail, which `GtRuntime.__gt_strand_next` is the other caller of — one
	// body, so a change to what "dropped" means cannot reach one consumer and miss the other. ⚠ `localPop`
	// rather than `entry` is the retry, so a reclaimed thread costs no second fairness tick; that is the only
	// argument this emitter takes, and the only thing the two consumers differ in.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE REFUSAL IS VACUOUS FROM *THIS* CALLER TODAY, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT (W226/A5).** This walk
	// only ever hands back STRAND TOKENS, which are green threads, and no green thread can be a tombstone:
	// `emitGtIsDropped` needs the consumer's teardown ticket, which only an `await` or a `__gt_promise_drop`
	// adds, and both need a **Promise** — while `spawn` yields a `<T>.handle`, an `async` frame is a coroutine on
	// its owner's strand queue, and a reply cell is swept as a coroutine. So the compare below is a constant
	// `false` on every program this tree can build, and a reader must not take its presence as evidence that
	// a producer exists.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS KEPT, NOT DELETED, AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT JUDGEMENT FROM THE ONE `__sched_sweep_dropped`
	// GOT.** The sweep's ring half was deleted because its peek DEREFERENCED AN UNCLAIMED THREAD — a fault,
	// not merely dead weight. This one asks the same question of a candidate it has already popped, so it is
	// safe at any processor count, it is two loads on a path that just took a lock, and it is the reaper the
	// deleted half's own note points a future producer at. Dead weight with a live sibling is worth keeping;
	// a latent access violation is not.
	emitGtTombstoneHandback(module, func: func, candidate: candidate, found: found, retry: localPop, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedFindRunnable'

// The walk `SchedAnyWorkName` describes.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ITS FIRST READ IS AN ACQUIRE, AND THAT ONE INSTRUCTION IS THE SEARCHER'S BARRIER.** Its callers have
// just decremented `__sched_nmspinning` with a store-release exclusive; the acquire load of the processor count
// is ordered after that store on arm64 (x86's `lock` prefix already orders it), and every later load here is
// ordered after the acquire. So the ring tails and the global head this reads are never older than the
// decrement — which is what the Dekker pair with a publisher's gate needs (`SchedSpinningLabel`).
//
// ⚠ **THE READS ARE UNLOCKED AND ANSWER A QUESTION, NOT A CLAIM.** A stale answer is either a search that finds
// the work already taken — one extra lap — or a publish too late for this read, which read the count after
// the decrement and woke somebody itself.
function buildSchedAnyWork(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedAnyWorkName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var ringTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awring", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awfound", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var none = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let numProcs = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let globalHasWork = emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module, block: entry, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let firstProc = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let index = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(index, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(globalHasWork, thenBlockId: found.id, elseBlockId: loopHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(firstProc)))

	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: index, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: none.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	let p = emitLoadProc(module, block: loopBody, index: index, ids: ids)
	let status = emitLoadField(module, block: loopBody, record: p, offset: POffStatus, ids: ids)
	let idle = emitCmp(module, block: loopBody, a: status, b: emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: PStatusActive, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(idle, thenBlockId: loopNext.id, elseBlockId: ringTest.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: ringTest, record: p, offset: POffRunqHead, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitLoadField(module, block: ringTest, record: p, offset: POffRunqTail, ids: ids)
	let ringHasWork = emitCmp(module, block: ringTest, a: head, b: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ringTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(ringHasWork, thenBlockId: found.id, elseBlockId: loopNext.id))

	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: loopNext, a: index, b: emitConst(module, block: loopNext, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopNext, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	loopNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextIndex)))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: found, value: SchedWorkFound, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: none, value: SchedNoWorkFound, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedAnyWork'

// What `__sched_any_work` answers. Named because its two callers compare against one of them, and a bare
// literal at each end would be the answer written three times.
let SchedWorkFound = 1 as ParsedInt
let SchedNoWorkFound = 0 as ParsedInt

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_sweep_dropped`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`__sched_sweep_dropped()` — RECLAIM THE DROPPED THREADS SITTING AT THE FRONT OF THIS P's RING AND OF
// THE GLOBAL QUEUE.** Its one caller is `__gt_promise_drop`, which is what puts one there.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS WHAT KEEPS A NEVER-SCHEDULED PROGRAM'S MEMORY BOUNDED, and without it the mark-and-skip
// protocol would be a leak with a spec that still passed.** `__gt_promise_drop` hands back the half a
// dropped thread's promise owns — its managed arguments and its slot in `__gt_live_count` — but its STACK
// and STRUCT can only be freed by a party that is allowed to take it out of a queue, and in a ring that is
// only the popper. A program that spawns and drops in a loop and never awaits anything (which is
// `async-promise-drop.spawn-drop-loop-bounded`, exactly) schedules NOTHING, so nothing would ever pop them.
// Sweeping the FRONT at the drop reclaims them in that shape immediately: the thread just marked is normally
// the newest AND the only one, so the ring is empty again by the time the loop comes round.
//
// ⛔⛔⛔ **THE RING HALF IS GONE (A3), AND THE REASON IS THAT IT WAS THE ONLY PLACE IN THIS RUNTIME THAT
// DEREFERENCED A GREEN THREAD IT HAD NOT CLAIMED.** What stood here was a PEEK-then-CAS: it loaded
// `slot[head]`, followed that pointer to read `GtOffTeardown` and `GtOffWaiter` — deciding whether it was a
// tombstone — and only then CAS'd the head to take it. Every other slot read in this file discards a lost
// race without following what it read (`__sched_runq_get` uses `g` only after winning, `__sched_steal`
// copies pointers without dereferencing them), and `__sched_find_runnable` asks the very same tombstone
// question of a thread it has ALREADY popped.
//
// **THE HAZARD, STATED SO IT IS NOT REDISCOVERED:** between the slot load and the deref a thief can take the
// thread, run it to completion and have it reclaimed — and the next spawn can take the struct off a free list
// and zero it. The struct is never handed back to the slab (`GtFreePutName`), so the deref cannot fault and a
// stale answer still loses the CAS, because the head has moved; but it is a read of another thread's words,
// which no other slot reader here makes and none needs to.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND DELETING IT COSTS NOTHING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A DELETION AND NOT A LOCK-FREE REWRITE.** Three
// facts, each checkable at the code:
//
//   1. **NO PRODUCER CAN PUT A TOMBSTONE IN A RING.** `emitGtIsDropped` is `teardown == consumerTicket and
//      waiter == 0`, and the consumer ticket is added only by `__gt_owner_done` — reached only from an
//      `await` or a `__gt_promise_drop`, both of which need a **Promise**. A `spawn` yields a `<T>.handle`
//      and never a promise (SV1 verified this at the code); an `async` frame is a COROUTINE and is published
//      to its owner's queue, never to a ring; and a reply cell has `owner != cell`, so
//      `__gt_promise_drop` sends it down `sweepCoro`. ⇒ nothing that a ring can hold is a thing a promise
//      can name, so this walk could only ever have found a live thread and stopped.
//   2. **THE SHAPE IT WAS BUILT FOR NO LONGER ROUTES HERE.** Its motivating program was
//      `async-promise-drop.spawn-drop-loop-bounded` — spawn and drop in a loop, schedule nothing — and since
//      `EC10` those spawns are coroutines on the owner's FIFO. The GLOBAL half below, which is
//      `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped`'s body, is what actually bounds that program, and it holds `__sched_lock`
//      across its peek, under which the head cannot move and the thread therefore cannot be popped, run,
//      reclaimed or scavenged. **That half is safe and stays.**
//   3. **A RING TOMBSTONE STILL HAS A REAPER THE DAY ONE CAN EXIST.** `__sched_find_runnable` pops the
//      candidate and THEN asks `emitGtIsDropped` of it (`emitGtTombstoneHandback`), which is the same
//      question asked of a thread the asker owns — the safe spelling, and the one this file should have had
//      only one of. The bound the front-sweep bought was latency on one shape, never correctness.
//
// ⇒ **IF A LATER RUNG GIVES A GREEN THREAD A PROMISE, DO NOT RESTORE THE PEEK.** Claim first — CAS the head,
// then dereference what you own, exactly as `__sched_find_runnable` does — or leave the reclamation to it.
//
// ⚠ This function is therefore the GLOBAL walk alone, and it is still worth its call: it is what reclaims a
// dropped coroutine that reached the global queue, and its caller (`__gt_promise_drop`'s `sweepGreen` arm)
// is what puts one there.
function buildSchedSweepDropped(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedSweepDroppedName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var globalStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swglobalstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The global half — the SAME walk `GtRuntime.__gt_coro_sweep_dropped` is, off the two `.data` ends
	// instead of an owner's two fields. Under the lock the head cannot move, so the peek and the dequeue
	// name one thread and no compare-and-swap is needed; that argument, the front-only bound and the reason
	// the sweep exists at all live once, at `emitGtFifoSweepDropped`.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO LOCK HERE — the walk takes and releases its own, per iteration (SV1).** It has to: it calls
	// `__gt_runner_done`, which must never run under `__sched_lock`. A bracket held by this caller would make
	// the walk's release a recursive decrement that released nothing, which is exactly the latent hang
	// `GtRuntime.GtRunnerDoneName` records.
	emitGtFifoSweepDropped(module, func: func, cur: globalStart, cont: doneBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.globalLabels, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedSweepDropped'

// ==========================================================================
// `__gt_free_put` and `__gt_free_get` — the GT-struct free list
// ==========================================================================

// See `GtFreePutName`.
function buildGtFreePut(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtFreePutName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	emitSchedListPushLocked(module, block: entry, headLabel: SchedGtFreeHeadLabel, linkOffset: GtOffNext, node: gt, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtFreePut'

// See `GtFreeGetName`.
function buildGtFreeGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtFreeGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var recycle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gfgrecycle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gfoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gfmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var counted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gfgcount", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var served = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gfgserved", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var empty = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gfgempty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	let reused = emitSchedListTakeHeadLocked(module, cur: entry, got: recycle, none: empty, headLabel: SchedGtFreeHeadLabel, linkOffset: GtOffNext, ids: ids)

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: recycle, ids: ids)
	let structBytes = emitConst(module, block: recycle, value: GtStructSize, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(recycle, op: StdOp.memFill(reused, byteCountId: structBytes, value: 0))
	let p = emitLoadCurrentPOrBail(module, cur: recycle, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, haveProc: counted, noProc: served, ids: ids)

	let recycledOne = emitConst(module, block: counted, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: counted, record: p, offset: POffGtRecycleCount, delta: recycledOne, multiM: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(counted, op: StdOp.branch(served.id))

	module.setTerminator(served, op: StdOp.ret(reused))

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: empty, ids: ids)
	let freshBytes = emitConst(module, block: empty, value: GtStructSize, ids: ids)
	let allocated = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(empty, op: StdOp.call(allocated, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(freshBytes)))
	module.setTerminator(empty, op: StdOp.ret(allocated))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtFreeGet'

// ==========================================================================
// The per-P counter sums
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE SUM OF ONE PER-P COUNTER OVER `__sched_procs`** — the floor of `__Builtins.schedStealCount()`
// (`POffStealCount`), `schedRetakeCount()` (`POffRetakeCount`), `slabRemoteFreeCount()`
// (`POffRemoteFreeCount`) and `schedGtRecycleCount()` (`POffGtRecycleCount`): the only ways a Maxon program
// can observe that work stealing happened, that a processor was retaken from a machine stuck in the kernel,
// that a box was freed on a machine other than the one that allocated it, and that a spawn reused a GT struct.
//
// ⚠ **IT SUMS RATHER THAN READING A GLOBAL, BECAUSE A `.data` WORD APPEARS IN THE `data {` SECTION OF EVERY
// GREEN-THREAD PROGRAM's GOLDEN.** Counting is per-P and the summing happens here, in a function no program
// that does not ask for it carries (`DeadFunctionElimination`).
//
// ⚠ **THE READ IS NOT ATOMIC AND DOES NOT NEED TO BE**, even for `POffRemoteFreeCount`, whose WRITE is atomic
// to serialise its many producers against each other. Each counter is one aligned word, so every value the
// walk reads is one some writer really stored; a concurrent step costs FRESHNESS, not a torn number, and a
// harness reads it after its work has finished, which is when it is exact.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE ROSTER DRIFT GUARD.** Every name is written down twice — once as a call site in
// `installSchedRuntime`, once as an arm of `isSchedPerProcSumCallee` — and a sum built here and left off that
// predicate gets `usesGt` from nobody, so its body is never installed and the program dies at
// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function`. ⇒ it fails at INSTALL instead, naming the predicate.
function buildSchedPerProcSum(module StdModule, name ByteArray, offset ByteOffset)
	if not isSchedPerProcSumCallee(name) 'offRoster'
		panic("buildSchedPerProcSum: '{String.from(name)}' is built as a per-P sum but is not on `isSchedPerProcSumCallee`'s roster — add it there, which is how it reaches `isSchedSubstrateQueryCallee`, the ONE membership `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` (install the scheduler) and `TargetFacilities.calleeHostFacility` (E3104 off its substrate) both read")
	end 'offRoster'

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"schdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let index = ids.mint()
	let total = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(index, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(total, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	// `__sched_num_procs` is 0 until `__sched_init_procs` has run, which answers 0 for a program that asks
	// before its first `async` — the truthful answer, and the reason the walk needs no separate null test on
	// `__sched_procs`.
	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: index, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	let countedHere = emitLoadField(module, block: loopBody, record: emitLoadProc(module, block: loopBody, index: index, ids: ids), offset: offset, ids: ids)
	let runningTotal = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: total, b: countedHere, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let step = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: index, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	loopBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextIndex, second: runningTotal)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(total))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedPerProcSum'

// `__sched_idle_processor_count() -> i64` — see `SchedIdleProcessorCountName`. `__gt_run_main` brings the
// scheduler up before `main`, so the word it answers has been counted by `__sched_init_procs` before any
// program code can read it.
function buildSchedIdleProcessorCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedIdleProcessorCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNpidleLabel, ids: ids)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedIdleProcessorCount'

// `__sched_park_wake_count() -> i64` — see `SchedParkWakeCountName`. The walk is unlocked, for
// `SchedAllMLabel`'s reason, and each read is one aligned word some machine really stored, for
// `buildSchedPerProcSum`'s.
function buildSchedParkWakeCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedParkWakeCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pwhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pwbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pwdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let firstMachine = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedAllMLabel, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let cursor = ids.mint()
	let total = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(cursor, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(total, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(firstMachine, second: zero)))

	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: cursor, b: emitConst(module, block: loopHdr, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: loopBody.id))

	let countedHere = emitLoadField(module, block: loopBody, record: cursor, offset: MOffParkWakes, ids: ids)
	let runningTotal = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: total, b: countedHere, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let nextMachine = emitLoadField(module, block: loopBody, record: cursor, offset: MOffAllNext, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopBody, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	loopBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextMachine, second: runningTotal)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(total))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedParkWakeCount'

// One more return of `m`'s blocking wait — see `MOffParkWakes`.
function emitSchedCountParkWake(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffParkWakes, delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: OneMore, ids: ids), multiM: false, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedCountParkWake'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_startm`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **BIND A MACHINE TO A PROCESSOR — Go's `startm` (`proc.go:3543`).** See `SchedStartMName` for why it
// takes the processor as its argument. In order:
//
//   1. **Get a processor.** The caller's, if it handed one over; otherwise `pidleget`, which marks it taken
//      and uncounts it in the same critical section that took it off the list. No processor means there is
//      nowhere to run anything, and a reserved credit goes back with `__sched_needspinning` raised (`smnop`).
//   2. **Take a machine off `__sched_midle`.** The processor's `POffOldM` if that machine is idle — Go's
//      `mgetSpecific`, which puts the processor back on the core that last ran its ring and its allocator
//      shard — and otherwise the head, Go's `mget`.
//   3. **Hand over the caller's spinning credit, if it reserved one, and deliver the processor through
//      `MOffNextP`** under the lock, then signal outside it. Or, with no idle machine, CREATE one on that
//      processor — which is how a process goes from one M to several, and the only `osThreadCreate` that
//      creates a machine that RUNS green threads.
//
// ⚠ **A NEW MACHINE TOUCHES NO WORD THIS CALL WRITES, WHICH IS WHY `osThreadCreate` MAY SIT INSIDE THE
// CRITICAL SECTION.** `emitSchedAllocM` states the split: the creator writes the park event, the handle,
// `MOffNextP` and the roster link, and the new thread writes only the syscall stack, `currentGt`, its inline
// GT's owner and its own TLS slot. No word is written by both, so nothing the new thread does can be
// corrupted by this call still running. ⚠ **IT MAY STILL BLOCK ON THIS LOCK A MOMENT LATER** — its first
// `wloop` reaches `__sched_find_runnable`, which can take it — which is harmless, because this call
// releases, and is not what the argument needs. What the argument needs is the
// DISJOINTNESS, and that is true field by field.
function buildSchedStartM(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedStartMName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notStopping = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smlive", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smtakep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var tookProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smtook", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smhavep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smnop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var askOldM = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smoldm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeHead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smmget", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pickMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smpick", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wakeIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var handOver = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smhandover", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var deliver = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smdeliver", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smspawn", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var tooManyMachines = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smmaxm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smspawnstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smspawnok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smspawnfailed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnFailedDrained = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smfaileddrained", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let handedProc = 0 as ValueId
	let credit = 1 as ValueId

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: entry, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SHUTDOWN READ THAT MAKES THE TEARDOWN'S JOIN A GUARANTEE, AND IT MUST BE *THIS* READ —
	// INSIDE THE LOCK — RATHER THAN `__sched_wake_or_spawn`'s.** That caller reads the flag unlocked, as a fast
	// refusal; a publisher that reads 0 there, is descheduled, and resumes after `__sched_stop_workers` has set
	// the flag, signalled every machine and released the lock would otherwise arrive HERE and create a brand-new
	// worker. That machine is prepended to `__sched_allm` — and the teardown reads the roster head for its join
	// walk AFTER releasing the lock, so a machine prepended after that read is **never joined**. The teardown
	// then returns with an OS thread still running, still stepping `__sched_active_workers` and still running
	// releaser decrefs, into `__mm_leak_check`: an intermittent exit 101 on a program with no leak, which is
	// verbatim the failure `__sched_allm` was built to prevent.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THE `smspawnstart` ARM SPECIFICALLY**, which is why it is read before anything
	// is taken off either list: `osThreadCreate` under this lock is what makes *"registered before the teardown
	// can look"* true, and it is only true of a machine the teardown has not already walked past.
	emitSchedBranchOnShutdown(module, cur: entry, stopping: noProc, live: notStopping, ids: ids)

	let entryZero = emitConst(module, block: notStopping, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingHanded = emitCmp(module, block: notStopping, a: handedProc, b: entryZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)

	// `haveProc` is the merge of the two ways to arrive holding a processor — handed one, or taken one — and
	// carries it as its block arg. A handed processor is already `PStatusActive` (`__sched_handoffp` receives
	// it off no list, from `__sched_retake`), so only the taken one needs the claim `pidleget` makes.
	let chosenProc = ids.mint()
	haveProc.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(chosenProc, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(notStopping, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingHanded, thenBlockId: takeProc.id, elseBlockId: haveProc.id))
	notStopping.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(haveProc.id, argIds: argsOf(handedProc)))

	let idleProc = emitSchedPidleGetLocked(module, func: func, cur: takeProc, got: tookProc, none: noProc, wakesMonitor: targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(tookProc, op: StdOp.branch(haveProc.id))
	tookProc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(haveProc.id, argIds: argsOf(idleProc)))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **NOTHING TO PLACE THE WORK WITH — AND A RESERVED CREDIT GOES BACK HERE, UNDER THE LOCK, WITH
	// `__sched_needspinning` RAISED (Go's `wakep` and `pidlegetSpinning`).** Two roads reach this block: every
	// processor is busy, or the scheduler is shutting down. The first is the one that matters. While the
	// reservation stood, a publisher could read the count, skip its own wake, and leave its work in its own
	// ring; the machine that must now find that work is one already holding a processor — possibly one about to
	// release it without having spun, whose park looks only at the global queue. The flag is what makes that
	// machine search again instead (`wpark`).
	//
	// ⛔ **THE GIVE-BACK IS INSIDE THIS CRITICAL SECTION, NOT AFTER IT.** A publisher that read the reserved
	// unit therefore published before this section, so the parking machine that reads the flag in a later
	// section also sees the publish; a publisher that reads the count after the give-back finds it without
	// this unit and wakes for itself. A give-back after the release would leave a window in which a publisher
	// skips its wake after the flag's reader has already looked.
	//
	// Both steps are branch-free on `credit` (1 or 0), so a caller that reserved nothing changes nothing — and
	// raising the flag at shutdown costs nothing, since no machine searches again after it.
	emitSchedGiveBackCredit(module, block: noProc, credit: credit, ids: ids)
	emitSchedRaiseNeedSpinningIfReserved(module, block: noProc, credit: credit, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: noProc, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noProc, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// Go's `mgetSpecific` before `mget`. A processor no machine has released yet names no `oldm`, and one
	// whose last machine is busy — running, or inside a kernel call — is not on the list; both take the head.
	let oldM = emitLoadField(module, block: haveProc, record: chosenProc, offset: POffOldM, ids: ids)
	let noOldM = emitConst(module, block: haveProc, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let neverReleased = emitCmp(module, block: haveProc, a: oldM, b: noOldM, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveProc, op: StdOp.condBranch(neverReleased, thenBlockId: takeHead.id, elseBlockId: askOldM.id))

	let chosenMachine = ids.mint()
	pickMachine.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(chosenMachine, argType: StdType.i64))
	let oldMIdle = emitSchedMidleHolds(module, block: askOldM, m: oldM, ids: ids)
	let notHeld = emitConst(module, block: askOldM, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let oldMBusy = emitCmp(module, block: askOldM, a: oldMIdle, b: notHeld, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(askOldM, op: StdOp.condBranch(oldMBusy, thenBlockId: takeHead.id, elseBlockId: pickMachine.id))
	askOldM.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(pickMachine.id, argIds: argsOf(oldM)))

	let idleHead = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: takeHead, label: SchedMidleLabel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(takeHead, op: StdOp.branch(pickMachine.id))
	takeHead.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(pickMachine.id, argIds: argsOf(idleHead)))

	let pickZero = emitConst(module, block: pickMachine, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noMachine = emitCmp(module, block: pickMachine, a: chosenMachine, b: pickZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pickMachine, op: StdOp.condBranch(noMachine, thenBlockId: spawnIt.id, elseBlockId: wakeIt.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE PROCESSOR IS DELIVERED *INSIDE* THE CRITICAL SECTION AND THE SIGNAL IS SENT OUTSIDE IT, AND
	// THAT SPLIT IS A CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A COST ONE.** `MOffNextP` is written under the same
	// lock that took this machine off `__sched_midle`, which makes *"taken off"* and *"given a processor"* one
	// transaction. Without that, two `__sched_startm` calls could each take a machine, and a machine woken by a
	// signal that handed it nothing could re-join the idle list before the first call's store landed — the second
	// call's store would then overwrite the first's, and the processor the first took would be stranded: off
	// `__sched_pidle`, marked active, named by nobody, for the life of the process.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SIGNAL IS OUTSIDE BECAUSE THE MACHINE IT WAKES WANTS THIS LOCK IMMEDIATELY** — `wafterwait`'s
	// first act on the far side of the wait is to take it — so signalling while holding it would hand the woken
	// machine a lock it must then wait for. Nothing is lost by the gap: the event is AUTO-reset, so a signal
	// that arrives before the machine reaches its `osWaitHandle` is not lost, and one that arrives after it
	// has already collected `MOffNextP` on another wake is a harmless spurious one.
	var unlinked = emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked(module, func: func, cur: wakeIt, m: chosenMachine, ids: ids)
	let unreserved = emitConst(module, block: unlinked, value: SchedStartMCredit.unreserved.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	let nothingToHand = emitCmp(module, block: unlinked, a: credit, b: unreserved, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unlinked, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingToHand, thenBlockId: deliver.id, elseBlockId: handOver.id))

	_ = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: handOver, m: chosenMachine, transition: SpinningTransition.handOverSpinning, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(handOver, op: StdOp.branch(deliver.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: deliver, record: chosenMachine, offset: MOffNextP, value: chosenProc)
	let parkEvent = emitLoadField(module, block: deliver, record: chosenMachine, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: deliver, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(deliver, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(parkEvent))
	module.setTerminator(deliver, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// ⭐⭐ **NO IDLE MACHINE ⇒ CREATE ONE — AFTER THE THREAD LIMIT, WHICH IS GO'S `checkmcount`.** Every machine
	// ever created is still on the roster, so `__sched_mcount` is the number that exists; creating the one past
	// `SchedMaxMachines` aborts rather than trying, for that constant's reason.
	let machines = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: spawnIt, label: SchedMcountLabel, ids: ids)
	let machineLimit = emitConst(module, block: spawnIt, value: SchedMaxMachines, ids: ids)
	let atLimit = emitCmp(module, block: spawnIt, a: machines, b: machineLimit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnIt, op: StdOp.condBranch(atLimit, thenBlockId: tooManyMachines.id, elseBlockId: spawnStart.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: tooManyMachines, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerThreadExhaustion, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE LOCK IS HELD ACROSS `osThreadCreate` DELIBERATELY.** `__sched_stop_workers` sets the shutdown
	// flag inside this same critical section and then walks `__sched_allm`; holding it here is what makes
	// *"no machine is created after the flag is set"* a fact rather than a window, and what makes every machine
	// on the roster already carry the handle that will join it. The new thread touches none of the words
	// written here (see `emitSchedAllocM`), so it is free to run the moment `osThreadCreate` returns.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND A RESERVED CREDIT IS GIVEN BACK RATHER THAN HANDED OVER** — `credit` is 1 or 0, so the step is
	// `-credit`. `MOffSpinning` belongs to a struct whose spinning state is meaningful only once the thread
	// exists, and a credit stamped on a machine that has not started is a unit no CAS can reclaim if
	// `osThreadCreate` then fails. The new machine claims its own at `__sched_find_runnable`'s spinner cap, if
	// the cap lets it search.
	//
	// ⇒ **The count is therefore 0 rather than 1 for the length of one thread creation**, which can only let a
	// concurrent publisher issue an EXTRA wakeup — and that wakeup finds `__sched_pidle` empty (this call took
	// the processor it wanted) and gives its own credit straight back. **The error is in the direction that
	// cannot lose work**: a suppressed wakeup strands a green thread with no machine coming for it, an extra one
	// costs two list operations. `SchedSpinningLabel` carries what the suppressed direction costs.
	emitSchedGiveBackCredit(module, block: spawnStart, credit: credit, ids: ids)
	let newMachine = emitSchedAllocM(module, block: spawnStart, nextP: chosenProc, ids: ids)
	let entryPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnStart, op: StdOp.funcAddr(entryPtr, funcName: SchedWorkerLoopName))
	let threadHandle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnStart, op: StdOp.osThreadCreate(threadHandle, entryPtrId: entryPtr, argId: newMachine))
	let spawnZero = emitConst(module, block: spawnStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let threadFailed = emitCmp(module, block: spawnStart, a: threadHandle, b: spawnZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnStart, op: StdOp.condBranch(threadFailed, thenBlockId: spawnFailed.id, elseBlockId: spawnOk.id))

	// ⭐ **THE HANDLE IS STORED AND THE ROSTER IS JOINED IN THAT ORDER, AND ONLY ON SUCCESS.** The roster is
	// what the teardown joins by, so a machine reachable through it must already carry a joinable handle —
	// and a machine that never started must not be counted at all, or `__sched_checkdead` would count a struct
	// nobody is running as a running machine and never fire again.
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: newMachine, offset: MOffOsThreadHandle, value: threadHandle)
	emitSchedRegisterMLocked(module, block: spawnOk, m: newMachine, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: spawnOk, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnOk, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// ⭐ **A `CreateThread` THAT FAILED MUST GIVE THE PROCESSOR BACK**, because it is marked active and is on no
	// list: without this it is a processor no machine will ever adopt and no `pidleget` can ever find — the box
	// quietly losing a core for the life of the process, with nothing hanging (the machines already running
	// still service the queues) and therefore nothing to notice. The M struct and its event are left behind
	// unreferenced, which is the same non-freeing every other M gets; the credit is already back, so the
	// processor is all there is to return. ⚠ A processor `handoffp` handed over may still hold the work it was
	// handed over for, so its ring goes to the global queue first. A reserved wake has started nobody, so
	// `__sched_needspinning` goes up exactly as at `smnop`.
	emitSchedDrainRingLocked(module, func: func, cur: spawnFailed, p: chosenProc, cont: spawnFailedDrained, hdrLabel: b"smfaileddrainhdr", bodyLabel: b"smfaileddrainbody", ids: ids)
	var spawnFailedIdled = emitSchedPidlePutLocked(module, func: func, cur: spawnFailedDrained, p: chosenProc, holdsWorkLabel: b"smfailedheldwork", emptyLabel: b"smfailedidle", ids: ids)
	emitSchedRaiseNeedSpinningIfReserved(module, block: spawnFailedIdled, credit: credit, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: spawnFailedIdled, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnFailedIdled, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedStartM'

// Step `__sched_nmspinning` down by `credit` (1 or 0): a reserved credit going back.
function emitSchedGiveBackCredit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, credit ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let delta = emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), b: credit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: block, label: SchedSpinningLabel, delta: delta, multiM: true, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedGiveBackCredit'

// Raise `__sched_needspinning` if `credit` (1 or 0) was reserved — see `SchedNeedSpinningLabel`. `__sched_lock`
// held.
function emitSchedRaiseNeedSpinningIfReserved(module StdModule, block IrBlock, credit ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let neededBefore = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedNeedSpinningLabel, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedNeedSpinningLabel, value: emitBin(module, block: block, a: neededBefore, b: credit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids), ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedRaiseNeedSpinningIfReserved'

// Call `__sched_startm` on `p` — 0 for *"take one off `__sched_pidle`"*.
function emitSchedStartM(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, credit SchedStartMCredit, ids ValueMinter)
	let creditArg = emitConst(module, block: block, value: credit.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedStartMName, args: twoArgs(p, second: creditArg)))
end 'emitSchedStartM'

// ⭐⭐ **TAKE `m` OFF `__sched_midle` IF IT IS STILL ON IT — the removal a SIGNALLED park needs, and Go's
// `stopm` does not.** Go wakes an idle machine only after `mget` has taken it off the list, so *"woken"* and
// *"removed"* are one event. Here a machine is also woken by signals that hand it nothing — the main machine's
// (`emitSchedSignalMainMachine`) and the teardown's — so a machine can wake on a road no `mget` took it off.
//
// ⇒ **the machine takes itself off on EVERY wake, in the same critical section that reads `MOffNextP`**, so
// the pair (on the list / holding a delivered processor) is read as one transaction. A machine that was
// taken off finds itself absent and does nothing; one that was only signalled unlinks itself and re-joins
// before its next wait, which keeps *"on `__sched_midle`" == "blocked on `MOffParkEvent`"* exact.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE WINDOW THIS OPENS LETS THE MACHINE COUNT DRIFT ABOVE THE PROCESSOR COUNT, AND IT IS BOUNDED.**
// Between `wdowait` returning and `wjoin` re-joining, a machine is off `__sched_midle` AND holds no
// processor, while the processor it released at `wpark` is still on `__sched_pidle`. A publish landing in
// that window reaches `__sched_startm`, finds a processor and no idle machine, and creates one — never
// reclaimed, because an M is never freed. It self-limits (k extra machines need k parking machines inside
// their windows at once), `multicore-stress/pin-matrix.sh` reads the high-water mark off
// `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()`, and `SchedMaxMachines` is the ceiling. ⚠ **Anything added between
// `wdowait` and `wjoin` widens the window and owes that reading again.**
function emitSchedMidleRemoveLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var stillIdle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mrmonlist", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let onList = emitSchedMidleHolds(module, block: cur, m: m, ids: ids)
	let absent = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let takenOff = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: onList, b: absent, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(takenOff, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: stillIdle.id))

	var unlinked = emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked(module, func: func, cur: stillIdle, m: m, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unlinked, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedMidleRemoveLocked'

// ==========================================================================
// The scheduler loop — `__sched_worker_loop` and `__sched_main_loop`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHICH MACHINE RUNS THE LOOP — the only thing the two loops differ in.** One body (`buildSchedLoop`)
// serves both, so the search, the strand runner, the park and the deadlock check are the same code on every
// machine, the main one included (Go's `mstart` → `schedule` on M0).
enum SchedLoopRole
	// `__sched_worker_loop(m)`: a `CreateThread` callback, born holding the processor `__sched_startm` gave it,
	// that leaves when `__sched_stop_workers` sets the shutdown flag.
	worker

	// `__sched_main_loop(until)`: the main OS thread, which is already an M when it enters, holding a
	// processor or not, and leaves when `until` is met.
	mainMachine
end 'SchedLoopRole'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE MAIN MACHINE RUNS THE SCHEDULER UNTIL**, and the word `__sched_main_loop`'s parameter carries
// for it. EXPORTED for `GtRuntime`, whose `__gt_run_main` and `__gt_drain_at_exit` are the two callers.
export enum SchedMainLoopUntil
	// `main`'s green thread has completed: `__sched_phase` has left `SchedPhase.mainRunning`.
	mainCompleted = 0

	// The exit drain is over: no promise is outstanding (`__gt_live_count` is 0), or nothing that owns one can
	// ever run again (`SchedPhase.stuck`).
	drained = 1
end 'SchedMainLoopUntil'

// Run the scheduler on the main machine until `until` is met. It returns holding a processor or not; the
// next call's entry takes either.
export function emitSchedMainLoop(module StdModule, block IrBlock, until SchedMainLoopUntil, ids ValueMinter)
	let word = emitConst(module, block: block, value: until.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedMainLoopName, args: argsOf(word)))
end 'emitSchedMainLoop'

// ⭐⭐ **WAKE THE MAIN MACHINE WHEREVER IT WAITS.** Its loop decides on `__sched_phase` and `__gt_live_count`,
// neither of which is work a search would find, so whoever moves them past the main machine's exit test
// signals it directly: `main`'s completion, the exit drain's last debit, and a stuck drain. The park event is
// AUTO-reset, so a signal that lands while the main machine is running leaves the event set and its next wait
// returns at once. EXPORTED for `GtRuntime.__gt_owner_done`; a phase move signals through
// `emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked`.
//
// ⚠ **THE POLLER IS BROKEN TOO, UNCONDITIONALLY.** The main machine may be the one blocked in `__np_poll`,
// where its event cannot reach it, and asking first would race its claim: the claim reads the count before it
// stores `__sched_lastpoll`, and a signaller would move the count before reading it — the two reads can pass
// each other. A break to a poller that is not the main machine, or to none, costs one early return.
export function emitSchedSignalMainMachine(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let mainMachine = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedMainMLabel, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: mainMachine, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollBreakName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
end 'emitSchedSignalMainMachine'

// ⭐⭐ **MOVE THE PROCESS TO `phase` AND WAKE THE MAIN MACHINE, WHOSE LOOP DECIDES BY IT.** `__sched_lock` held,
// which is what orders the move against every locked read of the word. EXPORTED for `GtRuntime.__gt_runner_done`,
// which moves it when `main` completes; `__sched_checkdead` is the other mover.
export function emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, phase SchedPhase, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedPhaseLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: phase.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitSchedSignalMainMachine(module, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked'

// 1 when `main` has completed — the phase has left `SchedPhase.mainRunning` — else 0, read unlocked
// with acquire. EXPORTED for `GtRuntime.__gt_owner_done`, whose last debit signals the main machine
// only once there is a drain to signal.
export function emitSchedMainHasCompleted(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let running = emitSchedPhaseIs(module, block: block, phase: emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedPhaseLabel, ids: ids), want: SchedPhase.mainRunning, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: running, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedMainHasCompleted'

// 1 when the loaded phase word `phase` is `want`, else 0.
function emitSchedPhaseIs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, phase ValueId, want SchedPhase, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: phase, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: want.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedPhaseIs'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ENDS A LOOP**, with the one value the main machine's test needs.
union SchedLoopLeave
	// A worker: `__sched_shutdown_flag` is set.
	shutdown

	// The main machine: the `SchedMainLoopUntil` word `until` carries is met.
	untilMet(until ValueId)
end 'SchedLoopLeave'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_checkdead()` — Go's `checkdead`, called at `mput` under `__sched_lock`.** A machine that has
// just joined `__sched_midle` asks whether any machine is still running. If none is, nothing can ever make a
// green thread runnable again unless something outside every machine will: a pending timer or a parked child
// (the last machine to go idle with either pending blocks in the poller rather than joining this list, and
// counts as running while it does), or an in-flight read (the IOCP completion thread readies its reader). With
// none of those either, the answer depends on the phase:
//
//   • `SchedPhase.mainRunning` — `main` can never finish: `RuntimeAbort.schedulerDeadlock`.
//   • `SchedPhase.draining` — the exit drain can never finish: the phase becomes `SchedPhase.stuck` and the main
//     machine is signalled, so the drain ends and the leak gate reports what is still owned.
//   • `SchedPhase.stuck` — already decided.
//
// ⭐⭐ **"RUNNING" IS `mcount − nmidle`, AND EVERY MACHINE OFF THE IDLE LIST COUNTS.** A machine in a kernel
// call, the one blocked in the poller, one a waker has just taken off the list, one being created, one
// waiting to regain a processor after a syscall, and the main machine between its two loops will each look for
// work again before it can park, and its own `mput` asks this again. Every machine that can publish is one of those
// or the IOCP completion thread, which the in-flight count covers.
//
// ⛔ **THE ANSWER IS TAKEN UNDER THE LOCK EVERY PARK'S FINAL LOOK IS TAKEN UNDER.** A machine joins the idle
// list only after finding the global queue empty in the same hold, and a processor is idled only with an empty
// ring (`emitSchedPidlePutLocked`), so with every machine idle every queue is empty.
function buildSchedCheckdead(module StdModule, usesCompletionPort bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedCheckdeadName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nobodyRunning = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cdnobody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nothingPending = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cdnothing", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notMainRunning = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cdnotmain", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var deadlocked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cddeadlock", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stuck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cdstuck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cddone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let machines = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedMcountLabel, ids: ids)
	let idleMachines = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNmidleLabel, ids: ids)
	let running = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: machines, b: idleMachines, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let someoneRunning = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: running, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(someoneRunning, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: nobodyRunning.id))

	var somethingPending = emitGtStoresPending(module, block: nobodyRunning, ids: ids)

	if usesCompletionPort 'aReadInFlightIsPending'
		let readInFlight = emitCmp(module, block: nobodyRunning, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: nobodyRunning, label: IoPendingLabel, ids: ids), b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
		somethingPending = emitBin(module, block: nobodyRunning, a: somethingPending, b: readInFlight, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	end 'aReadInFlightIsPending'

	module.setTerminator(nobodyRunning, op: StdOp.condBranch(somethingPending, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: nothingPending.id))

	let phase = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: nothingPending, label: SchedPhaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let mainRunning = emitSchedPhaseIs(module, block: nothingPending, phase: phase, want: SchedPhase.mainRunning, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nothingPending, op: StdOp.condBranch(mainRunning, thenBlockId: deadlocked.id, elseBlockId: notMainRunning.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: deadlocked, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerDeadlock, ids: ids)

	let draining = emitSchedPhaseIs(module, block: notMainRunning, phase: phase, want: SchedPhase.draining, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notMainRunning, op: StdOp.condBranch(draining, thenBlockId: stuck.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))

	emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked(module, block: stuck, phase: SchedPhase.stuck, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(stuck, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedCheckdead'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **A MACHINE's SCHEDULER LOOP — Go's `schedule()`.** Fire what is due, search, run the strand whose
// token it found on the machine's own scheduler context (`GtRuntime.__gt_run_strand`), and — with nothing to
// run — poll the stores, release the processor and park, asking `__sched_checkdead` as it joins the idle
// list. See `SchedLoopRole` for the two machines that run it.
//
// It does not "dequeue" anything — it asks `__sched_find_runnable`, which consults this P's ring, the global
// queue and every other P's ring in Go's order. There is no `runnext` slot, and `POffRunnext` carries the
// reason.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A `__`-PREFIXED RUNTIME FUNCTION, so `skipsGreenThreadStackGuard` keeps the prologue stack guard
// off it — which is correct and load-bearing: it runs on a real OS-thread stack with megabytes to spare, and
// in a worker a guard would consult a `currentGt` that does not exist until `emitSchedBecomeM` has run.**
function buildSchedLoop(module StdModule, role SchedLoopRole, target Target)
	// A worker answers the DWORD its `CreateThread` callback owes, which nobody reads.
	var func = match role 'funcOf'
		worker gives IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedWorkerLoopName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
		mainMachine gives IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedMainLoopName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	end 'funcOf'

	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopTop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wloop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var checkTimers = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wchecktimers", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireTimers = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wfiretimers", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dequeue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wdequeue", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveWork = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wrun", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var foundWork = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wfound", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var idlePoll = module.addBlock(func, label: b"widlepoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var idleFire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"widlefire", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var park = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doRelease = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wrelease", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var abandonPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wabandon", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkDrain = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wparkdrain", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unspunPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wunspun", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinRecheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wspinrecheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wparkcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkRegain = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wregain", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkResume = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wresume", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkStopTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wstoptest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var idleTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wtail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claimPoller = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pollDeadline = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpolldeadline", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pollWait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var polled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpolled", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var polledStores = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpolledstores", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var breakCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wbreakcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var breakPoller = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wbreak", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wjoin", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doWait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wdowait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var afterWait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wafterwait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var taken = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wtaken", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var adoptGiven = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wadopt", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var exitBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wexit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)

	let leave = match role 'leaveOf'
		worker gives SchedLoopLeave.shutdown
		mainMachine gives SchedLoopLeave.untilMet(0 as ValueId)
	end 'leaveOf'

	let m = match role 'machineOf'
		worker gives 0 as ValueId
		mainMachine gives emitLoadCurrentM(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	end 'machineOf'

	match role 'enterAs'
		worker then emitSchedWorkerBirth(module, func: func, entry: entry, m: m, cont: loopTop, target: target, ids: ids)
		mainMachine then emitSchedMainMachineEntry(module, func: func, entry: entry, m: m, holdingProc: loopTop, noProc: parkCheck, ids: ids)
	end 'enterAs'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE LOOP'S INVARIANT, AND EVERY BLOCK BELOW IS ON ONE SIDE OF IT OR THE OTHER: AT `wloop` THIS
	// MACHINE HOLDS A PROCESSOR.** `__sched_find_runnable` reads `M->currentP` and dereferences it three ops in,
	// before any test, so a P-less machine reaching it would fault. The blocks between `wpark` and `wadopt` are
	// the ones that hold none, and every road out of them adopts one before branching here.
	emitSchedLoopExitBranch(module, func: func, cur: loopTop, leave: leave, exitBlock: exitBlock, cont: checkTimers, holdsLock: false, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐ **FIRE WHAT IS DUE BEFORE EVERY SEARCH — Go's `checkTimers` at the head of `findRunnable`.** A machine
	// that never runs dry would otherwise never fire a deadline, and a sleeper would wake only when some machine
	// went idle. The due test reads the published earliest deadline without the lock
	// (`GtRuntime.emitGtBranchOnTimerDue`), so a schedule takes the lock only when a timer really is due. The
	// fire's wake debt is not paid here: this machine searches next and finds what it readied, and `wfound`
	// hands the looking on if more than that remains.
	let owed = ids.mint()
	dequeue.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(owed, argType: StdType.i64))
	let noDebt = emitConst(module, block: checkTimers, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)
	let checkedAt = emitGtBranchOnTimerDue(module, func: func, cur: checkTimers, due: fireTimers, notDue: dequeue, notDueArgs: argsOf(noDebt), ids: ids)

	let timerDebt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fireTimers, op: StdOp.call(timerDebt, callee: GtTimerCheckName, args: argsOf(checkedAt)))
	module.setTerminator(fireTimers, op: StdOp.branch(dequeue.id))
	fireTimers.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(dequeue.id, argIds: argsOf(timerDebt)))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SEARCH STARTS UNCREDITED, AS GO'S `findRunnable` DOES.** A machine back from running a strand
	// consults its own ring and the global queue as an ordinary machine; it becomes a SPINNER — one unit of
	// `__sched_nmspinning` — only when `__sched_find_runnable`'s spinner cap admits it to the steal rounds, or
	// when a waker handed it a credit before signalling it. `wfound` and `wpark` are the search's two exits and
	// both release whatever credit the machine holds; `SchedSpinningLabel` carries what a leaked one costs.
	let g = emitSchedFindRunnable(module, block: dequeue, m: m, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitCmp(module, block: dequeue, a: g, b: emitConst(module, block: dequeue, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dequeue, op: StdOp.condBranch(empty, thenBlockId: idlePoll.id, elseBlockId: foundWork.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **FOUND A TOKEN ⇒ A SPINNER HAS STOPPED LOOKING, AND IT OWES THE LOOKING TO SOMEBODY ELSE.** Go's
	// `resetspinning` — decrement, then `wakep` — for a machine that WAS spinning, and for one whose own fire
	// readied work and skipped the wake that work owed: either way a publish may be waiting that nobody was
	// woken for. `emitSchedHandOnTheLooking` carries the re-check.
	let wasSpinning = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: foundWork, m: m, transition: SpinningTransition.stopSpinning, ids: ids)
	let heldNoCredit = emitCasLost(module, block: foundWork, result: wasSpinning, ids: ids)
	let owesNothing = emitCmp(module, block: foundWork, a: owed, b: emitConst(module, block: foundWork, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let nothingSkipped = emitBin(module, block: foundWork, a: heldNoCredit, b: owesNothing, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	emitSchedHandOnTheLooking(module, func: func, cur: foundWork, nothingSkipped: nothingSkipped, cont: haveWork, checkLabel: b"wrecheck", wakeLabel: b"wwakemore", pollerLabel: b"wwakepoller", ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(haveWork, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRunStrandName, args: twoArgs(m, second: g)))
	module.setTerminator(haveWork, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **NOTHING TO RUN ⇒ FIRE WHAT IS DUE AND REAP WHAT HAS EXITED BEFORE GIVING THE PROCESSOR UP.** A
	// green thread this machine has just parked on a deadline that has already passed — every filesystem
	// operation's I/O yield parks on `now` — is woken here, by the machine that parked it, rather than by the
	// poller, and so is a child that exited. Unlocked peeks decide whether to take the lock and look
	// (`GtRuntime.emitGtPollStores`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE WAKE DEBT GOES TO `wdequeue` RATHER THAN BEING PAID.** The thread fired onto this ring is run by
	// this machine, which searches next; `wfound` hands the looking on if more remains.
	emitGtPollStores(module, func: func, cur: idlePoll, cont: idleFire, ids: ids)

	let idleDebt = ids.mint()
	idleFire.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(idleDebt, argType: StdType.i64))
	let firedAny = emitSchedAnyRunnable(module, block: idleFire, p: emitLoadField(module, block: idleFire, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let firedNone = emitCmp(module, block: idleFire, a: firedAny, b: emitConst(module, block: idleFire, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(idleFire, op: StdOp.condBranch(firedNone, thenBlockId: park.id, elseBlockId: dequeue.id))
	idleFire.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(dequeue.id, argIds: argsOf(idleDebt)))

	// ==================================================================================================
	// ⭐⭐⭐ THE PARK — ONE CRITICAL SECTION
	// ==================================================================================================
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE MISSED-WAKEUP ARGUMENT IS AN INTERLEAVING ARGUMENT OVER TWO CRITICAL SECTIONS.** Giving the
	// processor up, taking the FINAL look at every queue, and joining `__sched_midle` all happen inside one hold
	// of `__sched_lock`; `wakep`'s whole body — `pidleget`, `mget`, the delivery of the processor — happens
	// inside another. Two parties serialised by a mutex interleave in exactly two ways, and this machine is safe
	// in both:
	//
	//   • **THE PUBLISHER GOES FIRST.** Its `pidleget` runs before this processor has been released, so it
	//     either takes some OTHER idle processor (and that machine finds the work) or finds none and returns.
	//     Either way its publish is complete and visible before this machine's own critical section begins —
	//     so the final look below SEES it, and this machine takes a processor back rather than parking.
	//   • **THIS MACHINE GOES FIRST.** It leaves with its processor on `__sched_pidle` and itself on
	//     `__sched_midle`. The publisher's `pidleget` then finds that processor and its `mget` finds this
	//     machine, and it is signalled.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ONE WINDOW LEFT IS BETWEEN THE RELEASE AND THE `osWaitHandle` ITSELF, AND THE AUTO-RESET EVENT
	// IS WHAT COVERS IT** — a signal that lands there leaves the event signalled and the wait returns at once.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE SPINNING CREDIT COMES BACK BEFORE THE LOCK IS TAKEN, AND A MACHINE THAT HELD ONE LOOKS AGAIN
	// EVERYWHERE AFTER IT (`wspinrecheck`).** The decrement must precede that look, with a StoreLoad barrier
	// between; the other order loses work submitted between the search and the decrement. `SchedSpinningLabel`
	// carries the full dance.
	let wasSpinningAtPark = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: park, m: m, transition: SpinningTransition.stopSpinning, ids: ids)
	let hadNoCredit = emitCasLost(module, block: park, result: wasSpinningAtPark, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: park, ids: ids)
	let releasing = emitLoadField(module, block: park, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(park, op: StdOp.condBranch(hadNoCredit, thenBlockId: unspunPark.id, elseBlockId: spinRecheck.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **A MACHINE THAT WAS NOT SPINNING ABANDONS THE PARK WHEN `__sched_needspinning` IS RAISED** — Go's
	// `findRunnable` before `releasep`. Somebody counted work that no spinner will find and started nobody to
	// search for it; this machine still holds a processor, so it becomes the spinner. The read is inside the lock
	// every raise is made under, and a raiser that comes after this section finds the processor this machine
	// releases idle, so it has nothing to raise.
	let needed = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: unspunPark, label: SchedNeedSpinningLabel, ids: ids)
	let isNeeded = emitCmp(module, block: unspunPark, a: needed, b: emitConst(module, block: unspunPark, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unspunPark, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNeeded, thenBlockId: abandonPark.id, elseBlockId: parkDrain.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **A MACHINE THAT WAS SPINNING RE-CHECKS EVERY RUN QUEUE BEFORE IT PARKS — Go's `checkRunqsNoP` after
	// the `nmspinning` decrement (`proc.go:3638-3715`).** While this machine was counted in
	// `__sched_nmspinning`, every publisher that read the count skipped its wake on the strength of *"somebody
	// is already looking"* — including one that published to ANOTHER machine's ring, where the work waits until
	// that machine's strand gives it up. So having stopped looking, this machine asks `__sched_any_work` — the
	// global queue and every active processor's ring, its own included — and if anything is there it searches
	// again as a spinner.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ASKS BEFORE RELEASING, WHERE GO RELEASES FIRST AND THEN TAKES AN IDLE PROCESSOR BACK.** Go drops
	// `sched.lock` in between, so its processor may be gone by the time it looks; here the whole park is one
	// critical section, so a release followed by a `pidleget` would always hand back the processor just
	// released, and keeping it is the same outcome without the round trip.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ORDERING IS A DEKKER PAIR AND BOTH HALVES HAVE A BARRIER.** This side decrements the count with a
	// locked read-modify-write and only then reads the queues; a publisher writes its ring tail with a locked
	// read-modify-write and only then reads the count (`buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s gate). Each first read after
	// the store is an ACQUIRE, which on arm64 orders it after the store-release exclusive before it, as x86's
	// `lock` prefix does. So one of the two always sees the other: the publisher reads 0 and wakes somebody, or
	// this re-check sees its work.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY MACHINE THAT WAS SPINNING RE-CHECKS, NOT ONLY THE LAST ONE TO STOP** — Go's issue 43997: the count
	// can reach a spinner's decrement through a road that re-checks nothing. A machine that was NOT spinning
	// suppressed nobody's wake.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RE-SEARCH CANNOT SPIN IN PLACE OF A SLEEP.** It runs once per spinning episode rather than per park,
	// and a steal visits every active processor in each of its rounds and takes at least one token from a ring
	// that holds any — so it finds the work unless another machine took it first, and then the next re-check
	// finds that ring empty.
	let workAnywhere = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spinRecheck, op: StdOp.call(workAnywhere, callee: SchedAnyWorkName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let nowhere = emitCmp(module, block: spinRecheck, a: workAnywhere, b: emitConst(module, block: spinRecheck, value: SchedNoWorkFound, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spinRecheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(nowhere, thenBlockId: parkDrain.id, elseBlockId: abandonPark.id))

	// The two findings above that send this machine searching again: it keeps its processor and its ring.
	_ = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: abandonPark, m: m, transition: SpinningTransition.becomeSpinning, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: abandonPark, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(abandonPark, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE RING GOES TO THE GLOBAL QUEUE BEFORE THE PROCESSOR IS IDLED.** `__sched_find_runnable` answered
	// 0 a moment ago, but a publish can land in this ring between that answer and this lock, and a processor on
	// `__sched_pidle` must hold nothing (`emitSchedPidlePutLocked`'s assertion). The final look then sees the
	// drained work and this machine takes a processor straight back. No wake is owed: it looks again itself.
	emitSchedDrainRingLocked(module, func: func, cur: parkDrain, p: releasing, cont: doRelease, hdrLabel: b"wdrainhdr", bodyLabel: b"wdrainbody", ids: ids)

	var released = emitSchedReleaseProcLocked(module, func: func, cur: doRelease, m: m, p: releasing, holdsWorkLabel: b"wparkheldwork", emptyLabel: b"wparkidled", ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(released, op: StdOp.branch(parkCheck.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE FINAL LOOK: THE GLOBAL QUEUE.** Every road here arrives holding the lock and holding no
	// processor, which is this block's whole precondition: a machine that has just released its processor
	// (`wrelease`), one whose park ended without a processor handed over (`wtaken`), the poller back from its
	// wait (`wpolledstores`), and the main machine entering its loop without one.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT CANNOT BE `__sched_find_runnable`**: that function needs a ring, an rng and a tick, and
	// dereferences `M->currentP` to get them three ops in, before any test. A machine between releasing its
	// processor and joining the idle list would fault.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE GLOBAL QUEUE IS WHAT A PUBLISH OWING THIS MACHINE NO WAKE CAN STILL LEAVE BEHIND**: a yielding
	// strand's token, the ring's overflow, this machine's own drained ring, and every publish by a party with no
	// processor at all (the IOCP completion loop, and the poller's own fire below). A publish to a ring
	// owes its own wake, and `wspinrecheck` is where one skipped for a spinner is caught.
	// `__sched_find_runnable`'s global-batch arm takes what this read sees, so a positive answer always makes
	// progress.
	let anythingLeft = emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module, block: parkCheck, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let nothingLeft = emitCmp(module, block: parkCheck, a: anythingLeft, b: emitConst(module, block: parkCheck, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parkCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingLeft, thenBlockId: parkStopTest.id, elseBlockId: parkRegain.id))

	// There IS work. Take a processor back and go round again — Go's `checkRunqsNoP` arm. ⚠ **A failure here
	// is not a contradiction and must not park us in a special way**: it means every processor is now held,
	// which means every one of them has a machine on its way to `__sched_find_runnable`, so the work has an
	// owner and this machine may sleep. The road out is `wstoptest`, the same one an empty scheduler takes.
	let regained = emitSchedPidleGetLocked(module, func: func, cur: parkRegain, got: parkResume, none: parkStopTest, wakesMonitor: targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target), ids: ids)
	emitSchedResumeFromPark(module, block: parkResume, m: m, p: regained, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parkResume, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))

	// ⭐ **THE EXIT TEST IS INSIDE THE CRITICAL SECTION, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES AN UNTIMED WAIT SAFE.** Whoever
	// moves what the test reads — `__sched_stop_workers`'s flag, `__gt_runner_done`'s and `__sched_checkdead`'s
	// phase, `__gt_owner_done`'s last debit — either does so before this read or signals this machine and breaks
	// the poller (`emitSchedSignalMainMachine`), so whichever wait follows ends however early that landed: the
	// flag and the phase are written under this lock, and the last debit's unlocked read of the phase is half of
	// a Dekker pair with this read of the count (`GtRuntime.__gt_owner_done`).
	emitSchedLoopExitBranch(module, func: func, cur: parkStopTest, leave: leave, exitBlock: exitBlock, cont: idleTail, holdsLock: true, ids: ids)

	// ==================================================================================================
	// ⭐⭐⭐ THE IDLE TAIL — Go's `findRunnable` tail (`proc.go:3748-3811`)
	// ==================================================================================================
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ONE MACHINE WAITS FOR THE EARLIEST DEADLINE; EVERY OTHER IDLE MACHINE WAITS FOR A WAKER.** With a
	// timer or a child pending and nobody polling, this machine claims the poller (`__sched_lastpoll` → 0),
	// publishes the deadline it will wake at and blocks in `__np_poll` holding no processor — it is not on
	// `__sched_midle`, so `__sched_checkdead` counts it running, which it is. Otherwise it parks with no timeout:
	// a deadline armed earlier than the poller's breaks the poller (`__sched_wake_netpoller`), and the poller is
	// the one machine the clock needs.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CLAIM IS MADE IN THE SAME HOLD AS THE FINAL LOOK AND THE EXIT TEST**, so a timer added after it is
	// seen by the adder's `__sched_wake_netpoller` (which reads the claim), and one added before it is in the
	// deadline read here.
	let storesPending = emitGtStoresPending(module, block: idleTail, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(idleTail, op: StdOp.condBranch(storesPending, thenBlockId: claimPoller.id, elseBlockId: parkJoin.id))

	let lastPoll = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: claimPoller, label: SchedLastPollLabel, ids: ids)
	let somebodyPolls = emitCmp(module, block: claimPoller, a: lastPoll, b: emitConst(module, block: claimPoller, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(claimPoller, op: StdOp.condBranch(somebodyPolls, thenBlockId: breakCheck.id, elseBlockId: pollDeadline.id))

	// This machine is the poller. The deadline arrives as `wpolldeadline`'s own block argument once it is known.
	let deadline = ids.mint()
	var publishDeadline = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpollpublish", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	publishDeadline.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(deadline, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: pollDeadline, label: SchedLastPollLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: pollDeadline, value: 0, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitSchedPollDeadline(module, func: func, cur: pollDeadline, cont: publishDeadline, ids: ids)

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: publishDeadline, label: SchedPollUntilLabel, value: deadline, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: publishDeadline, ids: ids)
	let pollStart = emitGtNowNs(module, block: publishDeadline, ids: ids)
	let alreadyDue = emitCmp(module, block: publishDeadline, a: deadline, b: pollStart, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publishDeadline, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyDue, thenBlockId: polled.id, elseBlockId: pollWait.id))

	// ⭐ **THE ONE BLOCKING WAIT OF AN IDLE PROCESS**, rounded UP to the millisecond so it cannot end short of the
	// deadline and cost a second wait for the remainder, and capped at `PollerMaxWaitMs`.
	let untilDeadlineMs = emitCeilMs(module, block: pollWait, ids: ids, earliest: deadline, now: pollStart)
	let timeoutMs = emitSchedMinWord(module, block: pollWait, a: untilDeadlineMs, b: emitConst(module, block: pollWait, value: PollerMaxWaitMs, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(pollWait, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollPollName, args: argsOf(timeoutMs)))
	emitSchedCountParkWake(module, block: pollWait, m: m, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pollWait, op: StdOp.branch(polled.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE POLLER GIVES THE ROLE BACK, FIRES WHAT IS DUE AND REAPS WHAT EXITED, THEN LOOKS AGAIN.** What it
	// readies goes to the global queue (it holds no processor), and its wake debt is not paid: `wparkcheck` sees
	// that queue next and takes a processor to run it, so a program with nothing else running does not start a
	// machine for work this one is about to do. ⚠ **THIS IS THE PRODUCER FOR `emitSlabCurrentP`'s P-LESS-M
	// ARM**: anything the two walks allocate is allocated with `M->currentP` at 0, so
	// `SlabRuntime.emitSlabShardRow` routes it to the raw row under the slab's own lock. The walks take
	// `__sched_lock` again, recursively.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: polled, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: polled, label: SchedLastPollLabel, value: emitGtNowNs(module, block: polled, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: polled, label: SchedPollUntilLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: polled, value: 0, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitGtPollStores(module, func: func, cur: polled, cont: polledStores, ids: ids)
	polledStores.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(ids.mint(), argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(polledStores, op: StdOp.branch(parkCheck.id))

	// Somebody else is polling. Its deadline is the earliest there was when it claimed the poller, and every
	// earlier one armed since broke its wait; this re-check is Go's, and costs a break only in the window between
	// an arm and that break.
	let otherDeadline = ids.mint()
	var compareDeadline = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wbreakcompare", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	compareDeadline.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(otherDeadline, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitSchedPollDeadline(module, func: func, cur: breakCheck, cont: compareDeadline, ids: ids)

	let pollUntil = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: compareDeadline, label: SchedPollUntilLabel, ids: ids)
	let pollerLate = emitSchedPollerLate(module, block: compareDeadline, pollUntil: pollUntil, when: otherDeadline, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(compareDeadline, op: StdOp.condBranch(pollerLate, thenBlockId: breakPoller.id, elseBlockId: parkJoin.id))

	module.appendOp(breakPoller, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollBreakName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(breakPoller, op: StdOp.branch(parkJoin.id))

	// Go's `stopm`: `mput`, then Go's `checkdead`. This is the instant the machine becomes findable as idle, and
	// it is the LAST thing done under the lock — everything a waker needs (a processor on `__sched_pidle`, this
	// machine on `__sched_midle`) is in place together or not at all, and the deadlock check sees this machine
	// counted idle.
	var joined = emitSchedMidlePutLocked(module, func: func, cur: parkJoin, m: m, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(joined, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedCheckdeadName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: joined, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(joined, op: StdOp.branch(doWait.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE BLOCKS ON ITS OWN EVENT, NOT ON A PROCESSOR'S, AND FOR AS LONG AS IT TAKES.** It holds no
	// processor here: `M->currentP` reads 0 for the whole of this wait, which is the state `handoffp` has to be
	// able to produce.
	let parkEvent = emitLoadField(module, block: doWait, record: m, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)
	let waitResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doWait, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(waitResult, handleId: parkEvent, timeoutMsId: emitConst(module, block: doWait, value: WaitInfinite, ids: ids)))
	emitSchedCountParkWake(module, block: doWait, m: m, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doWait, op: StdOp.branch(afterWait.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **LEAVING THE IDLE LIST AND COLLECTING THE DELIVERED PROCESSOR ARE ONE TRANSACTION.** A machine
	// wakes for two reasons and cannot tell them apart from the wait's own answer: a `__sched_startm` popped it
	// off `__sched_midle` and stored a processor on `MOffNextP`, or a signal that hands over nothing (the main
	// machine's, `emitSchedSignalMainMachine`, or the teardown's) ended it — and in the second it is still on the
	// list. Reading the two facts under one hold of the lock is what makes the pair consistent.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: afterWait, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ Both halves are read HERE, in the block that dominates every exit the removal walk has, rather than
	// carried as a block arg out of it: the walk's exits are its own blocks and it answers nothing, so a value
	// defined in `wafterwait` is the only one all of them can see.
	let givenProc = emitLoadField(module, block: afterWait, record: m, offset: MOffNextP, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterWait, record: m, offset: MOffNextP, value: emitConst(module, block: afterWait, value: 0, ids: ids))
	emitSchedMidleRemoveLocked(module, func: func, cur: afterWait, m: m, cont: taken, ids: ids)

	// Handed nothing: back round the final look, holding the lock and no processor — its precondition.
	let nothingGiven = emitCmp(module, block: taken, a: givenProc, b: emitConst(module, block: taken, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(taken, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingGiven, thenBlockId: parkCheck.id, elseBlockId: adoptGiven.id))

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: adoptGiven, ids: ids)
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: adoptGiven, m: m, p: givenProc)
	module.setTerminator(adoptGiven, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))

	match role 'leaveAs'
		worker then emitSchedWorkerExit(module, func: func, exitBlock: exitBlock, m: m, ids: ids)
		mainMachine then emitSchedMainMachineExit(module, func: func, exitBlock: exitBlock, m: m, ids: ids)
	end 'leaveAs'

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedLoop'

// ⭐⭐ **THE MAIN MACHINE ENTERS HOLDING A PROCESSOR OR NOT.** Bring-up gives it P[0], and a loop it left from
// the park road left it none; without one it enters at the final look (`noProc`), holding the lock that block
// requires, and so either takes a processor back for queued work or parks like any idle machine.
function emitSchedMainMachineEntry(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, m ValueId, holdingProc IrBlock, noProc IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var enterIdle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wentryidle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let p = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	let holdsNone = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: p, b: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(holdsNone, thenBlockId: enterIdle.id, elseBlockId: holdingProc.id))

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: enterIdle, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(enterIdle, op: StdOp.branch(noProc.id))
end 'emitSchedMainMachineEntry'

// ⭐⭐ **HAND THE LOOKING ON IF A PUBLISH MAY HAVE SKIPPED ITS WAKE.** Unless `nothingSkipped`, ask
// `__sched_any_work` — every source a publisher may have skipped its wake for — and wake an idle M if it
// answers yes, or `__sched_wake_poller` if it answers no; then continue at `cont`. It terminates `cur`.
//
// ⭐ **IT ASKS FIRST WHERE GO'S `resetspinning` WAKES UNCONDITIONALLY.** `wakep` on an empty scheduler is a
// `SetEvent` plus a woken M's full search plus a `WaitForSingleObject`, paid to discover that there was
// nothing, and a publish that lands after the read sees the count already down and wakes for itself.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKS EVERY ACTIVE PROCESSOR'S RING, NOT ONLY THIS ONE'S.** A publish to another machine's ring that
// skipped its wake because this machine was spinning is stranded if nobody else looks: that ring's own machine
// reaches its search only when its strand gives the machine up, and a strand that runs without parking holds
// the work there for as long as it runs.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND WITH NO WORK LEFT IT STILL OWES A DEADLINE ITS POLLER.** The poller leaves its role to run what it
// fired, and resumes as a spinner — so a timer still pending with `__sched_lastpoll` non-zero is a deadline no
// parked machine will wake for, and a parked one is woken to claim it.
function emitSchedHandOnTheLooking(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, nothingSkipped ValueId, cont IrBlock, checkLabel ByteArray, wakeLabel ByteArray, pollerLabel ByteArray, ids ValueMinter)
	var check = module.addBlock(func, label: checkLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wake = module.addBlock(func, label: wakeLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var poller = module.addBlock(func, label: pollerLabel, heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingSkipped, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: check.id))

	let workRemains = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(check, op: StdOp.call(workRemains, callee: SchedAnyWorkName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let workLeft = emitCmp(module, block: check, a: workRemains, b: emitConst(module, block: check, value: SchedNoWorkFound, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(check, op: StdOp.condBranch(workLeft, thenBlockId: wake.id, elseBlockId: poller.id))

	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: wake, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wake, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	module.appendOp(poller, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedWakePollerName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(poller, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedHandOnTheLooking'

// 1 when a timer or a child is pending and no machine is blocked in the poller, else 0 — read unlocked, and
// every read picks an arm (`MultiMSharing.underSchedLock`): a stale "somebody polls" means that poller has just
// given the role back and looks again itself, and a stale "nothing pending" misses only a deadline whose adder
// calls `__sched_wake_netpoller` itself.
function emitSchedPollerOwed(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let timerPending = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module, block: block, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: TimerWhenEmpty, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let children = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(children, callee: GtProcCountName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let childPending = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: children, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let pending = emitBin(module, block: block, a: timerPending, b: childPending, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let nobodyPolls = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedLastPollLabel, ids: ids), b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: pending, b: nobodyPolls, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedPollerOwed'

// ⭐⭐ **THE MAIN MACHINE LEAVES ITS LOOP HOLDING NO SPINNING CREDIT.** It may leave from `wloop` holding one a
// park it abandoned or a deadline it fired gave it; kept, the count never comes down and every later publish
// reads it as *"somebody is already looking"*. So the credit comes back as `wfound` gives it back — with the
// re-check a spinner owes. It keeps its processor if it holds one, which the next loop's entry takes.
function emitSchedMainMachineExit(module StdModule, func IrFunction, exitBlock IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	var left = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wexitdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let wasSpinning = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: exitBlock, m: m, transition: SpinningTransition.stopSpinning, ids: ids)
	let heldNoCredit = emitCasLost(module, block: exitBlock, result: wasSpinning, ids: ids)
	emitSchedHandOnTheLooking(module, func: func, cur: exitBlock, nothingSkipped: heldNoCredit, cont: left, checkLabel: b"wexitrecheck", wakeLabel: b"wexitwake", pollerLabel: b"wexitpoller", ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(left, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitSchedMainMachineExit'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **A WORKER's BIRTH: BECOME THE M IT WAS HANDED, THEN COLLECT ITS PROCESSOR.** The processor is
// collected the same way on every road into `wloop` — here, and after every park — through `MOffNextP`: one
// field, one door, and no arm that adopts a processor it got some other way.
function emitSchedWorkerBirth(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, m ValueId, cont IrBlock, target Target, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedBecomeM(module, block: entry, m: m, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **THIS THREAD's SIGNAL STACK, BEFORE IT RUNS A GREEN THREAD.** `sigaltstack` is per thread, and a fault
	// handler registered `SA_ONSTACK` on a thread with none builds its frame on whatever green thread's few
	// kilobytes faulted. The main thread's is `mrt_runtime_init`'s, which every program runs.
	if gtFaultFrameHome(target) == FaultFrameHome.signalStack 'signalStack'
		module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SignalStackInstallName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	end 'signalStack'

	let bornProc = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: m, offset: MOffNextP, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: m, offset: MOffNextP, value: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids))
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: entry, m: m, p: bornProc)

	// ⭐ The live-worker count and its high-water mark. The count is an ATOMIC add: two Ms can enter and
	// leave concurrently, and a lost update here would make the count drift for the process's life. The MARK
	// is a plain load/compare/store, and that is deliberate — it is monotone, so a lost sample only ever
	// loses a transient reading while the mark still converges to the true peak and can never UNDER-report
	// two live workers, because this M's own increment is already visible in the reload. `multiM: true` for
	// the reason every counter in this file passes it; the door answers the PRIOR value, which is what
	// `nowActive` re-applies the delta to.
	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let priorActive = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: entry, label: SchedActiveWorkersLabel, delta: one, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	let nowActive = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: priorActive, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitSchedRaiseHighWater(module, func: func, cur: entry, live: nowActive, cont: cont, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedWorkerBirth'

// ⭐⭐ **LEAVE THE LOOP, OR CONTINUE AT `cont`** — `wloop`'s test, the park's under the lock, and the one after
// a park ended empty-handed. It terminates `cur`; a `holdsLock` caller's exit road releases the lock first.
function emitSchedLoopExitBranch(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, leave SchedLoopLeave, exitBlock IrBlock, cont IrBlock, holdsLock bool, ids ValueMinter)
	let stopping = emitSchedLoopExitTest(module, block: cur, leave: leave, ids: ids)

	if holdsLock 'releaseOnTheWayOut'
		var parkExit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wparkexit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: parkExit, ids: ids)
		module.setTerminator(parkExit, op: StdOp.branch(exitBlock.id))
		module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(stopping, thenBlockId: parkExit.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))
	end 'releaseOnTheWayOut' else 'noLockHeld'
		module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(stopping, thenBlockId: exitBlock.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))
	end 'noLockHeld'
end 'emitSchedLoopExitBranch'

// 1 when the loop is to be left, else 0. See `SchedShutdownFlagLabel` for why a worker reads the flag at more
// than one site, and `SchedPhase` for the word the main machine's test reads.
//
// ⚠ **THE PHASE IS READ WITH ACQUIRE**, because `__gt_run_main` reads `main`'s status and result once the
// loop is left: `main`'s completion is published before `__gt_runner_done` moves the phase, and an acquire is
// what orders the status read after this one on arm64 when the read is not under the lock.
function emitSchedLoopExitTest(module StdModule, block IrBlock, leave SchedLoopLeave, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return match leave 'testFor'
		shutdown gives emitSchedShuttingDown(module, block: block, ids: ids)
		untilMet(until) gives emitSchedMainLoopUntilMet(module, block: block, until: until, ids: ids)
	end 'testFor'
end 'emitSchedLoopExitTest'

// Branch-free: `(drain and (live == 0 or stuck)) or (not drain and phase != running)`, each operand a `cmp`
// result, so the bitwise operators are the logical ones.
function emitSchedMainLoopUntilMet(module StdModule, block IrBlock, until ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let phase = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedPhaseLabel, ids: ids)

	let mainDone = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitSchedPhaseIs(module, block: block, phase: phase, want: SchedPhase.mainRunning, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let nothingOwned = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtLiveCountLabel, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let isStuck = emitSchedPhaseIs(module, block: block, phase: phase, want: SchedPhase.stuck, ids: ids)
	let drainOver = emitBin(module, block: block, a: nothingOwned, b: isStuck, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)

	let draining = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: until, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: SchedMainLoopUntil.drained.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let awaitingMain = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: until, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: SchedMainLoopUntil.mainCompleted.rawValue as ParsedInt, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let drainMet = emitBin(module, block: block, a: draining, b: drainOver, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let mainMet = emitBin(module, block: block, a: awaitingMain, b: mainDone, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: drainMet, b: mainMet, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedMainLoopUntilMet'

// Leave a park holding processor `p`, which a `pidleget` in the same critical section has just handed this
// machine: release `__sched_lock`, adopt, and search as a spinner — the queue it saw may hold more than it
// takes, and no publisher's wake is owed for it (`wpolled`), so `wfound` hands the looking on.
function emitSchedResumeFromPark(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: block, m: m, p: p)
	_ = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: block, m: m, transition: SpinningTransition.becomeSpinning, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedResumeFromPark'

// ⭐ **THE DEADLINE THE POLLER WAITS FOR: THE EARLIEST TIMER'S, CAPPED AT `ChildPollNs` FROM NOW WHILE A CHILD IS
// PARKED.** `__sched_lock` held, so both reads are exact. It terminates `cur` and reaches `cont` with the deadline
// as `cont`'s one block argument; with neither pending that is `TimerWhenEmpty`, and no caller asks then.
function emitSchedPollDeadline(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var capped = module.addBlock(func, label: b"polldeadlinechild", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"polldeadlinecap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let earliest = emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	let children = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.call(children, callee: GtProcCountName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let noChild = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: children, b: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(noChild, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: capped.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cont.id, argIds: argsOf(earliest)))

	let cap = emitSchedChildPollDeadline(module, block: capped, ids: ids)
	let capSooner = emitCmp(module, block: capped, a: cap, b: earliest, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(capped, op: StdOp.condBranch(capSooner, thenBlockId: takeCap.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))
	capped.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cont.id, argIds: argsOf(earliest)))

	module.setTerminator(takeCap, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	takeCap.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(cont.id, argIds: argsOf(cap)))
end 'emitSchedPollDeadline'

// `ChildPollNs` from now: the latest the poller may wake while a child is parked. EXPORTED for
// `GtRuntime.__gt_register_park`, which owes the poller that deadline when it parks a child.
export function emitSchedChildPollDeadline(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitGtNowNs(module, block: block, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: ChildPollNs, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedChildPollDeadline'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **A WORKER's EXIT PUTS EVERY WORD THIS MACHINE PUBLISHED BACK DOWN, AND RELEASES THE PROCESSOR ONLY IF
// IT STILL HOLDS ONE.** Its roads in differ in exactly that: `wloop`'s shutdown arm arrives holding a
// processor, the park's two arrive holding none.
//
// ⚠ **THE SPINNING CREDIT COMES BACK, THOUGH NO ROAD HERE IS SPINNING BY ITS OWN DOING**, because a waker can
// have handed this machine a credit in between: `__sched_startm` reads the shutdown flag under the lock, and
// `wloop`'s arm read it without one. Left behind, that unit never comes down — every later publish reads the
// count as *"somebody is already looking"* and wakes nobody. The CAS makes the common case a no-op step of
// zero.
// The prior value of the worker count is discarded — nothing on the teardown road reads how many workers
// there were — which is exactly the shape `emitGlobalAccumulate`'s contract note says lets the isel pick a
// bare `lock add` with no destination register.
function emitSchedWorkerExit(module StdModule, func IrFunction, exitBlock IrBlock, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	var exitRelease = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wexitrelease", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var exitDrained = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wexitdrained", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var exitDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wexitdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let negOne = emitConst(module, block: exitBlock, value: -1, ids: ids)
	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: exitBlock, label: SchedActiveWorkersLabel, delta: negOne, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	_ = emitSchedSpinningTransition(module, block: exitBlock, m: m, transition: SpinningTransition.stopSpinning, ids: ids)
	let exitProc = emitLoadField(module, block: exitBlock, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	let exitZero = emitConst(module, block: exitBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let holdsNothing = emitCmp(module, block: exitBlock, a: exitProc, b: exitZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(exitBlock, op: StdOp.condBranch(holdsNothing, thenBlockId: exitDone.id, elseBlockId: exitRelease.id))

	// ⚠ **IT GOES BACK THROUGH THE SAME DOOR EVERY OTHER RELEASE USES, LOCK AND ALL.** Putting it on
	// `__sched_pidle` at teardown offers it to nobody — `__sched_startm` reads the shutdown flag under this
	// same lock and places nothing once it is set — so the push is inert here; taking the door anyway is what
	// keeps `POffStatus` down to the two writers its own note claims. The lock is free to take: every road into
	// this block released it first, and `__sched_stop_workers` joins these threads without holding it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RING IS DRAINED FIRST, BECAUSE A PROCESSOR CAN REACH HERE HOLDING WORK NOBODY OWES.** The exit
	// drain ends once no PROMISE is outstanding, and a strand whose remaining members are renounced coroutines
	// is owed by no promise — yet its token can still land in this ring after the flag is set: a machine that
	// fires a renounced sleeper at `wchecktimers` meets the flag at `wloop` with the token in its own ring.
	// Nothing runs it after shutdown wherever it goes, and `pidleput`'s assertion is what the drain satisfies.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: exitRelease, ids: ids)
	emitSchedDrainRingLocked(module, func: func, cur: exitRelease, p: exitProc, cont: exitDrained, hdrLabel: b"wexitdrainhdr", bodyLabel: b"wexitdrainbody", ids: ids)
	var exitReleased = emitSchedReleaseProcLocked(module, func: func, cur: exitDrained, m: m, p: exitProc, holdsWorkLabel: b"wexitheldwork", emptyLabel: b"wexitidled", ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: exitReleased, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(exitReleased, op: StdOp.branch(exitDone.id))

	// A `CreateThread` callback returns a DWORD nobody reads; 0 is the conventional success. The M struct and
	// its 64 KB region are NOT freed — an M outlives its thread's exit exactly as a P outlives everything,
	// and the roster still names it.
	emitConstReturn(module, block: exitDone, value: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedWorkerExit'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE FOUR THINGS THAT CAN HAPPEN TO A SPINNING CREDIT (MC1) — and they are an enum rather than
// four bodies because all four are the SAME two instructions with different constants**: one CAS on
// `m.spinning`, then one atomic step of `__sched_nmspinning` by however much that CAS decided. See
// `SchedSpinningLabel` for what the count is for and `MOffSpinning` for why the per-machine word exists —
// and note that it IS per-machine since W213-C2, where this enum's text used to call it a fact about the P.
enum SpinningTransition
	// A worker M that `__sched_find_runnable`'s spinner cap admits to the steal rounds (`frspinclaim`). Wins
	// the word 0 -> 1 and counts itself in. A LOST CAS is not a failure: it means a waker already handed this M
	// a credit before signalling it, and the M simply inherits it — which is the whole point of the hand-over,
	// since the reservation must span the entire `SetEvent` -> wake latency to suppress anything.
	becomeSpinning

	// A worker M leaving the search — it found a thread, it is about to block on its event, or it is exiting.
	// Wins 1 -> 0 and counts itself out; a lost CAS means it held no credit, and the step is zero. The answer
	// says which, and `wfound` reads it: only a machine that was spinning owes the search to somebody else.
	//
	// ⭐ **NO HAND-OVER CAN LAND ON A MACHINE BETWEEN ITS RELEASE AND ITS NEXT SEARCH.** A hand-over requires
	// taking the machine off `__sched_midle` under `__sched_lock`, and a machine on the park road is not on that
	// list until `wjoin`, which is after the last block that could have inherited one. A credit handed to a
	// parked machine that then finds the shutdown flag is reclaimed at `wexit`.
	//
	// ⛔ **EVERY EXIT FROM THE SEARCH MUST REACH THIS, INCLUDING THE ONE AT SHUTDOWN.** A credit left behind is
	// not merely untidy: the count never comes back down, so every later publish reads it as *"somebody is
	// already looking"* and stops waking anybody.
	stopSpinning

	// A WAKER, on the MACHINE it has just taken exclusive possession of by taking it off `__sched_midle` under
	// `__sched_lock`. It reserved a credit at the gate; this hands that credit to the machine that is about to
	// look. A LOST CAS means that M had already counted itself in — the reservation is then surplus and is
	// given straight back, which is the direction that cannot suppress a wakeup.
	handOverSpinning
end 'SpinningTransition'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DOOR A CREDIT CHANGES HANDS THROUGH: CAS `m.spinning`, THEN STEP THE COUNT BY WHAT THE CAS
// DECIDED.** Branch-free on purpose — the CAS answers 1 or 0 (`AtomicCasSucceeded`/`AtomicCasFailed`, the
// contract's own spelling), so each arm's step is that answer put through one subtraction rather than a
// block per outcome. Four sites' worth of block plumbing saved for two instructions, and a fifth site added
// later cannot get the pairing wrong because it does not spell it. It answers the CAS — `AtomicCasSucceeded`
// when this transition moved the word.
function emitSchedSpinningTransition(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, transition SpinningTransition, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	// One arm RELEASES the word (1 -> 0); the other two CLAIM it (0 -> 1). The direction is asked once here
	// so the two constants cannot disagree about which value means "held".
	let releasing = transition == SpinningTransition.stopSpinning
	let spinningAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffSpinning, ids: ids)
	let expected = emitConst(module, block: block, value: MSpinningHeld if releasing else MSpinningFree, ids: ids)
	let replacement = emitConst(module, block: block, value: MSpinningFree if releasing else MSpinningHeld, ids: ids)
	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: spinningAddr, expectedId: expected, replacementId: replacement))

	// An M that wins counts itself in (+1) or out (-1). A waker that wins has handed its reservation on and
	// owes nothing (0); one that LOSES found the M already counted, so its reservation is surplus (-1).
	let delta = match transition 'deltaFor'
		becomeSpinning gives won
		stopSpinning gives emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), b: won, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
		handOverSpinning gives emitBin(module, block: block, a: won, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	end 'deltaFor'

	// ⭐ **THE STEP GOES THROUGH `MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate`, which is ALREADY the door for exactly this
	// and is what the file header's audit item 2 names.** One `lock xadd`, whose LOCK prefix is also the
	// StoreLoad barrier the dance at `SchedSpinningLabel` requires between a decrement and the re-check that
	// follows it. `multiM: true` is not a choice: the word is laid out only under `usesGt`, and a
	// green-thread program is precisely one where a second M can exist. The PRIOR value is discarded — Go
	// throws on a negative result here (`"wakep: negative nmspinning"`), and what stands in for that assert
	// is that these three arms and the waker's give-back are the count's only writers, each stepping by what
	// its own CAS decided.
	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: block, label: SchedSpinningLabel, delta: delta, multiM: true, ids: ids)

	// A new spinner answers for whatever `__sched_needspinning` was raised over: it re-checks every queue before
	// it stops (Go's `becomeSpinning`).
	if transition == SpinningTransition.becomeSpinning 'answersTheNeed'
		emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedNeedSpinningLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	end 'answersTheNeed'

	return won
end 'emitSchedSpinningTransition'

// Raise `__sched_max_active_workers` to `live` if `live` is higher, then continue at `cont`. See the entry
// comment in `buildSchedLoop` for why this is deliberately not atomic.
function emitSchedRaiseHighWater(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, live ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var raise = module.addBlock(func, label: b"whiwater", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let mark = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel, ids: ids)
	let higher = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: live, b: mark, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(higher, thenBlockId: raise.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: raise, label: SchedMaxActiveWorkersLabel, value: live, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(raise, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedRaiseHighWater'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_wake_or_spawn`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_wake_or_spawn()` — GO'S `wakep` (`proc.go:3228`), AND W213-C2 TURNED IT THE RIGHT WAY
// ROUND.** Called once at the tail of every GREEN-THREAD publish, with the run-queue lock ALREADY RELEASED:
// the GT is published, and this decides whether anybody needs waking for it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO SCAN FOR A MACHINE AND THEN LOOK FOR A PROCESSOR; IT NOW TAKES A PROCESSOR AND BINDS A
// MACHINE TO IT.** The old body walked `P[1..numProcs)` for a `POffIdleFlag` of 1 and signalled that P's
// event, then walked again for a P no M had claimed and started one there. Both walks found a parked machine
// *through the processor it was still holding* — which is precisely the arrangement this rung ends. What is
// left here is the GATE and nothing else; `__sched_startm` is the rest, and it is a separate function
// because `handoffp` will call it with a processor of its own.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE GATE IS UNCHANGED FROM MC1 AND IS WHERE THE MEASURED COST GOES.** Go's `wakep` opens with
// exactly this pair: a plain load as the fast path, then a `CompareAndSwap(0, 1)` that RESERVES the one
// spinning M this wakeup is allowed to create. Everything below is skipped outright when either answers no,
// and in a program whose work is finer-grained than a park/unpark round trip that is almost every publish.
// `SchedSpinningLabel` carries the reading that justified it: **172 ms of CPU at one processor against
// 3,109 ms at sixteen**, before the gate existed.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE FENCE THIS READ NEEDS IS THE ONE THE OLD `idleFlag` SCAN NEEDED, AND BOTH ARMS STILL CARRY IT.**
// The queue publish must be visible before the count is read, or a publisher can skip a wake on the strength
// of a spinner whose search preceded its publish. The GLOBAL TAIL's fence is the caller's
// `LeaveCriticalSection`; the LOCAL RING's is `emitRunqPublishTail`'s `lock xadd`, whose LOCK prefix is the
// StoreLoad barrier — that function states it at the instruction and forbids the "optimisation" back to a
// plain store, and this is the reader it protects. ⚠ A caller that already HELD the lock and pushed to the
// ring inside it has neither: the `lock xadd` is what covers it, which is why `__gt_timer_check` and
// `__gt_proc_check` may publish inside their walk and wake once after it.
//
// ⭐ **AND `__sched_startm` TAKES `__sched_lock`, WHICH THIS FUNCTION DELIBERATELY DID NOT — MC1 MEASURED
// THAT TRADE AND ITS PREMISE IS WHAT THIS RUNG REMOVED.** MC1 kept a lock-free linear scan against Go's
// `pidleget`/`pidleput` on the ground that *"this scan runs with the run-queue lock deliberately RELEASED,
// because the release IS the StoreLoad fence the `idleFlag` protocol depends on"*, and priced the list at
// *"0.2% of the cost"* (**108,322 `idleFlag` loads for the whole of `service-torture` at 480,000 messages and
// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=16`**, against 953 ms of CPU). There is no `idleFlag` protocol any more — the park is one
// critical section (`buildSchedLoop`) — so there is no fence to preserve by staying outside a lock, and
// the O(1) list is now the cheaper option as well as the only one that can find a P-less machine. The gate
// above still runs lock-free, so a publish that wakes nobody costs one load exactly as it did.
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SYSTEM MONITOR'S ONE-SHOT START (W213-C3), AND THIS IS THE ONE SITE** — see
// `SchedSysmonStartedLabel` for why it is here and not at `__sched_init_procs` or at `__sched_startm`'s spawn
// arm. Reaching `cur` means a GREEN THREAD has been published, which is the whole precondition for a second
// machine ever to exist and therefore for a processor ever to be worth retaking.
//
// ⚠ It is a function of its own rather than three blocks inline because its CALLER is now conditional (gate
// site 2 of `targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls`), and a caller that branches around half of a block sequence is
// how a lane comes to emit a terminator twice. It terminates `cur` and lands on `cont` from every arm.
function emitSchedSysmonLazyStart(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var monitorClaim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sysmonclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var monitorStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sysmonstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let sysmonStarted = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedSysmonStartedLabel, ids: ids)
	let sysmonUnstarted = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let mayNeedMonitor = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: sysmonStarted, b: sysmonUnstarted, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(mayNeedMonitor, thenBlockId: monitorClaim.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))

	// The CAS is what makes the start happen once; the plain load above is what keeps its `lock` prefix off the
	// publish path after that. A machine that loses this CAS simply carries on — another publisher started it.
	let monitorAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(monitorClaim, op: StdOp.globalAddr(monitorAddr, label: SchedSysmonStartedLabel))
	let monitorExpected = emitConst(module, block: monitorClaim, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let monitorReplacement = emitConst(module, block: monitorClaim, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let wonMonitor = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(monitorClaim, op: StdOp.atomicCas(wonMonitor, addrId: monitorAddr, expectedId: monitorExpected, replacementId: monitorReplacement))
	let lostMonitor = emitCasLost(module, block: monitorClaim, result: wonMonitor, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(monitorClaim, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostMonitor, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: monitorStart.id))

	// ⚠ **A `CreateThread` THAT FAILS IS NOT AN ERROR HERE AND THE HANDLE IS DISCARDED.** Without a monitor a
	// blocking kernel call costs its processor for the duration, which is exactly the behaviour every build
	// before this rung had; refusing to run at all because a diagnostic thread could not be created would turn
	// a degradation into a failure. Nothing ever joins it, so nothing needs the handle either.
	let monitorEntry = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(monitorStart, op: StdOp.funcAddr(monitorEntry, funcName: SchedSysmonName))
	let monitorArg = emitConst(module, block: monitorStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(monitorStart, op: StdOp.osThreadCreate(ids.mint(), entryPtrId: monitorEntry, argId: monitorArg))
	module.setTerminator(monitorStart, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedSysmonLazyStart'

function buildSchedWakeOrSpawn(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedWakeOrSpawnName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spincheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spintest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spinClaim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spinclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var startIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wakestart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// A shutdown in progress must start nothing and wake nobody. ⚠ This read is the FAST refusal and not the
	// authoritative one: `__sched_startm` reads the flag again inside `__sched_lock`, which is the same
	// critical section `__sched_stop_workers` sets it in, and that is what makes *"no machine is created
	// after the flag is set"* a fact rather than a window this early read leaves open.
	emitSchedBranchOnShutdown(module, cur: entry, stopping: doneBlock, live: spinCheck, ids: ids)

	// ⛔ **GATE SITE 2 OF 4 — THE MONITOR'S LAZY START. See `targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls`.** On a lane
	// with no bracket the monitor is not built at all (gate site 1), so there is nothing to start and no
	// `.data` word to CAS (gate site 3); `spincheck` then falls straight through to the spinning gate.
	if targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target) 'theMonitorCanRetake'
		emitSchedSysmonLazyStart(module, func: func, cur: spinCheck, cont: spinTest, ids: ids)
	end 'theMonitorCanRetake' else 'noMonitorOnThisLane'
		module.setTerminator(spinCheck, op: StdOp.branch(spinTest.id))
	end 'noMonitorOnThisLane'

	// ⭐ **THE RESERVATION IS A LOAN, NOT A COUNT OF THIS THREAD.** `__sched_startm` settles it: the machine it
	// wakes takes it over through `MOffSpinning`, and every other road gives it back inside that call.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE READ IS AN ACQUIRE, BECAUSE IT IS THE PUBLISHER'S HALF OF THE DEKKER PAIR.** The publish before
	// it ends in a locked read-modify-write — the ring tail (`emitRunqPublishTail`) or the release of
	// `__sched_lock` — and on arm64 a plain load may be satisfied before that store-release exclusive is
	// visible, while an acquire may not. A stale count read EARLY is a wake skipped for a spinner that has
	// already re-checked: work stranded. x86's `lock` prefix orders the plain load already, and the acquire
	// costs it nothing.
	let spinCountAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spinTest, op: StdOp.globalAddr(spinCountAddr, label: SchedSpinningLabel))
	let spinning = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spinTest, op: StdOp.loadAcquire(spinning, addrId: spinCountAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let spinZero = emitConst(module, block: spinTest, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let alreadyLooking = emitCmp(module, block: spinTest, a: spinning, b: spinZero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spinTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyLooking, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: spinClaim.id))

	let claimExpected = emitConst(module, block: spinClaim, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let claimReplacement = emitConst(module, block: spinClaim, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let reserved = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spinClaim, op: StdOp.atomicCas(reserved, addrId: spinCountAddr, expectedId: claimExpected, replacementId: claimReplacement))
	let lostReservation = emitCasLost(module, block: spinClaim, result: reserved, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spinClaim, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostReservation, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: startIt.id))

	// ⭐ **PASSING NO PROCESSOR IS "PICK ONE", AND THE CREDIT IS THE ONE RESERVED ABOVE.** A publish has no
	// processor in mind; `handoffp` has exactly one and no credit.
	emitSchedStartM(module, block: startIt, p: emitConst(module, block: startIt, value: 0, ids: ids), credit: SchedStartMCredit.reserved, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(startIt, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedWakeOrSpawn'

// `__sched_wake_netpoller(when)` — see `SchedWakeNetpollerName`.
//
// ⭐ **BOTH READS ARE UNLOCKED AND EACH PICKS AN ARM THAT IS RIGHT WHATEVER IT ANSWERS.** The caller armed `when`
// under `__sched_lock`, so a claim of the poller made after that hold reads `when` in its own deadline, and one
// made before it is visible here. A stale "somebody polls" breaks a wait that has already ended, which costs the
// next poll one early return, and the machine that ended it looks again itself; a stale "nobody polls" wakes a
// parked machine that finds the poller taken and parks again.
function buildSchedWakeNetpoller(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedWakeNetpollerName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var polling = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wnppolling", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var breakIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wnpbreak", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wakeOne = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wnpwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wnpdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let when = 0 as ValueId
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)

	let lastPoll = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedLastPollLabel, ids: ids)
	let somebodyPolls = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: lastPoll, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(somebodyPolls, thenBlockId: polling.id, elseBlockId: wakeOne.id))

	let pollUntil = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: polling, label: SchedPollUntilLabel, ids: ids)
	let pollerLate = emitSchedPollerLate(module, block: polling, pollUntil: pollUntil, when: when, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(polling, op: StdOp.condBranch(pollerLate, thenBlockId: breakIt.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))

	module.appendOp(breakIt, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollBreakName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(breakIt, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.appendOp(wakeOne, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedWakePollerName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(wakeOne, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedWakeNetpoller'

// 1 when a poller whose published deadline is `pollUntil` would wake after `when`, else 0. A published 0 is a
// poller that has claimed the role and not yet published, or has already cleared it on its way out: a break
// costs either one early return.
function emitSchedPollerLate(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pollUntil ValueId, when ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let noDeadline = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: pollUntil, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let laterDeadline = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: pollUntil, b: when, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: noDeadline, b: laterDeadline, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedPollerLate'

// `__sched_wake_poller()` — see `SchedWakePollerName`. The owed test is the unlocked peek
// `emitSchedPollerOwed` argues; the machine is taken off the list under the lock, as `mget` takes one, so two
// callers never signal the same one, and signalled after it, for `buildSchedStartM`'s reason.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AN UNLOCKED PEEK AT `__sched_midle` DECIDES WHETHER TO TAKE THE LOCK AT ALL, AND A STALE "EMPTY" IS
// SAFE.** Every caller has just made the deadline pending or found it pending after a hold of `__sched_lock` —
// the armer's add, the poller's release of the role in `wpolled`. A machine that joined the list in a hold
// before that one is visible to this read, which follows the caller's own acquire of the lock; one that parks
// after it reaches the idle tail with the deadline pending and nobody polling, and claims the poller itself
// rather than joining the list.
function buildSchedWakePoller(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedWakePollerName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var peek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wppeek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wptake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unlink = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpunlink", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noneParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wpdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitSchedPollerOwed(module, block: entry, ids: ids), thenBlockId: peek.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))

	let seenHead = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: peek, label: SchedMidleLabel, ids: ids)
	let seenEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: peek, a: seenHead, b: emitConst(module, block: peek, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(peek, op: StdOp.condBranch(seenEmpty, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: take.id))

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: take, ids: ids)
	let parked = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: take, label: SchedMidleLabel, ids: ids)
	let nobodyParked = emitCmp(module, block: take, a: parked, b: emitConst(module, block: take, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(take, op: StdOp.condBranch(nobodyParked, thenBlockId: noneParked.id, elseBlockId: unlink.id))

	var unlinked = emitSchedMidleUnlinkLocked(module, func: func, cur: unlink, m: parked, ids: ids)
	let parkEvent = emitLoadField(module, block: unlinked, record: parked, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: unlinked, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(unlinked, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(parkEvent))
	module.setTerminator(unlinked, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: noneParked, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noneParked, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedWakePoller'

// ==========================================================================
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE SYSCALL HANDOFF (W213-C3) — `__sched_handoffp`, `__sched_retake`, `__sysmon`,
// `__sched_syscall_regain`
// ==========================================================================

// The walk `SchedHandoffPName` describes. `p` arrives OFF `__sched_pidle`, still marked `PStatusActive` and
// named by no machine — the state `__sched_retake`'s second CAS leaves it in — so nothing else in the runtime
// can reach it and every arm below has it exclusively.
//
// ⭐⭐ **GO'S FIVE ARMS ARE FOUR HERE.** Go's *"no spinning M and no idle P ⇒ start one spinning"* would be
// unreachable, so it is not built: `__sched_retake`'s `rtworth` hands a processor over only when its ring or the
// global queue holds a green thread, and arm 1 starts a machine for every such processor before the spinning arm
// would be asked. The arm and a retake that also hands over idle processors belong together, and neither is here.
//
// ⚠ **THE DEFAULT ARM'S `pidleput` ASSERTS AN EMPTY RING, AND ARM 1 IS WHAT MAKES THAT TRUE.** Once
// `M->currentP` has been zeroed no machine can PUSH to this ring — `__sched_runq_put` pushes to the ring of
// the processor its own machine holds — so a thief may still drain it but nobody can fill it. Arm 1's
// unlocked read is therefore not merely a hint: an empty answer stays empty all the way to the assertion.
function buildSchedHandoffP(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedHandoffPName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var startIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hopstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var recheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hoprecheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lockedStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hoplockedstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var idleIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hopidle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lastProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hoplastp", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let p = 0 as ValueId

	// Arm 1 — this processor's own ring, or the global queue, holds a green thread. Both reads are unlocked,
	// which is `emitSchedAnyRunnable`'s stated exception: they pick an arm, and both arms are correct whatever
	// they answer. A false positive costs a machine that searches and finds nothing; a false negative is caught
	// by the locked re-check below.
	let runnable = emitSchedAnyRunnable(module, block: entry, p: p, ids: ids)
	let entryZero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingToRun = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: runnable, b: entryZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingToRun, thenBlockId: recheck.id, elseBlockId: startIt.id))

	// ⚠ **`__sched_startm` MAY PLACE NOTHING, AND THAT NEEDS NO ARM HERE.** A failed `osThreadCreate` moves the
	// ring to the global queue and puts the processor back on `__sched_pidle` itself. The shutdown road, which
	// it reads under `__sched_lock`, drops the processor — and one orphaned at teardown is one nobody needs:
	// `__gt_drain_at_exit` has already run the scheduler until no promise is outstanding before the flag is set,
	// and putting it back on `__sched_pidle` would offer it to a `__sched_startm` that reads the same flag and
	// places nothing.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE MACHINE IS STARTED WITHOUT A SPINNING CREDIT** — Go's `startm(pp, false)` — because this call
	// reserved none; see `SchedStartMCredit`.
	emitSchedStartM(module, block: startIt, p: p, credit: SchedStartMCredit.unreserved, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(startIt, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// Go's spinning arm sits between arm 1 and this one; see this function's header for why it is not built.
	//
	// Arm 3 — Go's *"recheck the global queue under sched.lock"*. Arm 1's read was unsynchronized, and the
	// global queue is the one tier a party holding no processor can both see and fill (`buildSchedLoop`'s
	// final look states the same set from the other side).
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: recheck, ids: ids)
	let globalHasWork = emitSchedGlobalQueueIs(module, block: recheck, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let recheckZero = emitConst(module, block: recheck, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let stillNothing = emitCmp(module, block: recheck, a: globalHasWork, b: recheckZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(stillNothing, thenBlockId: lastProc.id, elseBlockId: lockedStart.id))

	// Arm 4 — Go's *"last running P and nobody polling the network"*: every other processor is idle and no
	// machine waits in the poller, so a deadline could come due with nobody to wake for it. Both reads are under
	// the lock their writers hold.
	let othersIdle = emitBin(module, block: lastProc, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: lastProc, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: lastProc, value: OneMore, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let isLastProc = emitCmp(module, block: lastProc, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: lastProc, label: SchedNpidleLabel, ids: ids), b: othersIdle, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let nobodyPolls = emitCmp(module, block: lastProc, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: lastProc, label: SchedLastPollLabel, ids: ids), b: recheckZero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let needsPoller = emitBin(module, block: lastProc, a: isLastProc, b: nobodyPolls, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lastProc, op: StdOp.condBranch(needsPoller, thenBlockId: lockedStart.id, elseBlockId: idleIt.id))

	// The lock is released BEFORE the call: `__sched_startm` takes it itself, and may `osThreadCreate` while
	// holding it.
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: lockedStart, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lockedStart, op: StdOp.branch(startIt.id))

	// Arm 5 — nothing to run anywhere: park the PROCESSOR, which is Go's `pidleput(pp, 0)` and the sentence
	// `POffPidleNext` was created to make expressible.
	var idled = emitSchedPidlePutLocked(module, func: func, cur: idleIt, p: p, holdsWorkLabel: b"hopheldwork", emptyLabel: b"hopidled", ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: idled, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(idled, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedHandoffP'

// The walk `SchedRetakeName` describes: over the processors, take each one away from the machine holding it when
// that machine has been inside ONE bracketed kernel call for longer than `SysmonRetakeAfterNs` and the processor
// has work waiting. Answers how many it took.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **IT ONLY STEALS WHERE THERE IS WORK TO RUN, AND THAT IS A DIVERGENCE FROM GO THAT MAKES THIS
// SCHEDULER SAFER RATHER THAN LAZIER.** Go's third arm skips a retake when the P's ring is empty AND a
// spinning M or an idle P exists AND the block is younger than 10 ms — three terms, two of which need
// `sched.npidle`. The compiler asks the ONE question those three approximate: **would anything actually run on this
// processor if it were handed over?** If not, the handoff is pure cost — a machine started, a search run, a
// park paid — and, worse, it forces the blocked machine through `__sched_syscall_regain` on its way out for
// no benefit at all. A blocking read on an otherwise idle scheduler therefore costs nothing whatever, which is
// the common case and is exactly what the old runtime already did correctly.
//
// ⛔ **GO ALSO PREEMPTS A LONG-*RUNNING* GOROUTINE HERE AND THE COMPILER DOES NOT.** The compiler is cooperative by
// construction; there is no `preemptone` to port and no signal to deliver. `POffRunnext` carries what the
// absence still costs — it is why a `runnext` slot cannot be taken on the throughput case alone.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT WALKS THE PROCESSORS — Go's `allp` — SO A LAP COSTS THE PROCESSOR COUNT, NOT THE MACHINE COUNT.** A
// machine with no processor has nothing to retake, and every machine that holds one is named by it (`POffM`).
//
// ⚠ **THE PROCESSOR-TO-MACHINE READ IS UNLOCKED AND NAMES ONLY A CANDIDATE.** A stale `POffM` names a machine
// that has since released this processor and may hold another. The pass reads that machine's state word, then
// its `MOffCurrentP`, and goes on unless the second is this processor; the compare-and-swap at `rtclaim` then
// proves the machine never left the call its word names, and inside a call `MOffCurrentP` has no writer. So a
// win proves the machine held this processor for the whole of that call, whatever `POffM` said — and the
// machine a stale read hid is reached through the processor it does hold, on this lap or the next.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO DEADLOCK-CHECK BRACKET HERE, WHERE GO BRACKETS THIS BODY IN `incidlelocked(±1)`.** Go's
// `checkdead` counts IDLE LOCKED Ms, so a victim leaving its syscall and parking mid-handoff makes the count
// reach the deadlock threshold. `__sched_checkdead` counts every machine off `__sched_midle` as running, and
// the victim of a retake is on that list at no point: it is inside its kernel call, then waiting for a
// processor in `__sched_syscall_regain`. ⇒ **the state Go's bracket exists to hide cannot be produced here.**
function buildSchedRetake(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedRetakeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rthdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var askProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtask", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var inSyscall = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtsyscall", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noteIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtnote", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var recordIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtrecord", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var oldEnough = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtold", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var worthIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtworth", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claimIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var tookIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rttook", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nextProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rtdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let now = 0 as ValueId

	// ⚠ Both words are read unlocked, which `sealedBeforeWorkers` allows only because the monitor cannot start
	// during bring-up: `__sched_init_procs` publishes the count before it builds the array, and the monitor is
	// started by the first publish (`emitSchedSysmonLazyStart`), after bring-up has returned.
	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let index = ids.mint()
	let taken = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(index, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(taken, argType: StdType.i64))
	let entryZero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(entryZero, second: entryZero)))

	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: loopHdr, a: index, b: numProcs, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loopHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: askProc.id))

	// Every arm below that declines carries the running count through this block arg. Only `rttook` supplies a
	// different one, which is what makes the count a fact about what this pass DID rather than about how far it
	// walked.
	let carriedTaken = ids.mint()
	nextProc.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(carriedTaken, argType: StdType.i64))

	// The machine holding this processor, if any — a candidate, for this function's header's reason.
	let proc = emitLoadProc(module, block: askProc, index: index, ids: ids)
	let machine = emitLoadField(module, block: askProc, record: proc, offset: POffM, ids: ids)
	let unheld = emitCmp(module, block: askProc, a: machine, b: emitConst(module, block: askProc, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(askProc, op: StdOp.condBranch(unheld, thenBlockId: nextProc.id, elseBlockId: haveMachine.id))
	askProc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE WORD IS READ FIRST, AND THE PROCESSOR IS READ AFTER IT — THE OTHER ORDER IS THE ABA.** Every
	// test below is a HINT until the compare-and-swap at `rtclaim`; what makes the hints usable is that the CAS
	// re-presents the EXACT word this block read, so a machine that has moved on since cannot match it. Reading
	// the word first is what makes the processor read below a fact about THIS call rather than about whatever
	// the machine happened to hold a moment earlier. `MOffSyscallState`'s header carries the interleaving that
	// the two-word version of this got wrong.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE READ IS AN ACQUIRE, BECAUSE THE ORDER HAS TO BE THE HARDWARE'S AS WELL AS THE PROGRAM'S.** Two
	// plain loads may be satisfied out of order on arm64, and a `MOffCurrentP` older than the word would name
	// the processor the machine held BEFORE this call. Pairing with the release in `entersyscall`'s step is
	// what makes the processor read below see the value the machine entered the call with.
	let stateNow = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: haveMachine, record: machine, offset: MOffSyscallState, ids: ids)
	let stateMask = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: MSyscallStateMask, ids: ids)
	let inCallOnly = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: MSyscallInCall, ids: ids)
	let stateBits = emitBin(module, block: haveMachine, a: stateNow, b: stateMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)

	// EXACTLY `MSyscallInCall` — a machine whose stolen bit is also set has already had its processor taken and
	// is inside `__sched_syscall_regain` waiting for another, which is the one state this pass must leave alone.
	let notInCall = emitCmp(module, block: haveMachine, a: stateBits, b: inCallOnly, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveMachine, op: StdOp.condBranch(notInCall, thenBlockId: nextProc.id, elseBlockId: inSyscall.id))
	haveMachine.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERATION TEST IS A COMPARISON OF THE WHOLE WORD**, because the generation lives in it — see
	// `MOffSysmonWord`. A word that has moved means a DIFFERENT call, so the clock starts again; a word that has
	// not means this machine has been inside ONE call since the previous poll, which is the only reading that
	// tells a one-second read from a thousand `print`s.
	let wordSeen = emitLoadField(module, block: inSyscall, record: machine, offset: MOffSysmonWord, ids: ids)
	let sameCall = emitCmp(module, block: inSyscall, a: stateNow, b: wordSeen, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(inSyscall, op: StdOp.condBranch(sameCall, thenBlockId: noteIt.id, elseBlockId: recordIt.id))

	// ⛔ **THE NOTE IS WRITTEN ONLY ON THE ARM THAT DECLINES, AND A BLOCK OF ITS OWN IS WHAT MAKES THAT
	// POSSIBLE.** Writing `MOffSysmonWhen` unconditionally would restart the clock on every pass, so the
	// elapsed test below could never be satisfied and no processor would ever be retaken — a mechanism that
	// compiles, runs and does nothing.
	emitStoreField(module, block: recordIt, record: machine, offset: MOffSysmonWord, value: stateNow)
	emitStoreField(module, block: recordIt, record: machine, offset: MOffSysmonWhen, value: now)
	module.setTerminator(recordIt, op: StdOp.branch(nextProc.id))
	recordIt.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	let since = emitBin(module, block: noteIt, a: now, b: emitLoadField(module, block: noteIt, record: machine, offset: MOffSysmonWhen, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let threshold = emitConst(module, block: noteIt, value: SysmonRetakeAfterNs, ids: ids)
	let tooYoung = emitCmp(module, block: noteIt, a: since, b: threshold, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noteIt, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooYoung, thenBlockId: nextProc.id, elseBlockId: oldEnough.id))
	noteIt.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	// ⭐ **THE MACHINE'S OWN PROCESSOR, READ AFTER THE WORD, MUST BE THIS ONE.** The read is unlocked, and what
	// makes it trustworthy is the CAS below, which proves the machine never left the call this word names — and a
	// machine inside a call touches `MOffCurrentP` at neither of its two writers (`emitSchedAdoptProc`,
	// `emitSchedReleaseProcLocked`), both of which run outside one.
	let heldProc = emitLoadField(module, block: oldEnough, record: machine, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	let elsewhere = emitCmp(module, block: oldEnough, a: heldProc, b: proc, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(oldEnough, op: StdOp.condBranch(elsewhere, thenBlockId: nextProc.id, elseBlockId: worthIt.id))
	oldEnough.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	// ⭐ The divergence this function's header argues: a processor with nothing to run is not worth taking.
	let wouldRun = emitSchedAnyRunnable(module, block: worthIt, p: proc, ids: ids)
	let worthZero = emitConst(module, block: worthIt, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingWaiting = emitCmp(module, block: worthIt, a: wouldRun, b: worthZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(worthIt, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingWaiting, thenBlockId: nextProc.id, elseBlockId: claimIt.id))
	worthIt.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE LINEARIZATION POINT, AND IT CAS-es THE EXACT WORD THIS PASS MEASURED.** `stateNow` carries
	// the call's GENERATION as well as its state bits, so the value below is one **no later call can present**:
	// every mutation of this word only ever raises the generation (`MOffSyscallState` states the three and why
	// none of them lowers it). A machine that has left the call — or left it and entered another, on another
	// processor — therefore fails this CAS and the pass declines.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AN EARLIER CUT COMPARED A BARE `Active` HERE AND THAT WAS AN ABA.** `Active` recurs on every
	// subsequent bracketed call, so the CAS could not tell *"the call I measured"* from *"a later one that looks
	// the same"* — and the handoff below would then start a machine on a processor a DIFFERENT machine was
	// already running. The generation is what makes success a PROOF of ownership rather than a likelihood.
	//
	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO ROAD BACK FROM A WIN**, which is why every decision is taken above this line: see
	// `MOffSyscallState` for why the protocol has no release arm and why it needs none.
	let stateAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: claimIt, record: machine, offset: MOffSyscallState, ids: ids)
	let markStolen = emitConst(module, block: claimIt, value: MSyscallStolen, ids: ids)
	let toStolen = emitBin(module, block: claimIt, a: stateNow, b: markStolen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let claimed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(claimIt, op: StdOp.atomicCas(claimed, addrId: stateAddr, expectedId: stateNow, replacementId: toStolen))
	let lostClaim = emitCasLost(module, block: claimIt, result: claimed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(claimIt, op: StdOp.condBranch(lostClaim, thenBlockId: nextProc.id, elseBlockId: tookIt.id))
	claimIt.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	// The second CAS, whose ANSWER IS DELIBERATELY DISCARDED — `MOffSyscallState`'s header carries why both
	// outcomes are correct and why a plain store here would be a wrong answer rather than a slower one.
	let procAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: tookIt, record: machine, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	let releasedProc = emitConst(module, block: tookIt, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(tookIt, op: StdOp.atomicCas(ids.mint(), addrId: procAddr, expectedId: proc, replacementId: releasedProc))

	// The processor is the thief's now, and names no machine until the handoff gives it one.
	emitStoreField(module, block: tookIt, record: proc, offset: POffM, value: releasedProc)

	// The counter `__Builtins.schedRetakeCount()` sums. sysmon is its only writer, so `multiM: false` — the
	// plain arm. The prior value has no reader.
	let oneRetake = emitConst(module, block: tookIt, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: tookIt, record: proc, offset: POffRetakeCount, delta: oneRetake, multiM: false, ids: ids)

	// The note is refreshed to the word this CAS just wrote, and the clock restarts. Neither is strictly
	// required — the stolen bit alone keeps the next pass off this machine, because `rtmachine` demands the state
	// bits be EXACTLY `MSyscallInCall` — but leaving a note that names a word no longer in memory is the kind
	// of stale fact the next reader has to re-derive.
	emitStoreField(module, block: tookIt, record: machine, offset: MOffSysmonWord, value: toStolen)
	emitStoreField(module, block: tookIt, record: machine, offset: MOffSysmonWhen, value: now)
	module.appendOp(tookIt, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedHandoffPName, args: argsOf(proc)))
	let takenNow = emitBin(module, block: tookIt, a: taken, b: oneRetake, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(tookIt, op: StdOp.branch(nextProc.id))
	tookIt.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nextProc.id, argIds: argsOf(takenNow)))

	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: nextProc, a: index, b: emitConst(module, block: nextProc, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nextProc, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	nextProc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextIndex, second: carriedTaken)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(taken))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedRetake'

// The loop `SchedSysmonName` describes — Go's `sysmon`: sleep a lap (`SysmonMinSleepUs`'s ladder), sleep deeply
// while every processor is idle (`SchedSysmonWaitLabel`), take a non-blocking look at the poller when nobody has
// for `SysmonPollStaleNs`, and retake.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS NOT AN M AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE.** It never calls `emitSchedBecomeM`, so its TLS slot stays 0
// for its whole life — which is what makes the syscall shim take its DIRECT arm on every kernel call this
// thread makes (`emitLoadCurrentGtOrBail`'s first test), what routes every allocation `__sched_startm` makes
// on its behalf to the allocator's raw shard (`SlabRuntime.emitSlabCurrentP`'s bail), and what keeps it off
// `__sched_allm` and `__sched_mcount` — so `__sched_checkdead` never counts it as a running machine and
// `__sched_stop_workers` never tries to join it. **Those three are one property, stated once here rather than
// arranged for three times.**
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING JOINS IT AT TEARDOWN.** It reads the shutdown flag at the top of every lap and returns from its
// thread procedure; one asleep on an all-idle scheduler holds nothing anybody needs, and the process exit ends
// it.
//
// ⚠ **A DEEP SLEEP KEEPS THE LADDER WHERE IT WAS**, which is Go's rule for a wake that did not come from a
// syscall: a process that sleeps most of the time should not buy fifty fast laps at every wake.
function buildSysmon(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedSysmonName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var started = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonstarted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopTop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonloop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonnap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var deepSleep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smondeep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonawake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var afterNap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonafternap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claimPoll = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonclaimpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stalePoll = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notStale = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonpolltaken", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var retakeBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonretake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var retook = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonretook", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var idled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonidle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var backOff = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonbackoff", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var keepPace = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonkeep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var exitBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"smonexit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The `lpParameter` `osThreadCreate` hands this thread carries nothing: the monitor's whole state is the
	// block args below plus what it reads out of the roster. It is named rather than left unmentioned so the
	// arity matches the one thread-entry shape the runtime has.
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)

	// Its sleep's timer and its deep sleep's event, both its own.
	let timer = ids.mint()
	started.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(timer, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitNetpollOpenTimer(module, func: func, cur: entry, target: target, cont: started, ids: ids)

	let monitorEvent = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(started, op: StdOp.osEventCreate(monitorEvent))
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: started, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: started, label: SchedSysmonEventLabel, value: monitorEvent, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: started, ids: ids)

	let delayUs = ids.mint()
	let idleLaps = ids.mint()
	loopTop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(delayUs, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopTop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(idleLaps, argType: StdType.i64))
	let firstDelay = emitConst(module, block: started, value: SysmonMinSleepUs, ids: ids)
	let noLaps = emitConst(module, block: started, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(started, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))
	started.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopTop.id, argIds: twoArgs(firstDelay, second: noLaps)))

	emitSchedBranchOnShutdown(module, cur: loopTop, stopping: exitBlock, live: nap, ids: ids)

	_ = emitNetpoll(module, block: nap, verb: NetpollVerb.sleepOnTimer, operands: twoArgs(delayUs, second: timer), ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: nap, ids: ids)
	let idleProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: nap, label: SchedNpidleLabel, ids: ids)
	let allIdle = emitCmp(module, block: nap, a: idleProcs, b: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: nap, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nap, op: StdOp.condBranch(allIdle, thenBlockId: deepSleep.id, elseBlockId: awake.id))

	// The flag goes up inside the lock `pidleget` reads it under, so a processor taken after this hold finds it
	// raised and signals, and one taken before it is visible in the count this hold just read. The event is
	// auto-reset: a signal landing between the release and the wait ends the wait at once.
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: deepSleep, label: SchedSysmonWaitLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: deepSleep, value: 1, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: deepSleep, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(deepSleep, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(ids.mint(), handleId: monitorEvent, timeoutMsId: emitConst(module, block: deepSleep, value: WaitInfinite, ids: ids)))
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: deepSleep, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: deepSleep, label: SchedSysmonWaitLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: deepSleep, value: 0, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: deepSleep, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(deepSleep, op: StdOp.branch(afterNap.id))

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: awake, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(awake, op: StdOp.branch(afterNap.id))

	// ⭐ **THE STALE POLL — Go's *"poll network if not polled for more than 10ms"*.** The unlocked peek picks
	// whether to take the lock; the claim under it is what decides, so a blocking poller that claimed meanwhile
	// is never looked past. The look is non-blocking, so it takes nothing that belongs to a blocking poller.
	let now = emitGtNowNs(module, block: afterNap, ids: ids)
	let peeked = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: afterNap, label: SchedLastPollLabel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterNap, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitSchedPollIsStale(module, block: afterNap, lastPoll: peeked, now: now, ids: ids), thenBlockId: claimPoll.id, elseBlockId: retakeBlock.id))

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: claimPoll, ids: ids)
	let lastPoll = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: claimPoll, label: SchedLastPollLabel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(claimPoll, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitSchedPollIsStale(module, block: claimPoll, lastPoll: lastPoll, now: now, ids: ids), thenBlockId: stalePoll.id, elseBlockId: notStale.id))

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: stalePoll, label: SchedLastPollLabel, value: now, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: stalePoll, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(stalePoll, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollPollName, args: argsOf(emitConst(module, block: stalePoll, value: 0, ids: ids))))
	module.setTerminator(stalePoll, op: StdOp.branch(retakeBlock.id))

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: notStale, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notStale, op: StdOp.branch(retakeBlock.id))

	let retakes = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(retakeBlock, op: StdOp.call(retakes, callee: SchedRetakeName, args: argsOf(now)))
	let tookNothing = emitCmp(module, block: retakeBlock, a: retakes, b: emitConst(module, block: retakeBlock, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(retakeBlock, op: StdOp.condBranch(tookNothing, thenBlockId: idled.id, elseBlockId: retook.id))

	// A lap that took something is evidence the process is blocking, so the interval snaps back to its
	// shortest — the next block is likely and the cost of noticing it late is a stalled processor.
	let snapBack = emitConst(module, block: retook, value: SysmonMinSleepUs, ids: ids)
	let resetLaps = emitConst(module, block: retook, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(retook, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))
	retook.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopTop.id, argIds: twoArgs(snapBack, second: resetLaps)))

	let lapsNow = emitBin(module, block: idled, a: idleLaps, b: emitConst(module, block: idled, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let enoughLaps = emitConst(module, block: idled, value: SysmonIdlePollsBeforeBackoff, ids: ids)
	let quietEnough = emitCmp(module, block: idled, a: lapsNow, b: enoughLaps, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(idled, op: StdOp.condBranch(quietEnough, thenBlockId: backOff.id, elseBlockId: keepPace.id))

	// Doubled, then capped: `min(2·delay, SysmonMaxSleepUs)` spelled branch-free as the cap plus the part of the
	// difference below zero.
	let doubled = emitBin(module, block: backOff, a: delayUs, b: delayUs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let longDelay = emitSchedMinWord(module, block: backOff, a: doubled, b: emitConst(module, block: backOff, value: SysmonMaxSleepUs, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(backOff, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))
	backOff.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopTop.id, argIds: twoArgs(longDelay, second: lapsNow)))

	module.setTerminator(keepPace, op: StdOp.branch(loopTop.id))
	keepPace.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopTop.id, argIds: twoArgs(delayUs, second: lapsNow)))

	// A `CreateThread` callback returns a DWORD nobody reads; 0 is the conventional success, which is what
	// `__sched_worker_loop` answers on its own exit road for the same reason.
	emitConstReturn(module, block: exitBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSysmon'

// 1 when nobody is blocked in the poller and nobody has polled for `SysmonPollStaleNs`, else 0.
function emitSchedPollIsStale(module StdModule, block IrBlock, lastPoll ValueId, now ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let nobodyPolls = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: lastPoll, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let staleAt = emitBin(module, block: block, a: lastPoll, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: SysmonPollStaleNs, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let stale = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: staleAt, b: now, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: nobodyPolls, b: stale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedPollIsStale'

// The smaller of two words, branch-free: `b + ((a - b) & ((a - b) >> 63))`. Neither operand here is near the
// ends of the range, so the difference cannot overflow.
function emitSchedMinWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, a ValueId, b ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let difference = emitBin(module, block: block, a: a, b: b, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let signMask = emitBin(module, block: block, a: difference, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: WordSignShift, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: b, b: emitBin(module, block: block, a: difference, b: signMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedMinWord'

// A signed word's sign, shifted down to fill it.
let WordSignShift = 63 as ParsedInt

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_enter_syscall` / `__sched_exit_syscall`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`entersyscall` — *"this machine is about to be unreachable; its processor may be taken."***
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE `atomicRmw add` DOES BOTH HALVES, BECAUSE THE GENERATION AND THE STATE SHARE A WORD.**
// `MSyscallEnterDelta` steps the generation AND sets the in-call bit in a single indivisible step, so there
// is no interval in which a new call is announced without its generation or the other way round — see
// `MOffSyscallState` for the ABA a two-word cut of this carries. On arm64 the step lowers to LL/SC, which
// supplies the acquire/release that word's own ordering note asks for.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE GUARD IS *"AN M EXISTS"*, WHICH IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM THE x64 SHIM'S GUARD.** That one
// additionally requires a green thread with a non-zero `stackBase`, because what it is guarding is the STACK
// SWITCH onto the processor's syscall stack — a machine running on its own OS stack must not switch. The
// bracket has no such concern: it touches one word on the M, and `main`'s own blocking calls are exactly as
// worth handing a processor off for as a spawned green thread's. This is Go's shape (`entersyscall` runs on
// every goroutine including the main one), and it is why the two guards differ rather than one being a copy
// of the other.
//
// ⚠ **THE OFFSET TEST IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS NOT AN ABUNDANCE OF CAUTION.** Before `__sched_init_procs` the
// TEB offset is 0, and slot 0 of a thread's own storage is a real, NON-NULL pointer on the lanes this runs on
// — so an unguarded read would treat it as an M and step a word inside somebody else's structure.
// `emitLoadCurrentMOrBail` is that test, shared with the three other walks that need it.
function buildSchedEnterSyscall(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedEnterSyscallName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"esoff", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"esm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"esnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let machine = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: entry, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: noMachine, bailValues: ParsedIntArray.create(), ids: ids)

	let delta = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: MSyscallEnterDelta, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: haveMachine, record: machine, offset: MOffSyscallState, delta: delta, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveMachine, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.setTerminator(noMachine, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedEnterSyscall'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`exitsyscall` — ONE ATOMIC ON THE FAST PATH, A CALL OUT ONLY WHEN THE PROCESSOR IS GONE.**
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE `atomicRmw add` ANSWERS BOTH QUESTIONS AT ONCE, WHICH IS WHY THERE IS NO RETRY LOOP AND NO CAS.**
// Adding `-MSyscallInCall` clears the in-call bit, and the op hands back the PRIOR word — whose stolen bit is
// the entire verdict. Everything above that bit is the generation and is none of this side's business, which
// is what the mask is for.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE STOLEN BIT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* CLEARED HERE — `__sched_syscall_regain` clears it.** Leaving it
// set is what marks this machine as *"holding a green thread with no processor"* for the two parties that
// must see that state: `__sched_retake`, which requires exactly `MSyscallInCall` and therefore skips it, and
// `__sched_stop_workers`, which must NOT join a machine that may never come back. `MOffSyscallState` carries
// both halves.
//
// ⚠ **THE ENTRY AND THE EXIT AGREE BY CONSTRUCTION.** Nothing between them can change which M this OS thread
// is on — a handoff moves the PROCESSOR, never the machine — so the exit's guarded walk answers the same M
// the entry's did, and a bail on one side is a bail on the other.
function buildSchedExitSyscall(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedExitSyscallName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"xsoff", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"xsm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var regainProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"xsregain", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"xsdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let machine = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: entry, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: doneBlock, bailValues: ParsedIntArray.create(), ids: ids)

	let delta = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: 0 - MSyscallInCall, ids: ids)
	let prior = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: haveMachine, record: machine, offset: MOffSyscallState, delta: delta, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	let stolenMask = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: MSyscallStolen, ids: ids)
	let stolenBit = emitBin(module, block: haveMachine, a: prior, b: stolenMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let notStolen = emitConst(module, block: haveMachine, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let wasRobbed = emitCmp(module, block: haveMachine, a: stolenBit, b: notStolen, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveMachine, op: StdOp.condBranch(wasRobbed, thenBlockId: regainProc.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))

	module.appendOp(regainProc, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedSyscallRegainName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(regainProc, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedExitSyscall'

// The road `SchedSyscallRegainName` describes: take a processor off `__sched_pidle`, or wait on this machine's
// own event until `pidleput` hands it one (Go's `stopm` on `exitsyscall`'s slow path) — and, once it has one,
// clear the stolen bit `exitsyscall` deliberately left standing.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT WAITS ON A LIST OF ITS OWN, NOT ON `__sched_midle`.** A machine on that list is a machine
// `__sched_startm` may hand a SPINNING CREDIT to (`SpinningTransition.handOverSpinning`), and one it counts
// idle; this machine is neither — it holds a runnable green thread. `__sched_regain_head` is served by
// `pidleput` before a processor would go idle, and joining it happens in the same hold of `__sched_lock` as the
// `pidleget` that found nothing, so no processor released after that look can pass it by.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE STOLEN BIT IS CLEARED LAST, AND ONLY ONCE A PROCESSOR IS IN HAND.** Until then this machine is
// *"holding a green thread with no processor"*, and two parties read that state off this word:
// `__sched_retake`, which requires the state bits to be EXACTLY `MSyscallInCall` and therefore leaves this
// machine alone, and `__sched_stop_workers`, which must not join it. Clearing the bit earlier would open a
// window in which the teardown could block for ever on a thread that is still waiting.
//
// ⚠ **THE CLEAR IS A PLAIN LOAD/SUB/STORE AND THAT IS ARGUED, NOT ASSUMED.** This word's only other writer is
// `__sched_retake`'s compare-and-swap, which fires only on state bits of exactly `MSyscallInCall`; ours are
// `MSyscallStolen`, so no concurrent write to this word is possible while these three ops run. The generation
// in the high bits is preserved by construction — a subtraction of `MSyscallStolen` touches bit 1 alone.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE SHUTDOWN ESCAPE HAS TWO ARMS, AND WHICH ONE A MACHINE TAKES IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A THREAD
// THE PROCESS EXIT DISCARDS AND THE THREAD THAT *RUNS* THE PROCESS EXIT.** `__sched_shutdown_flag` is read
// under the same `__sched_lock` that `__sched_stop_workers` sets it in, both before joining the waiters and after
// every wake — the teardown signals every machine's event — so the read is authoritative rather than a race.
// What the flag does NOT say is whether anything still needs this OS thread to come back, and that is the whole
// of the decision below:
//
//   • **A WORKER PARKS FOR EVER, AND PARKING COSTS NOTHING.** By the time the flag is set,
//     `__gt_drain_at_exit` has already run the scheduler until no promise is outstanding and `main` has
//     returned — so the green thread this machine holds has no consumer left, and finishing it would answer a
//     promise nobody will read.
//     Nothing waits for the thread either: the stolen bit is still set, so `__sched_stop_workers` skips its
//     join (see there); it holds no lock, allocates nothing and steps no counter, so `__mm_leak_check` reads
//     a still world; and the process exit ends it. **This is Go's answer too — Go does not join threads at
//     exit at all.** ⇒ handing it back a processor-less green thread would buy nothing and would put an
//     `M->currentP == 0` machine on a road whose `__sched_find_runnable` dereferences that word three ops in,
//     before any test.
//   • ⭐⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE THAT RUNS THE PROCESS EXIT RETURNS, BECAUSE PARKING IT IS A HANG.**
//     `__sched_shutdown_flag` has exactly one writer, `__sched_stop_workers`, whose one caller
//     `__gt_drain_at_exit` runs on M0's scheduler context once `main`'s green thread has completed — so a
//     machine that finds the flag set and IS that thread is somewhere between the teardown and the OS exit,
//     with the rest of the exit path still ahead of it. It returns holding no processor, which is safe on the
//     road that is left **because of the order `__gt_drain_at_exit` fixes**: the scheduler has run out BEFORE
//     the flag is set, so this machine enters no scheduler loop again and `__sched_find_runnable` is
//     unreachable from here. The two doors the exit path does still use — an allocation
//     (`SlabRuntime.emitSlabCurrentP`) and a publish (`__sched_runq_put`) — each test `M->currentP` on their
//     own account and carry a P-less arm.
//
// Either arm leaves the waiters first, so a processor released afterwards reaches `__sched_pidle`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO ARE TOLD APART BY `MOffOsThreadHandle`, AND `rgntry`'s LOCK IS WHAT MAKES THAT TEST EXACT
// RATHER THAN OVERWHELMINGLY LIKELY.** A zero handle is a thread this runtime never created, which is the
// main one; the only other machine that can carry a zero there is one whose creator has not stored the handle
// yet, and no such machine can be here. `__sched_startm` holds `__sched_lock` from before `osThreadCreate`
// until after that store, so a machine's FIRST acquisition of the lock is ordered after its own handle store
// — and this function has acquired it, at `rgntry`, before any road reaches the test. `__sched_stop_workers`
// reads the same word for the same meaning.
//
// ⚠ **THE WAIT IS ORDERED PROCESSOR-FIRST, SO THE ESCAPE FIRES ONLY WHEN THERE IS GENUINELY NOTHING.** A
// worker that reaches `wexit` releases its processor through the ordinary door, which hands it to a waiter, so
// the common teardown interleaving is that this machine takes one, clears its bit and exits cleanly.
//
// ⭐ **A MACHINE THAT TAKES THE HANDED-OFF PROCESSOR NEVER HOLDS IT WAITING FOR THIS GREEN THREAD.** Every
// wait parks the waiting green thread and returns its machine to the scheduler loop, whose park releases the
// processor when nothing is runnable — through `pidleput`, which hands it here.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT JOIN `__sched_midle`**, so `__sched_checkdead` counts it as running, which it is: it holds a
// green thread it will run once it has a processor.
function buildSchedSyscallRegain(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedSyscallRegainName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var tryTop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgntry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gotOne = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgngot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noneYet = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var enlist = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnenlist", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnwait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var woke = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnwoke", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var handed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnhanded", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stillWaiting = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnstill", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var keepWaiting = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnagain", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var leaveList = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnleave", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var leftList = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnleft", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var giveUp = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnexit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var resumeExit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnnoproc", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkForEver = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let selfMachine = emitLoadCurrentM(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(tryTop.id))

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: tryTop, ids: ids)
	let regained = emitSchedPidleGetLocked(module, func: func, cur: tryTop, got: gotOne, none: noneYet, wakesMonitor: true, ids: ids)
	emitSchedRegainResume(module, block: gotOne, m: selfMachine, p: regained, ids: ids)

	// The shutdown read is inside the lock the teardown sets the flag in, which is what makes it a decision
	// rather than a race — `buildSchedLoop`'s `wstoptest` reads it in the same place for the same reason.
	var stopUnlisted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnstopunlisted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitSchedBranchOnShutdown(module, cur: noneYet, stopping: stopUnlisted, live: enlist, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: stopUnlisted, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(stopUnlisted, op: StdOp.branch(giveUp.id))

	emitSchedListPushLocked(module, block: enlist, headLabel: SchedRegainHeadLabel, linkOffset: MOffRegainNext, node: selfMachine, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: enlist, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(enlist, op: StdOp.branch(parkIt.id))

	let parkEvent = emitLoadField(module, block: parkIt, record: selfMachine, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(parkIt, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(ids.mint(), handleId: parkEvent, timeoutMsId: emitConst(module, block: parkIt, value: WaitInfinite, ids: ids)))
	emitSchedCountParkWake(module, block: parkIt, m: selfMachine, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: parkIt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parkIt, op: StdOp.branch(woke.id))

	// ⭐ **A DELIVERED PROCESSOR MEANS THE GIVER HAS ALREADY TAKEN THIS MACHINE OFF THE LIST** — both under this
	// lock (`emitSchedPidlePutLocked`) — and none means it is still on it, woken by the teardown or by a stray
	// signal.
	let givenProc = emitLoadField(module, block: woke, record: selfMachine, offset: MOffNextP, ids: ids)
	let nothingGiven = emitCmp(module, block: woke, a: givenProc, b: emitConst(module, block: woke, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(woke, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingGiven, thenBlockId: stillWaiting.id, elseBlockId: handed.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: handed, record: selfMachine, offset: MOffNextP, value: emitConst(module, block: handed, value: 0, ids: ids))
	emitSchedRegainResume(module, block: handed, m: selfMachine, p: givenProc, ids: ids)

	emitSchedBranchOnShutdown(module, cur: stillWaiting, stopping: leaveList, live: keepWaiting, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: keepWaiting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(keepWaiting, op: StdOp.branch(parkIt.id))

	emitSchedRegainUnlinkLocked(module, func: func, cur: leaveList, m: selfMachine, cont: leftList, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: leftList, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(leftList, op: StdOp.branch(giveUp.id))

	// The two-armed escape this function's header argues. A machine this runtime created is a worker and is
	// discarded by the process exit; a machine it did not create is the one RUNNING that exit and must be given
	// its thread back.
	let handle = emitLoadField(module, block: giveUp, record: selfMachine, offset: MOffOsThreadHandle, ids: ids)
	let handleZero = emitConst(module, block: giveUp, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let runsTheExit = emitCmp(module, block: giveUp, a: handle, b: handleZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(giveUp, op: StdOp.condBranch(runsTheExit, thenBlockId: resumeExit.id, elseBlockId: parkForEver.id))

	// ⛔ **THE STOLEN BIT STAYS UP ON THIS ROAD, AND IT IS TELLING THE TRUTH: this machine is still holding a
	// green thread with no processor.** Its two readers both want that reading — `__sched_retake` leaves the
	// machine alone, and `__sched_stop_workers` (which this thread has already returned from) would not join
	// it. A later blocking call on the exit path therefore comes back through this same road and, if a
	// processor has been released meanwhile, leaves through `rgngot` with the bit cleared.
	module.setTerminator(resumeExit, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// ⛔ **PARK FOR EVER.** The wait is bounded by nothing and is meant to be: the process is ending, nothing
	// joins this machine, and the OS exit is what ends the thread. It blocks on the machine's OWN park event
	// rather than sleeping so that the thread costs no wakeups at all while the exit path finishes.
	let forever = emitConst(module, block: parkForEver, value: WaitInfinite, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(parkForEver, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(ids.mint(), handleId: emitLoadField(module, block: parkForEver, record: selfMachine, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids), timeoutMsId: forever))
	module.setTerminator(parkForEver, op: StdOp.branch(parkForEver.id))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedSyscallRegain'

// Leave `__sched_syscall_regain` holding processor `p`: release `__sched_lock`, adopt it, and bring the stolen
// bit down — see that function's header for the two readers that depend on it having stayed up, and for why
// these three ops need no atomic. It terminates `block` with the return.
function emitSchedRegainResume(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitSchedAdoptProc(module, block: block, m: m, p: p)
	let stolenBit = emitConst(module, block: block, value: MSyscallStolen, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffSyscallState, value: emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffSyscallState, ids: ids), b: stolenBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitSchedRegainResume'

// 1 once `__sched_stop_workers` has set its flag, else 0. Each reader argues at its own site whether it holds
// `__sched_lock`.
function emitSchedShuttingDown(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedShuttingDown'

// Terminate `cur`: to `stopping` once `__sched_stop_workers` has set its flag, else to `live`.
function emitSchedBranchOnShutdown(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, stopping IrBlock, live IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitSchedShuttingDown(module, block: cur, ids: ids), thenBlockId: stopping.id, elseBlockId: live.id))
end 'emitSchedBranchOnShutdown'

// ⭐ **TAKE `m` OFF `__sched_regain_head`, WHERE IT MUST BE** — a walk, because only the teardown's escape takes a
// machine out of the middle. `__sched_lock` held. It terminates `cur` and continues at `cont`; a walk that
// reaches the end has lost a waiter, which is `schedulerRegainWaiterLost`.
function emitSchedRegainUnlinkLocked(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var unlinkHead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnunlinkhead", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var walkHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnunlinkhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var walkStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnunlinkstep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unlinkInner = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnunlinkinner", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lost = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnunlinklost", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cleared = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rgnunlinkclear", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let head = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedRegainHeadLabel, ids: ids)
	let isHead = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: head, b: m, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(isHead, thenBlockId: unlinkHead.id, elseBlockId: walkHdr.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(walkHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(head)))

	let after = emitLoadField(module, block: unlinkHead, record: m, offset: MOffRegainNext, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: unlinkHead, label: SchedRegainHeadLabel, value: after, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unlinkHead, op: StdOp.branch(cleared.id))

	// walkHdr(prev): `prev` is on the list and is not `m`.
	let prev = ids.mint()
	walkHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(prev, argType: StdType.i64))
	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: walkHdr, a: prev, b: emitConst(module, block: walkHdr, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(walkHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: lost.id, elseBlockId: walkStep.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: lost, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerRegainWaiterLost, ids: ids)

	let next = emitLoadField(module, block: walkStep, record: prev, offset: MOffRegainNext, ids: ids)
	let found = emitCmp(module, block: walkStep, a: next, b: m, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(walkStep, op: StdOp.condBranch(found, thenBlockId: unlinkInner.id, elseBlockId: walkHdr.id))
	walkStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(walkHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(next)))

	emitStoreField(module, block: unlinkInner, record: prev, offset: MOffRegainNext, value: emitLoadField(module, block: unlinkInner, record: m, offset: MOffRegainNext, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(unlinkInner, op: StdOp.branch(cleared.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: cleared, record: m, offset: MOffRegainNext, value: emitConst(module, block: cleared, value: 0, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(cleared, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitSchedRegainUnlinkLocked'

// ==========================================================================
// `__sched_stop_workers`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__sched_stop_workers()` — SEAL THE SCHEDULER AND JOIN EVERY WORKER M OUT (SV1), AND W213-C2 MADE
// IT WALK THE MACHINES.** Its one caller is `GtRuntime.__gt_drain_at_exit`, which has already run the
// scheduler until no promise is outstanding; this is what makes the Ms that were servicing it STOP, and stop
// before anything downstream reads a counter they can still step.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT WALKED `__sched_procs` AND READ `POffOsThreadHandle`, AND THAT WAS A LANDMINE WITH A DATE ON
// IT.** The handle stored on a P names the machine that CLAIMED that P. While a processor belonged to one
// machine for that machine's life the two were the same thing; from this rung a processor is released at
// every park and handed to whoever `__sched_startm` gives it to, so a P-keyed handle names a thread that has
// moved on — and joining it **SUCCEEDS**, because the handle is still live. The teardown would return with a
// worker still running, still stepping the allocation counters `__mm_leak_check` reads two calls later: an
// intermittent exit 101 on a program with no leak. **Worse, a machine parked with no processor is on no P at
// all, so the old walk could not have signalled it and would have blocked for ever on a thread nobody had
// told to stop.** Both are why `__sched_allm` exists.
//
// Three steps, in this order and for these reasons:
//
//   1. **`__sched_shutdown_flag = 1` AND THE SIGNALS, BOTH INSIDE `__sched_lock`.** ⭐⭐ The lock is
//      W213-C2's addition and it is what closes the last spawn race: `__sched_startm` reads the flag while
//      holding this same lock and registers a new machine on `__sched_allm` before releasing it, so the two
//      orders are the only two there are — either it sees the flag and creates nothing, or its machine is on
//      the roster before this walk reads the head. There is no interleaving in which a worker exists and is
//      not signalled. A worker parked inside its own critical section is not a deadlock risk here: it holds
//      the lock only for the length of its park transaction and never across its `osWaitHandle`.
//   2. **`osSetEvent` on EVERY machine's park.** The events are AUTO-reset, so a signal that arrives while
//      its machine is still running is not lost — it leaves the event signalled and the next `osWaitHandle`
//      returns at once. Signalling M0, which is running the exit path by then, is harmless for the same
//      reason.
//   3. **JOIN each worker's thread handle, INFINITE, WITH THE LOCK RELEASED.** ⛔ The alternative is not
//      "exit sooner", it is a RACE: a worker's `__gt_reclaim` runs releaser decrefs, which step the very
//      allocation counters `__mm_leak_check` reads two calls later on the exit path. `ExitProcess` would kill
//      the workers mid-decref and the gate would read a count that was still moving. ⛔⛔ **And the lock MUST
//      be released first**: a worker on its way out can be inside the park's critical section, so joining it
//      while holding the lock is a deadlock rather than a slow exit.
//
// ⚠ **THE SECOND WALK IS UNLOCKED AND THAT IS SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION.** `__sched_allm` is append-only, and
// step 1 has already made it impossible for anything to be appended: no machine is created once the flag is
// set, and the flag was set under the lock this walk's head read is ordered after.
//
// ⚠ **M0's HANDLE IS ZERO AND IS SKIPPED BY THE SAME TEST ANY UNSTARTED MACHINE IS.** The main OS thread was
// never created by this runtime, so there is no handle for it and joining it would be joining ourselves.
function buildSchedStopWorkers(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SchedStopWorkersName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wakeHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopwakehdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wakeBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopwakebody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinWait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinwait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopjoinnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stopdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedShutdownFlagLabel, value: one, ids: ids)
	let firstMachine = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SchedAllMLabel, ids: ids)
	let wakeCursor = ids.mint()
	wakeHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(wakeCursor, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(wakeHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(wakeHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(firstMachine)))

	// `__sched_allm` is 0 until the first `__sched_init_procs`, so a program that never started the scheduler
	// walks nothing — the same zero `__sched_steal_count` rests on.
	let wakeZero = emitConst(module, block: wakeHdr, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let wakeDone = emitCmp(module, block: wakeHdr, a: wakeCursor, b: wakeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(wakeDone, thenBlockId: joinStart.id, elseBlockId: wakeBody.id))

	module.appendOp(wakeBody, op: StdOp.osSetEvent(emitLoadField(module, block: wakeBody, record: wakeCursor, offset: MOffParkEvent, ids: ids)))
	let nextWake = emitLoadField(module, block: wakeBody, record: wakeCursor, offset: MOffAllNext, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wakeBody, op: StdOp.branch(wakeHdr.id))
	wakeBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(wakeHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextWake)))

	// The join is a SECOND walk rather than a step of the first: every machine must have been told to stop
	// before we block on any one of them, or a machine signalled late would be joined while it was still
	// parked on an event nobody had set. The lock is released between the two for the reason step 3 gives.
	// ⚠ A machine blocked in the poller is not on its event, so the poller is broken as well.
	module.appendOp(joinStart, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollBreakName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: joinStart, ids: ids)
	let joinFirst = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: joinStart, label: SchedAllMLabel, ids: ids)
	let joinCursor = ids.mint()
	joinHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(joinCursor, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(joinStart, op: StdOp.branch(joinHdr.id))
	joinStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joinHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(joinFirst)))

	let joinEndZero = emitConst(module, block: joinHdr, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let joinDone = emitCmp(module, block: joinHdr, a: joinCursor, b: joinEndZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(joinHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(joinDone, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: joinBody.id))

	// ⛔⛔ **A MACHINE INSIDE A BLOCKING KERNEL CALL CANNOT BE WOKEN AND MUST NOT BE JOINED (W213-C3).** The
	// signal walk above sets that machine's event, which it is not waiting on; `WaitInfinite` on its thread
	// handle then blocks the exit path until the kernel call returns — and the whole subject of
	// `specs/sched-syscall-handoff.md` is calls that block for a second or for ever. **Go does not join
	// threads at exit at all**, which is the same answer arrived at from the other end.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TEST IS THE SYSCALL BRACKET'S OWN STATE BITS, NOT A TIMEOUT, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS IT FROM
	// MASKING A LIVE-BUT-SLOW WORKER.** A bounded wait would treat *"still finishing its last green thread"* and
	// *"parked in the kernel"* as one state and would silently stop waiting for the first, which is exactly the
	// class of bug `MOffOsThreadHandle` exists to have fixed: the teardown would return while a worker was still
	// stepping the counters `__mm_leak_check` reads. **State bits of zero is a machine running ordinary Maxon
	// code, and every such machine is still joined with no timeout.**
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **IT COVERS TWO STATES AND THE SECOND ONE IS WHY THE STOLEN BIT OUTLIVES THE CALL.**
	// `MSyscallInCall` is a machine inside the kernel, which cannot be woken and must not be joined.
	// `MSyscallStolen` is a machine that has LEFT the kernel and is inside `__sched_syscall_regain`, waiting for
	// a processor it may never be given — and that one is a join that hangs just as hard. `exitsyscall`
	// deliberately clears only the in-call bit for exactly this reason; `MOffSyscallState` carries the pair.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE READ IS UNLOCKED AND MAY BE STALE, AND BOTH DIRECTIONS ARE SAFE.** A machine that has cleared its
	// bits since the read is joined, and it is on its way to `wexit` because the shutdown flag is already set; a
	// machine that has set them since is skipped, which is the arm this block exists to take.
	let threadHandle = emitLoadField(module, block: joinBody, record: joinCursor, offset: MOffOsThreadHandle, ids: ids)
	let nextJoin = emitLoadField(module, block: joinBody, record: joinCursor, offset: MOffAllNext, ids: ids)
	let syscallWord = emitLoadField(module, block: joinBody, record: joinCursor, offset: MOffSyscallState, ids: ids)
	let joinStateMask = emitConst(module, block: joinBody, value: MSyscallStateMask, ids: ids)
	let joinZero = emitConst(module, block: joinBody, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noThread = emitCmp(module, block: joinBody, a: threadHandle, b: joinZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let inKernel = emitCmp(module, block: joinBody, a: emitBin(module, block: joinBody, a: syscallWord, b: joinStateMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids), b: joinZero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let unjoinable = emitBin(module, block: joinBody, a: noThread, b: inKernel, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let carriedNext = ids.mint()
	joinNext.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(carriedNext, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(joinBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(unjoinable, thenBlockId: joinNext.id, elseBlockId: joinWait.id))
	joinBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joinNext.id, argIds: argsOf(nextJoin)))

	// ⚠ **THE LINK IS READ BEFORE THE JOIN, AND IT IS CARRIED ACROSS IT.** The machine is not freed, so the
	// word would still be readable afterwards; reading it first is what keeps the walk independent of
	// anything the joined thread's last instructions touch, which is the property that made the roster
	// append-only in the first place.
	let infinite = emitConst(module, block: joinWait, value: WaitInfinite, ids: ids)
	let joinResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(joinWait, op: StdOp.osWaitHandle(joinResult, handleId: threadHandle, timeoutMsId: infinite))
	module.setTerminator(joinWait, op: StdOp.branch(joinNext.id))
	joinWait.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joinNext.id, argIds: argsOf(nextJoin)))

	module.setTerminator(joinNext, op: StdOp.branch(joinHdr.id))
	joinNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joinHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(carriedNext)))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSchedStopWorkers'
